"""TSO Edu Grammar Academy curriculum.
Static lesson content: plain-English explanation, a quick text illustration/diagram,
step-by-step guide, formal rule, structure, breakdown, examples, common mistakes,
memory tip and practice quiz.
"""

GRAMMAR_LESSONS = [
{"id": "g01", "day": 1, "level": "A1", "title": "Parts of Speech", "summary": "Identify the basic jobs words do in a sentence.", "plainExplanation": "Think of a sentence as a small team, and every word has a job on that team. Nouns name the players (dog, city, freedom). Pronouns are stand-ins that save you from repeating a noun (he, it, they). Verbs are the action — what the team is actually doing (run, is, decide). Adjectives and adverbs are the commentary that adds colour: adjectives describe nouns (a fast dog), adverbs describe verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs (runs quickly). Prepositions and conjunctions are the glue that connects everything (in, on, and, because). Once you can point at a word and say what job it's doing, every later grammar rule — agreement, tense, word order — becomes something you can reason about instead of memorise.", "illustration": "🏗️ SENTENCE = A TEAM WITH JOBS\n[The] [careful] [student] [studies] [English] [quietly] [in the library].\n  det   adj      NOUN      VERB      noun      ADV       PREP + noun\n            ↑subject         ↑action    ↑object      ↑how          ↑where\nNoun = who/what | Verb = the action | Adjective = describes a noun | Adverb = describes the verb", "steps": ["Find the main action or state first — that word is the verb.", "Ask 'who or what does the action?' — that word (or phrase) is the subject, usually a noun or pronoun.", "Look for words that describe the noun (what kind? which one? how many?) — those are adjectives.", "Look for words that describe the verb, an adjective, or another adverb (how? when? where?) — those are adverbs.", "Find any small connecting word showing place, time or direction (in, on, at, under) — that is a preposition.", "Find any word joining two ideas together (and, but, because) — that is a conjunction."], "rule": "Every English word does one of eight jobs in a sentence. These jobs are called the eight parts of speech: noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, conjunction and interjection. Learning to recognise each one is the foundation for every other grammar rule, because rules like subject-verb agreement, article use and word order all depend on knowing which part of speech a word is.", "structure": "Subject (noun/pronoun) + verb + object/complement", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Noun", "definition": "A noun names a person, place, thing, animal or idea. Nouns can be singular or plural, and they are often the subject or object of a sentence.", "example": "Teacher, Yangon, book, happiness — \"The teacher gave the book to the student.\""}, {"term": "2. Pronoun", "definition": "A pronoun replaces a noun so we do not repeat it. Common pronouns include I, you, he, she, it, we, they, this and someone.", "example": "\"Maria is late. She missed the bus.\" (She replaces Maria.)"}, {"term": "3. Verb", "definition": "A verb shows an action or a state of being. Every complete sentence needs a verb.", "example": "Run, study, is, become — \"Students study English every evening.\""}, {"term": "4. Adjective", "definition": "An adjective describes or modifies a noun or pronoun, telling us what kind, which one, or how many.", "example": "Careful, tall, three — \"The careful student passed three exams.\""}, {"term": "5. Adverb", "definition": "An adverb modifies a verb, an adjective or another adverb. It often tells us how, when, where or to what degree, and many end in -ly.", "example": "Quickly, very, yesterday — \"She spoke very quickly yesterday.\""}, {"term": "6. Preposition", "definition": "A preposition shows the relationship between a noun/pronoun and another word in the sentence — often location, time or direction.", "example": "In, on, at, under, before — \"The keys are under the table.\""}, {"term": "7. Conjunction", "definition": "A conjunction joins words, phrases or clauses together, such as and, but, or, because and although.", "example": "\"I wanted to leave, but the rain continued.\""}, {"term": "8. Interjection", "definition": "An interjection is a short word or phrase that expresses sudden emotion, like surprise, pain or excitement. It is often followed by an exclamation mark.", "example": "Wow! Ouch! Well, — \"Wow! That result is amazing.\""}], "examples": ["Students study English.", "The careful student works quietly.", "Knowledge is valuable."], "mistakes": ["Confusing an adjective with an adverb: 'She sings beautiful' should be 'She sings beautifully' (adverb modifying the verb sings).", "Treating every -ing word as a verb: in 'Swimming is good exercise', swimming is a noun (a gerund), not the main verb.", "Forgetting that pronouns must match the noun they replace in number: 'Every student must bring their own pen' works because their is now widely accepted as a neutral singular pronoun in modern usage."], "tip": "Say the sentence and ask four quick questions: Who/what is it? (noun/pronoun) What happens? (verb) What kind? (adjective) How/when/where? (adverb). That covers most words in any sentence.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which word is the verb in 'Students study English'?", "options": ["Students", "study", "English", "the"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which word is an adjective in 'a careful student'?", "options": ["a", "careful", "student", "is"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g02", "day": 2, "level": "A1", "title": "Subject and Verb Agreement", "summary": "Match singular and plural subjects with the correct verb form.", "plainExplanation": "Subject-verb agreement is really just a matching game: the verb has to match the subject in number. If the subject is one person or thing (he, she, it, or a singular noun like 'the manager'), the present-simple verb wears an -s or -es ending, like a uniform showing it belongs to a singular subject. If the subject is more than one (they, we, or a plural noun like 'the managers'), the verb stays bare — no -s. The tricky part is that some words look plural but count as singular in grammar: 'everyone', 'everybody', 'the team', 'the news' all take a singular verb, because grammatically they're treated as one unit, not many. When two subjects are joined by 'and', treat them as plural ('Tom and Ana study together'); when joined by 'or'/'nor', match the verb to whichever subject sits closest to it.", "illustration": "🔗 MATCH THE VERB TO THE SUBJECT\nSINGULAR  → he / she / it / the manager      → verb + -s   → \"The manager approves.\"\nPLURAL    → I / you / we / they / the managers → base verb  → \"The managers approve.\"\nTRICKY SINGULARS (feel plural, act singular): everyone · everybody · the team · the news\n  \"Everyone agrees.\"  ✅        \"Everyone agree.\"  ❌", "steps": ["Identify the subject of the sentence — is it one thing/person, or more than one?", "If singular (he/she/it or a singular noun), add -s or -es to the present simple verb.", "If plural (I/you/we/they or a plural noun), use the base verb with no ending.", "Check tricky subjects like everyone, the team, or the news — these look plural but are grammatically singular.", "When two subjects are joined by 'and', treat them as plural; when joined by 'or'/'nor', match the verb to the subject closest to it."], "rule": "A verb must agree in number with its subject: a singular subject takes a singular verb form, and a plural subject takes a plural verb form. In the present simple this shows up as the -s/-es ending on third-person singular verbs (he works), which disappears for plural or first/second-person subjects (they work). Agreement can be tricky with indefinite pronouns, collective nouns and subjects joined by and/or.", "structure": "Singular subject + verb-s/es | Plural subject + base verb", "breakdown": [{"term": "Singular subject", "definition": "He, she, it or a singular noun takes the -s/-es verb form in the present simple.", "example": "\"The manager approves every report.\""}, {"term": "Plural subject", "definition": "I, you, we, they or a plural noun takes the base verb form (no -s).", "example": "\"The managers approve every report.\""}, {"term": "Indefinite pronouns", "definition": "Words like everyone, everybody, someone and each are grammatically singular, even though they suggest many people.", "example": "\"Everyone in the class studies hard.\""}, {"term": "Collective nouns", "definition": "Words like team, family and staff are usually treated as singular when acting as one unit.", "example": "\"The team is preparing for the final.\""}, {"term": "Subjects joined by and / or", "definition": "Two subjects joined by and usually take a plural verb; subjects joined by or/nor agree with the nearer subject.", "example": "\"Tom and Lisa study together.\" / \"Neither the teacher nor the students were late.\""}], "examples": ["She works every day.", "They work every day.", "The students study together."], "mistakes": ["Adding -s to the verb after a plural subject: 'The students studies' should be 'The students study'.", "Treating 'everyone' or 'everybody' as plural: 'Everyone are ready' should be 'Everyone is ready'.", "Losing track of the real subject in a long sentence: 'The list of items (is/are) on the table' — the verb agrees with list, so it should be 'is'."], "tip": "Find the true subject first, ignore any words in between, then ask: one thing, or more than one? That decides the verb ending.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["He work hard.", "He works hard.", "He working hard.", "He workes hard."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["The students studies.", "The students studying.", "The students study.", "The students studys."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g03", "day": 3, "level": "A1", "title": "Present Simple", "summary": "Use the present simple for routines, facts and repeated actions.", "plainExplanation": "Use the present simple whenever you're talking about something that's generally true, not just happening this second: daily routines ('I wake up at seven'), permanent facts ('Water boils at 100°C'), habits ('She plays tennis on Sundays'), and fixed schedules ('The train leaves at nine'). The verb itself changes shape depending on the subject: with I/you/we/they it stays in its plain base form (I work, they play), but with he/she/it it grows an -s or -es ending (she works, it rains, he watches). For negatives and questions, English borrows a helper verb — do or does — instead of changing the main verb: 'I don't work' and 'Does she work?' Because 'does' already carries the -s for he/she/it, the main verb goes back to its plain base form even in third person: 'Does she work?' not 'Does she works?'", "illustration": "📅 PRESENT SIMPLE = ROUTINES, FACTS, SCHEDULES\nI / you / we / they  +  base verb        → \"I work.\"  \"They play.\"\nhe / she / it        +  verb + -s/-es    → \"She works.\"  \"It rains.\"\nNEGATIVE:  don't / doesn't  +  base verb  → \"She doesn't work.\"  (the -s moved to 'doesn't')\nQUESTION:  Do / Does + subject + base verb?  → \"Does she work?\"\nSignal words: always · usually · every day · on Sundays · never", "steps": ["Decide if the sentence is about a habit, a fact, or a schedule rather than something happening right now.", "Use the base verb for I/you/we/they (I work, they play).", "Add -s or -es for he/she/it (she works, it rains) — remember -es after s, x, ch, sh, o.", "For negatives, use do not/does not + base verb (I don't work, she doesn't work).", "For questions, put do/does before the subject (Do you work? Does she work?)."], "rule": "The present simple describes routines, habits, permanent facts and fixed schedules — anything true again and again, not just happening right now. Use the base form of the verb with I/you/we/they, and add -s or -es for he/she/it. Negatives and questions are formed with do/does plus the base verb, and does already carries the -s, so the main verb stays in its base form.", "structure": "Subject + base/-s verb | Do/Does + subject + base verb?", "breakdown": [{"term": "Habits and routines", "definition": "Actions repeated regularly, often with frequency adverbs like always, usually, often, sometimes and never.", "example": "\"I usually wake up at six.\""}, {"term": "General truths and facts", "definition": "Things that are permanently or scientifically true.", "example": "\"Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius.\""}, {"term": "Schedules and timetables", "definition": "Fixed events, even in the future, when they follow an official timetable.", "example": "\"The train leaves at 9 a.m. every day.\""}, {"term": "Negatives with do/does not", "definition": "Use don't/doesn't + base verb; never add -s to the main verb after does.", "example": "\"She doesn't like coffee.\" (not 'doesn't likes')"}, {"term": "Questions with do/does", "definition": "Do/Does moves to the front of the question, and the main verb stays in the base form.", "example": "\"Does he study every day?\""}], "examples": ["I study at night.", "She studies at night.", "Does he study every day?"], "mistakes": ["Double marking the -s: 'She doesn't studies' should be 'She doesn't study' — does already shows the -s.", "Using present simple for a temporary action happening right now instead of present continuous: 'I study now' should usually be 'I am studying now'.", "Forgetting -es after s, sh, ch, x, o: 'She wash the dishes' should be 'She washes the dishes'."], "tip": "If you can add always, usually or every day and the sentence still makes sense, present simple is almost certainly correct.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence describes a routine?", "options": ["I studied yesterday.", "I am studying now.", "I study every evening.", "I will study tomorrow."], "answer": 2}, {"q": "Choose the correct question.", "options": ["Does she likes tea?", "Does she like tea?", "Do she like tea?", "Does she liking tea?"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g04", "day": 4, "level": "A1", "title": "Present Continuous", "summary": "Describe actions happening now or around the present time.", "plainExplanation": "The present continuous (also called present progressive) describes something happening right now, in progress, not finished yet — imagine pointing at the action as it unfolds. You build it with a form of 'be' (am/is/are) plus the main verb with -ing added: 'I am working', 'she is studying', 'they are waiting'. It's also used for temporary situations that are true around now but won't last forever ('I'm staying with my parents this month') and for future plans that are already arranged ('We're meeting the client tomorrow at 10'). The key contrast to keep in mind is with the present simple: present simple is for routines and permanent facts ('I work in sales'), while present continuous is for what's happening at this specific moment or period ('I'm working on a report right now'). Watch out for a small group of verbs — like know, believe, want, love, own — that describe states rather than actions; these are rarely used in the continuous form, even when something feels 'ongoing' ('I am knowing the answer' is wrong; 'I know the answer' is right).", "illustration": "⏳ PRESENT CONTINUOUS = IN PROGRESS RIGHT NOW\nam / is / are  +  verb-ing\n   I  am  working.\n  She is  studying.\n They are waiting.\nNEGATIVE:  am/is/are + not + verb-ing  → \"I'm not working.\"\nQUESTION:  Am/Is/Are + subject + verb-ing?  → \"Are you working?\"\nSignal words: now · right now · at the moment · these days · Look! · Listen!\nState verbs usually skip -ing:  know · want · believe · love · own", "steps": ["Check whether the action is happening now, or is a temporary/changing situation (not a permanent fact).", "Choose the correct form of 'be' for the subject: am (I), is (he/she/it), are (you/we/they).", "Add -ing to the main verb (work → working, run → running — double the final consonant for short verbs like run, sit, stop).", "For negatives, add not after the be-verb (I am not working, she isn't working).", "For questions, move the be-verb before the subject (Are you working? Is she working?)."], "rule": "The present continuous (be + verb-ing) describes an action in progress at the moment of speaking, a temporary situation around now, or a fixed future arrangement. It also highlights change over time. Some verbs describing states, thoughts, feelings or senses — such as know, believe, want, love, seem — are not normally used in the continuous form because they describe a condition rather than an ongoing action.", "structure": "Subject + am/is/are + verb-ing", "breakdown": [{"term": "Action happening now", "definition": "Something in progress at the exact moment of speaking.", "example": "\"Please be quiet — I am taking a test.\""}, {"term": "Temporary situation", "definition": "Something true only for a limited period, not permanently.", "example": "\"She is staying with her cousin this month.\""}, {"term": "Future arrangement", "definition": "A plan already fixed with a time and place, usually involving another person.", "example": "\"We are meeting the tutor at 5 p.m. tomorrow.\""}, {"term": "Change or trend", "definition": "A situation that is gradually changing over time.", "example": "\"More students are choosing online courses these days.\""}, {"term": "State verbs (usually not continuous)", "definition": "Verbs of thought, feeling, possession and the senses often stay in the simple form even when talking about now.", "example": "\"I understand the rule\" (not 'I am understanding')."}], "examples": ["I am reading now.", "She is preparing dinner.", "They are studying for an exam."], "mistakes": ["Using state verbs in the continuous: 'I am knowing the answer' should be 'I know the answer'.", "Forgetting the auxiliary be: 'She studying now' should be 'She is studying now'.", "Using present continuous for permanent facts: 'The Earth is going around the Sun' should usually be 'The Earth goes around the Sun'."], "tip": "Ask: is this happening right now, or only for a limited time? If yes to either, be + verb-ing is usually the right choice.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["She is study now.", "She studying now.", "She is studying now.", "She studies now yesterday."], "answer": 2}, {"q": "Which form completes 'They ___ working'?", "options": ["is", "are", "am", "be"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g05", "day": 5, "level": "A1", "title": "Past Simple", "summary": "Talk about completed actions in the past.", "plainExplanation": "The past simple is for actions and situations that started and finished at a specific point in the past — the story is over, and you're often naming when it happened ('I visited Paris last year', 'She called me yesterday'). Regular verbs are easy: just add -ed (walk → walked, visit → visited). Irregular verbs, unfortunately, don't follow that pattern and simply have to be memorised one by one (go → went, see → saw, buy → bought, write → wrote) — there's no shortcut, but the good news is the most common ones repeat constantly in everyday speech, so they become automatic with exposure. For negatives and questions, the auxiliary 'did' takes over the job of showing past tense, which means the main verb goes back to its plain base form: 'I didn't go' (not 'didn't went'), 'Did you go?' (not 'Did you went?'). This is the same pattern you saw with do/does in the present simple, just shifted one tense back.", "illustration": "📖 PAST SIMPLE = FINISHED ACTION, SPECIFIC TIME\nRegular:    walk → walked   |   visit → visited   (just add -ed)\nIrregular:  go → went   |   see → saw   |   buy → bought   (memorise these)\nNEGATIVE:  didn't + base verb  → \"I didn't go.\"   (never \"didn't went\")\nQUESTION:  Did + subject + base verb?  → \"Did you go?\"   (never \"Did you went?\")\nSignal words: yesterday · last week · in 2019 · two years ago · ago", "steps": ["Confirm the action is finished and happened at a definite past time.", "For regular verbs, add -ed to the base form (work → worked, study → studied).", "For irregular verbs, learn the past form individually — it does not follow the -ed pattern (go → went, see → saw).", "For negatives, use did not (didn't) + base verb, even with irregular verbs (I didn't go, not I didn't went).", "For questions, put did before the subject and use the base verb (Did you go? Did she see it?)."], "rule": "The past simple describes actions or situations that started and finished at a definite time in the past. Regular verbs form the past tense by adding -ed (or -d, -ied), while irregular verbs change form completely and must be memorised (go → went, see → saw). Negatives and questions use did, and once did appears, the main verb returns to its base form.", "structure": "Subject + past verb", "breakdown": [{"term": "Regular verbs (-ed)", "definition": "Most verbs simply add -ed, with small spelling changes for verbs ending in -e, -y or a single stressed vowel + consonant.", "example": "work → worked, study → studied, stop → stopped."}, {"term": "Irregular verbs", "definition": "Common verbs have unique past forms that do not follow the -ed pattern and must be learned individually.", "example": "go → went, see → saw, have → had, buy → bought."}, {"term": "Time expressions", "definition": "Words like yesterday, last week, in 2019 and ... ago anchor the action to a finished point in the past.", "example": "\"They arrived two hours ago.\""}, {"term": "Negatives with did not", "definition": "Use didn't + base verb; the past marking moves to did, so the main verb loses its -ed ending.", "example": "\"She didn't finish the report.\" (not 'didn't finished')"}, {"term": "Questions with did", "definition": "Did moves to the front, and the main verb stays in the base form.", "example": "\"Did you visit Bagan last year?\""}], "examples": ["I visited Yangon last week.", "She went home early.", "They finished the project yesterday."], "mistakes": ["Double-marking the past: 'She didn't went' should be 'She didn't go'.", "Forgetting irregular forms: 'I goed to school' should be 'I went to school'.", "Mixing past simple with an unfinished time word like 'since': 'I lived here since 2020' should usually be 'I have lived here since 2020'."], "tip": "If the sentence names a finished time (yesterday, last year, in 2018), past simple is almost always correct.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct past form of 'go'.", "options": ["goed", "went", "gone", "going"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["They finish yesterday.", "They finished yesterday.", "They have finish yesterday.", "They are finish yesterday."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g06", "day": 6, "level": "A2", "title": "Past Continuous", "summary": "Describe an action that was in progress at a particular past time.", "plainExplanation": "The past continuous paints the background scene of a past story — it shows an action that was already in progress at a certain moment, like a movie playing in the background while something else happens in the foreground. You build it with was/were plus the main verb + -ing: 'I was reading', 'they were cooking'. Its most common job is setting up a longer action that gets interrupted by a shorter one: 'I was reading (long, ongoing) when the phone rang (short, sudden).' Notice the pattern — the longer, background action uses past continuous, while the shorter, interrupting action uses past simple. It's also used to show two things happening at the same time in the past ('While I was cooking, she was cleaning') and to describe the general scene-setting atmosphere of a story ('The sun was shining, birds were singing...').", "illustration": "🎬 PAST CONTINUOUS = THE BACKGROUND ACTION\nwas / were  +  verb-ing\n   I / he / she / it  → was  + verb-ing   → \"I was reading.\"\n   you / we / they    → were + verb-ing   → \"They were cooking.\"\nTHE CLASSIC PATTERN — long background action + short interruption:\n  [I was reading  ————————●]  ← phone rang (past simple, sudden, short)\n       (past continuous = ongoing, in progress at that moment)", "steps": ["Identify the longer, 'in-progress' action and the shorter action that interrupted or happened at the same time.", "Use was/were + verb-ing for the longer action (was working, were studying).", "Use the past simple for the shorter, interrupting action (...when the phone rang).", "Join the two with 'when' (interruption) or 'while' (two actions happening together).", "Remember: was for I/he/she/it, were for you/we/they."], "rule": "The past continuous (was/were + verb-ing) describes an action that was already in progress at a specific past moment. It is often used together with the past simple: the longer, background action is past continuous, and the shorter action that interrupts it is past simple. It can also describe two actions happening at the same time in the past, or simply set the scene for a story.", "structure": "Subject + was/were + verb-ing", "breakdown": [{"term": "Background action interrupted", "definition": "A longer action in progress when a shorter action happened.", "example": "\"I was studying when the power went out.\""}, {"term": "Two parallel past actions", "definition": "Two actions happening at the same time, usually joined with while.", "example": "\"She was cooking while he was cleaning.\""}, {"term": "Action at a specific past time", "definition": "Something in progress at a stated clock time in the past.", "example": "\"At 8 p.m. I was still working.\""}, {"term": "Setting the scene", "definition": "Used at the start of a story to describe the background situation.", "example": "\"The rain was falling and the streets were empty.\""}], "examples": ["I was studying at 8 p.m.", "They were talking when I arrived.", "She was sleeping while it was raining."], "mistakes": ["Using the wrong form of be: 'They was talking' should be 'They were talking'.", "Using past continuous for a short, completed action: 'I was arriving at 6' usually should be 'I arrived at 6'.", "Forgetting -ing: 'She was study' should be 'She was studying'."], "tip": "Picture a photo of the past moment — if the action looks unfinished, mid-way, it's past continuous.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct form: 'They ___ watching TV when I called.'", "options": ["was", "were", "are", "have"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which sentence is grammatically correct?", "options": ["He was drive home.", "He were driving home.", "He was driving home.", "He driving home."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g07", "day": 7, "level": "A2", "title": "Present Perfect", "summary": "Connect past actions with the present.", "plainExplanation": "The present perfect is the bridge tense — it connects the past to right now, without saying exactly when something happened. Build it with have/has plus the past participle (the third form of the verb): 'I have finished', 'she has visited', 'they have gone'. Use it for three main situations: (1) a life experience with no specific time mentioned ('I have visited Japan' — at some point in your life, doesn't matter exactly when), (2) something that started in the past and is still true or still happening now ('We have lived here for five years' — and we still live here), and (3) a very recent action whose result matters right now ('I have lost my keys' — the losing happened recently and I still don't have them). Two small words often travel with this tense and matter a lot: 'for' pairs with a duration ('for five years'), while 'since' pairs with a starting point ('since 2019'). And remember — if you name a specific finished time (yesterday, last year, in 2020), switch to past simple instead.", "illustration": "🌉 PRESENT PERFECT = THE BRIDGE FROM PAST TO NOW\nhave / has  +  past participle\n   I / you / we / they  → have + p.p.   → \"I have finished.\"\n   he / she / it         → has  + p.p.   → \"She has visited.\"\nPAST ─────────●═══════════════► NOW      (action connects to the present)\nFOR = a duration (\"for five years\")   |   SINCE = a starting point (\"since 2019\")\n⚠️ Named specific past time (yesterday, in 2020)? → switch to PAST SIMPLE", "steps": ["Check if there is no specific past time word (no 'yesterday', 'in 2020') — if there is one, use past simple instead.", "Build the form with have/has + past participle (have worked, has gone, have seen).", "Use 'has' for he/she/it and 'have' for I/you/we/they.", "Use it for life experience (I have visited Paris), unfinished time (I have lived here for 5 years), or a past action with a present result (I have lost my keys — I don't have them now).", "Pair with 'ever/never', 'already/yet', 'since/for', and 'just' as common signal words."], "rule": "The present perfect (have/has + past participle) connects the past to the present. It is used for life experiences without a stated time, actions with a visible result now, and situations that started in the past and continue up to now. Unlike the past simple, it does not focus on exactly when something happened — it focuses on the connection to the present moment.", "structure": "Subject + have/has + past participle", "breakdown": [{"term": "Life experience", "definition": "Something that happened at an unspecified time in your life, often with ever/never.", "example": "\"Have you ever visited Japan?\""}, {"term": "Recent action with a present result", "definition": "Something that just happened and still matters now, often with just, already or yet.", "example": "\"I have just finished the report.\""}, {"term": "Unfinished time period", "definition": "An action or state continuing from the past to now, usually with since (a point) or for (a duration).", "example": "\"They have lived here since 2022.\" / \"...for four years.\""}, {"term": "Change over time", "definition": "Describing how something has changed up to now.", "example": "\"Prices have increased a lot this year.\""}], "examples": ["I have finished my work.", "She has visited Thailand twice.", "They have lived here since 2022."], "mistakes": ["Using present perfect with a finished time word: 'I have seen him yesterday' should be 'I saw him yesterday'.", "Confusing since and for: 'since three years' should be 'for three years'.", "Using the wrong participle: 'She has went' should be 'She has gone'."], "tip": "If you can add a specific past date or time (yesterday, in 2019), switch to past simple; if the time is open or unstated, present perfect fits.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["She has went home.", "She has gone home.", "She have gone home.", "She gone home."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which word commonly introduces a starting point?", "options": ["since", "during", "ago", "while"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g08", "day": 8, "level": "A2", "title": "Past Perfect", "summary": "Show that one past event happened before another past event.", "plainExplanation": "The past perfect is 'the past before the past' — it's for when you're already telling a story in the past tense, and you need to point to something that happened even earlier, before that. Build it with had plus the past participle: 'I had finished', 'they had left'. Picture two events on a timeline, both in the past, but one clearly before the other: 'When I arrived (event 2, past simple), the film had already started (event 1, past perfect — it started earlier).' The past perfect always marks the earlier of the two events; the past simple marks the later, more recent one. It's especially common with words like 'already', 'just', 'by the time', and 'before' that signal one thing happened ahead of another. Without the past perfect, a two-event past story can genuinely be ambiguous about which thing happened first — the past perfect removes that ambiguity.", "illustration": "⏮️ PAST PERFECT = THE EARLIER OF TWO PAST EVENTS\nhad  +  past participle\nTIMELINE:\n  [The film had started] ────────────► [I arrived]\n     (EARLIER — past perfect)              (LATER — past simple)\n\"By the time we arrived, the film had started.\"\nSignal words: already · just · by the time · before · after (with two past events)", "steps": ["Identify two past events and decide which one happened earlier.", "Use had + past participle for the earlier event (had finished, had left).", "Use the past simple for the later event (...when I arrived).", "Join the two events with words like 'when', 'after', 'before', or 'by the time'.", "Avoid overusing the past perfect — only use it when the order of two past events needs to be made clear."], "rule": "The past perfect (had + past participle) shows that one past event happened before another past event — it is the 'past of the past'. The earlier event uses had + past participle, and the later event uses the ordinary past simple. It is especially common with time words like before, after, by the time, and already, and in reported speech.", "structure": "Earlier event: had + past participle → later event: past simple", "breakdown": [{"term": "Two past events in order", "definition": "The earlier action is past perfect; the later action is past simple.", "example": "\"The train had left before we arrived.\""}, {"term": "With before / after / by the time", "definition": "These time words often signal which event needs the past perfect.", "example": "\"By the time I called, she had already left.\""}, {"term": "With already / just / never (past)", "definition": "Similar to present perfect, but shifted one step further into the past.", "example": "\"I had never seen snow before that trip.\""}, {"term": "Reporting an earlier statement", "definition": "Used in reported speech when the original event happened before the reporting verb's time.", "example": "\"He said he had finished the exam.\""}], "examples": ["The train had left before we arrived.", "She had finished the report when the manager called.", "I had never seen snow before that trip."], "mistakes": ["Using past perfect for the only event in the sentence: 'I had visited Bagan last year' (with no second event) should usually just be 'I visited Bagan last year'.", "Reversing the order: 'I had eaten after I arrived home' mixes up which event came first — it should be 'I ate after I had arrived home' or simply 'I arrived home and then ate'.", "Using have instead of had for the earlier past event: 'They have left before I arrived' should be 'They had left before I arrived'."], "tip": "Ask which event happened first. The first (earlier) event gets had + past participle; the second event stays in simple past.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["They had left before I arrived.", "They have left before I arrived.", "They had leave before I arrived.", "They leaving before I arrived."], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Which event normally uses past perfect?", "options": ["The earlier past event", "The later past event", "A future event", "A general fact"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g09", "day": 9, "level": "A2", "title": "Future Forms", "summary": "Use will, be going to and present continuous for different future meanings.", "plainExplanation": "English has several ways to talk about the future, and choosing the right one depends on the situation. Use 'will' for decisions made right at the moment of speaking and for promises or offers ('It's cold — I'll close the window'). Use 'going to' for plans you already decided on before this conversation ('We're going to start the new project next week' — that decision was already made) and for predictions based on visible evidence right now ('Look at those clouds — it's going to rain'). Use the present continuous for a fixed arrangement with a specific time and (often) another person involved, like an appointment already in the diary ('I'm meeting the client tomorrow at 10'). The differences are subtle but meaningful: 'will' feels spontaneous, 'going to' feels pre-planned or evidence-based, and present continuous feels like it's already arranged and confirmed.", "illustration": "🔮 FOUR WAYS TO TALK ABOUT THE FUTURE\nWILL          → spontaneous decision, promise, offer   → \"I'll close the window.\"\nGOING TO      → pre-made plan, or evidence right now    → \"We're going to launch it.\" / \"It's going to rain.\"\nPRESENT CONT. → fixed arrangement, diary confirmed       → \"I'm meeting the client at 10.\"\nPRESENT SIMPLE → fixed timetable/schedule                → \"The train leaves at 9.\"", "steps": ["For a spontaneous decision made at the moment of speaking, use will + base verb (I'll answer the phone).", "For a prior plan or intention, use be going to + base verb (I'm going to visit my parents).", "For a fixed schedule or timetable, use the present simple (The train leaves at 6pm).", "For a definite personal arrangement already made with someone, use the present continuous (I'm meeting her tomorrow).", "For an action that will be in progress at a future time, use will be + verb-ing (I'll be working at 8pm)."], "rule": "English has several ways to talk about the future, and the choice depends on meaning, not just time. Will often expresses spontaneous decisions, predictions and promises. Be going to expresses plans already made or predictions based on present evidence. Present continuous expresses fixed arrangements with another person, and present simple expresses future events on an official timetable.", "structure": "will + base | am/is/are going to + base | am/is/are + verb-ing", "breakdown": [{"term": "will", "definition": "Spontaneous decisions made at the moment of speaking, general predictions, offers and promises.", "example": "\"I'll help you carry that.\" (decided right now)"}, {"term": "be going to", "definition": "A plan or intention decided before now, or a prediction based on present evidence.", "example": "\"Look at those clouds — it is going to rain.\""}, {"term": "present continuous", "definition": "A fixed arrangement with a specific time, place and usually another person.", "example": "\"I am meeting my tutor at 5 p.m. tomorrow.\""}, {"term": "present simple", "definition": "Future events fixed by an official schedule or timetable.", "example": "\"The bus leaves at 7 a.m. tomorrow.\""}], "examples": ["I will call you later.", "We are going to start a course.", "I am meeting my tutor tomorrow."], "mistakes": ["Using will for a plan already decided: 'I will start a course next week' (already arranged) is more naturally 'I am going to start a course next week'.", "Adding to after will: 'I will to help you' should be 'I will help you'.", "Using present continuous for a prediction with no arrangement: 'It is raining tomorrow' (guess, not arranged) should be 'It is going to rain tomorrow'."], "tip": "Ask: is this a fixed plan with a time and place? → present continuous. A spur-of-the-moment decision? → will. Evidence right now? → going to.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the best form for a prior plan: 'We ___ visit the museum tomorrow.'", "options": ["are going to", "have", "were", "did"], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["I will to help you.", "I will help you.", "I will helping you.", "I am will help you."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g10", "day": 10, "level": "A2", "title": "Articles: a, an, the", "summary": "Use articles to show whether a noun is general or specific.", "plainExplanation": "Articles — a, an, and the — tell your listener how specific you're being about a noun. Use 'a' or 'an' the first time you mention a countable singular noun, when it's just one example out of many and the listener doesn't know exactly which one yet ('I saw a cat' — could be any cat). Choose 'an' instead of 'a' when the next word starts with a vowel sound (not just a vowel letter) — 'an umbrella', 'an honest man' (the h is silent, so it sounds like it starts with a vowel), but 'a university' (starts with a 'y' sound, so 'a', not 'an', even though 'u' is a vowel letter). Switch to 'the' once both speaker and listener know exactly which one you mean — often because you already mentioned it ('I saw a cat. The cat was black'), because there's only one of it in the world or context ('the sun', 'the internet'), or because extra words in the sentence pin down exactly which one you mean ('the cat next door'). Uncountable and plural general nouns often take no article at all when speaking generally ('I like music', not 'I like the music', unless you mean one specific piece).", "illustration": "🎯 ARTICLES = HOW SPECIFIC ARE YOU BEING?\nA / AN  →  first mention, any one of many, listener doesn't know which\n           \"a\" before consonant SOUND: a cat, a university (y-sound)\n           \"an\" before vowel SOUND: an umbrella, an honest man (silent h)\nTHE     →  both people know exactly which one\n           already mentioned → \"I saw a cat. The cat was black.\"\n           only one exists   → \"the sun\", \"the moon\"\n(no article) →  general statement, plural/uncountable → \"I like music.\"", "steps": ["Decide if the noun is countable and singular — only then can you use a/an at all.", "Use 'a' before a consonant sound and 'an' before a vowel sound (a university, an hour — go by sound, not spelling).", "Use 'a/an' the first time you mention something, or when it doesn't matter which one you mean.", "Use 'the' when both speaker and listener know exactly which one you mean (already mentioned, unique, or obvious from context).", "Use no article at all for general/plural or uncountable nouns speaking in general (Dogs are loyal; I like music)."], "rule": "Articles show whether a noun is general/non-specific or specific/already known to the listener. Use a/an before a singular countable noun the first time it is mentioned, or when it is not a specific one. Use the when both speaker and listener know exactly which noun is meant — because it was already mentioned, is unique, or is clear from context. No article (zero article) is used with plural or uncountable nouns in a general sense, and with most proper nouns.", "structure": "a/an + singular countable noun | the + specific noun", "breakdown": [{"term": "a / an — first mention", "definition": "Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound, for a non-specific singular countable noun.", "example": "\"I saw a dog. The dog was friendly.\" / \"She is an engineer.\""}, {"term": "the — specific or known", "definition": "Use the once the listener knows exactly which one is meant, or for something unique.", "example": "\"Please close the door.\" / \"The sun rose early.\""}, {"term": "zero article — general plurals/uncountables", "definition": "No article is needed for plural or uncountable nouns used in a general sense.", "example": "\"Dogs are loyal animals.\" / \"Water is essential for life.\""}, {"term": "zero article — most proper nouns", "definition": "Names of people, most countries, cities and languages usually take no article.", "example": "\"Maria studies in Japan.\" (not 'the Japan')"}], "examples": ["I saw a dog. The dog was friendly.", "She is an engineer.", "Please close the door."], "mistakes": ["Dropping the article before a singular countable noun: 'She is engineer' should be 'She is an engineer'.", "Using a/an by spelling instead of sound: 'a hour' should be 'an hour' because hour starts with a vowel sound.", "Using the for a first, non-specific mention: 'I bought the car yesterday' (first mention, no shared context) should usually be 'I bought a car yesterday'."], "tip": "First time you mention a singular countable thing → a/an. Every time after that, once it's identified → the.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["She is engineer.", "She is an engineer.", "She is the engineer always.", "She is an engineers."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "After introducing 'a book', which is natural when referring to the same book again?", "options": ["a book", "an book", "the book", "book the"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g11", "day": 11, "level": "A2", "title": "Countable and Uncountable Nouns", "summary": "Use nouns and quantifiers accurately.", "plainExplanation": "Nouns split into two families that behave differently: countable nouns can be counted one by one and have a plural form ('one book, two books'), while uncountable nouns describe things treated as a whole mass or concept, with no natural plural ('advice', 'furniture', 'music', 'information', 'homework'). This matters because countable and uncountable nouns pair with different quantity words. With countable plurals, use 'many', 'a few', or a number ('many problems', 'three chairs'). With uncountables, use 'much', 'a little', or 'a bit of' instead ('much traffic', 'a little advice') — and never add a plural -s to them, so 'furnitures', 'advices', and 'homeworks' are all incorrect, no matter how tempting they feel. A handy trick: many uncountable nouns describe categories made of countable sub-parts (furniture is made of chairs, tables, and sofas; luggage is made of bags and suitcases), which is why the whole category itself doesn't pluralise even though its parts could be counted individually.", "illustration": "🧮 COUNTABLE vs UNCOUNTABLE\nCOUNTABLE  → has a plural, use 'many' / 'a few' / a number\n            one chair → two chairs   |   many problems   |   a few ideas\nUNCOUNTABLE → no plural, use 'much' / 'a little' / 'a bit of'\n            furniture (NOT furnitures)   |   much traffic   |   a little advice\nCommonly uncountable in English: advice · furniture · music · information · homework · traffic · luggage", "steps": ["Ask: can I put a number directly in front of this noun? (one chair, two chairs) — if yes, it's countable.", "If the noun describes a substance, liquid, or abstract idea with no natural 'one unit', it's likely uncountable.", "For countable nouns, use a/an, many, few, a number of, and a plural form.", "For uncountable nouns, use some, much, a little, a lot of — and keep the noun singular in form.", "Watch for words that are countable in some languages but uncountable in English, like advice, information, furniture, and news."], "rule": "Countable nouns can be counted individually and have a singular and plural form (one book, two books). Uncountable nouns describe things treated as a whole mass, idea or substance, and normally have no plural form and never take a/an. Different quantifiers pair with each type, so choosing the right quantifier depends on recognising whether the noun is countable or uncountable.", "structure": "a/an + singular countable | some/much + uncountable | many + plural countable", "breakdown": [{"term": "Countable nouns", "definition": "Can be singular or plural and can be counted with numbers.", "example": "a student, two students, three ideas."}, {"term": "Uncountable nouns", "definition": "Treated as a whole, with no plural form and no a/an; use a unit of measure to count them.", "example": "information, advice, water, furniture — \"a piece of advice\"."}, {"term": "much / many", "definition": "Many pairs with plural countable nouns; much pairs with uncountable nouns, mostly in questions and negatives.", "example": "\"How many books?\" / \"How much water?\""}, {"term": "a few / a little", "definition": "A few (a small but positive number) pairs with countable nouns; a little (a small but positive amount) pairs with uncountable nouns.", "example": "\"a few ideas\" / \"a little time\"."}, {"term": "some / any", "definition": "Some is common in positive sentences and offers; any is common in negatives and questions — both work with either type.", "example": "\"I have some information.\" / \"Do you have any advice?\""}], "examples": ["a student / two students", "some information", "many ideas", "much time"], "mistakes": ["Pluralising an uncountable noun: 'many informations' should be 'a lot of information' or 'many pieces of information'.", "Using a/an with an uncountable noun: 'an advice' should be 'a piece of advice' or simply 'advice'.", "Using much with a countable plural noun: 'much students' should be 'many students'."], "tip": "Try putting a number in front of the noun (two ___). If it sounds natural, it's countable; if not, it's uncountable.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which phrase is correct?", "options": ["an information", "many information", "some information", "two informations"], "answer": 2}, {"q": "Which noun is countable here?", "options": ["water", "advice", "idea", "information"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g12", "day": 12, "level": "A2", "title": "Pronouns and Possessives", "summary": "Avoid repeated nouns and show ownership clearly.", "plainExplanation": "Pronouns replace nouns so you don't have to keep repeating names, but English pronouns change shape depending on their job in the sentence — this is called 'case'. Subject pronouns (I, you, he, she, it, we, they) do the action and usually come before the verb: 'He gave me the keys.' Object pronouns (me, you, him, her, it, us, them) receive the action and usually come after the verb or a preposition: 'He gave me the keys' — 'me' is receiving, not doing. Possessive adjectives (my, your, his, her, its, our, their) sit directly before a noun to show ownership: 'their house'. Possessive pronouns (mine, yours, his, hers, its, ours, theirs) stand completely alone, replacing both the owner and the noun together, with no noun following and no apostrophe: 'This is mine' (not 'this is my' or 'this is mine's'). A common trap is 'it's' vs 'its' — 'it's' is always a contraction of 'it is' or 'it has', while 'its' (no apostrophe) is the possessive form, matching the pattern of his/her/their, none of which take an apostrophe either.", "illustration": "👤 PRONOUN CASES — SAME PERSON, DIFFERENT SHAPE BY JOB\nSUBJECT (does the action)    → I · you · he · she · it · we · they\nOBJECT (receives the action) → me · you · him · her · it · us · them\nPOSSESSIVE + noun            → my · your · his · her · its · our · their house\nPOSSESSIVE alone (no noun)   → mine · yours · his · hers · its · ours · theirs\n\"He gave me the keys.\"   →  He=subject, me=object\n\"This laptop is mine.\"   →  mine stands alone, no apostrophe", "steps": ["Identify whether the pronoun is doing the action (subject: I, you, he, she, it, we, they).", "Or receiving the action (object: me, you, him, her, it, us, them).", "Choose a possessive adjective before a noun to show ownership (my book, her car).", "Choose a possessive pronoun to replace a noun + possessive adjective entirely (This book is mine, not This book is my).", "Use reflexive pronouns (myself, himself, themselves) when the subject and object are the same person."], "rule": "Pronouns replace nouns so language does not become repetitive, and possessives show ownership. English pronouns change form depending on their grammatical role: subject pronouns act as the subject, object pronouns follow verbs or prepositions, possessive adjectives sit directly before a noun, possessive pronouns replace a noun + possessive adjective entirely, and reflexive pronouns refer back to the subject.", "structure": "I/he/she/they | me/him/her/them | my/his/her/their + noun | mine/his/hers/theirs", "breakdown": [{"term": "Subject pronouns", "definition": "I, you, he, she, it, we, they — used as the subject of a verb.", "example": "\"She called me.\""}, {"term": "Object pronouns", "definition": "Me, you, him, her, it, us, them — used after a verb or preposition.", "example": "\"She called me.\" / \"Give it to him.\""}, {"term": "Possessive adjectives", "definition": "My, your, his, her, its, our, their — always followed directly by a noun.", "example": "\"This is my book.\""}, {"term": "Possessive pronouns", "definition": "Mine, yours, his, hers, its, ours, theirs — stand alone, without a following noun.", "example": "\"The blue bag is mine.\""}, {"term": "Reflexive pronouns", "definition": "Myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, themselves — used when the subject and object are the same person.", "example": "\"She taught herself to code.\""}], "examples": ["She called me.", "This is my book.", "The blue bag is mine."], "mistakes": ["Using a subject pronoun after a preposition: 'between you and I' should be 'between you and me'.", "Confusing possessive adjective and possessive pronoun: 'This is mine book' should be 'This is my book' or 'This book is mine'.", "Using its for it's: 'The dog wagged it's tail' should be 'The dog wagged its tail' (its is possessive; it's means 'it is')."], "tip": "If a noun comes right after the word, use my/your/his/her/their. If the word stands completely alone, use mine/yours/his/hers/theirs.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["Me am ready.", "I am ready.", "Mine am ready.", "My am ready."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct possessive form: 'This phone belongs to her.'", "options": ["her phone", "hers phone", "she phone", "her's phone"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g13", "day": 13, "level": "B1", "title": "Modal Verbs", "summary": "Express ability, possibility, permission, advice and obligation.", "plainExplanation": "Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would, shall) are special helper verbs that add meaning like ability, permission, obligation, possibility, or advice — without ever changing their own form. Two rules make them unusually simple compared to normal verbs: first, they never take -s, even with he/she/it ('she can', never 'she cans'); second, they're always followed directly by the base form of the main verb, with no 'to' in between ('you must arrive', never 'you must to arrive'). Each modal carries its own flavour: 'can' for ability or informal permission ('I can swim', 'Can I leave early?'), 'could' for past ability or polite requests ('Could you help me?'), 'must' for strong obligation or confident deduction ('You must finish this today'; 'That must be true'), 'should' for advice or recommendation ('You should see a doctor'), and 'might'/'may' for possibility or formal permission. Negatives are formed simply by adding 'not' straight after the modal, sometimes contracted: can't, mustn't, shouldn't — but be careful, because 'must not' and 'don't have to' mean very different things (must not = forbidden; don't have to = not required, but still allowed).", "illustration": "⚙️ MODAL VERBS — TWO RULES THAT NEVER CHANGE\nRULE 1: never add -s, even for he/she/it   → \"She can\" NOT \"She cans\"\nRULE 2: always + base verb, never + 'to'   → \"You must arrive\" NOT \"must to arrive\"\ncan/could   → ability, permission     \"I can swim.\"     \"Could you help?\"\nmust        → obligation, deduction   \"You must finish.\"  \"That must be true.\"\nshould      → advice                  \"You should rest.\"\nmight/may   → possibility, permission \"It might rain.\"", "steps": ["Decide what kind of meaning you need: ability, permission, advice, obligation, or possibility.", "Choose the modal that matches — can/could for ability, may/might for possibility, must/have to for obligation, should for advice.", "Always follow a modal verb with the base form of the main verb, with no 'to' (You must go, not You must to go).", "Modals do not change form for he/she/it — no extra -s is added (She can swim, not She cans swim).", "For negatives, add 'not' directly after the modal (cannot, should not, must not)."], "rule": "Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would, shall) add meaning such as ability, permission, possibility, advice or obligation to a main verb. They are always followed by the base form of the verb with no to, and they never take -s in the third person singular. Each modal can carry more than one meaning depending on context.", "structure": "Subject + modal + base verb", "breakdown": [{"term": "Ability — can / could", "definition": "Can expresses present ability; could expresses past ability or a more polite request.", "example": "\"He can swim.\" / \"She could read at the age of four.\""}, {"term": "Permission — can / may", "definition": "Both ask for or give permission; may is more formal.", "example": "\"May I leave early today?\""}, {"term": "Advice — should / ought to", "definition": "Recommends the best action without expressing strict obligation.", "example": "\"You should practise every day.\""}, {"term": "Obligation — must / have to", "definition": "Must often expresses the speaker's own rule; have to often expresses an external rule.", "example": "\"Students must follow the rules.\" / \"I have to renew my visa.\""}, {"term": "Possibility — might / may / could", "definition": "Expresses that something is possible but not certain.", "example": "\"It might rain later.\""}], "examples": ["You should practise.", "He can swim.", "Students must follow the rules."], "mistakes": ["Adding -s after a modal: 'She can speaks English' should be 'She can speak English'.", "Adding to after a modal: 'He must to leave' should be 'He must leave'.", "Confusing must not (prohibition) with don't have to (no obligation): 'You mustn't come' means it is forbidden, while 'You don't have to come' means it is optional."], "tip": "After any modal verb, always use the plain base form of the next verb — no -s, no -ing, no to.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["She can speaks English.", "She can speak English.", "She can speaking English.", "She can spoke English."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which modal commonly gives advice?", "options": ["should", "mustn't", "can", "wouldn't"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g14", "day": 14, "level": "B1", "title": "Comparatives and Superlatives", "summary": "Compare people, things and ideas accurately.", "plainExplanation": "Comparatives compare two things ('faster than', 'more interesting than'), and superlatives single out the most extreme one in a group of three or more ('the fastest', 'the most interesting'). Which form to use depends mostly on how long the adjective is. Short adjectives (usually one syllable, sometimes two) add -er/-est directly: fast → faster → fastest, big → bigger → biggest (note the doubled consonant), happy → happier → happiest (y changes to i). Longer adjectives (usually three or more syllables, and many two-syllable ones) instead use 'more'/'most' in front, leaving the adjective itself unchanged: interesting → more interesting → most interesting, beautiful → more beautiful → most beautiful. A handful of very common adjectives break both patterns and simply have to be memorised: good → better → best, bad → worse → worst, far → farther/further → farthest/furthest. To express equality rather than difference, use 'as...as': 'He is as tall as his brother' means they're the same height — no comparative form needed at all.", "illustration": "📏 COMPARATIVE (2 things) vs SUPERLATIVE (3+ things, the extreme)\nSHORT adjectives (1 syllable)  → add -er / -est\n   fast → faster → the fastest\n   big  → bigger → the biggest   (double the consonant)\nLONG adjectives (3+ syllables) → use more / most\n   interesting → more interesting → the most interesting\nIRREGULAR (memorise!)\n   good → better → best   |   bad → worse → worst\nEQUALITY:  as + adjective + as   → \"He is as tall as his brother.\"", "steps": ["Count the syllables in the adjective.", "For short adjectives (1 syllable, some 2-syllable), add -er/-est (tall → taller → tallest).", "For longer adjectives (2+ syllables), use more/most before the adjective (more interesting, most interesting).", "Watch for spelling changes: happy → happier, big → bigger (double the consonant).", "Memorise irregular forms separately: good → better → best, bad → worse → worst."], "rule": "Comparatives compare two things, and superlatives single out the extreme within a group of three or more. Short adjectives (one syllable, or two syllables ending in -y) usually add -er/-est; longer adjectives use more/most. A small group of common adjectives is irregular and must be memorised, and equality is expressed with as...as.", "structure": "A + be + comparative + than B | the + superlative", "breakdown": [{"term": "Short adjectives (-er / -est)", "definition": "One-syllable adjectives, and many two-syllable ones ending in -y, add -er/-est (with spelling changes like happy → happier).", "example": "cheap → cheaper → the cheapest."}, {"term": "Long adjectives (more / most)", "definition": "Adjectives with two or more syllables generally use more/most instead of an ending.", "example": "important → more important → the most important."}, {"term": "Irregular forms", "definition": "A few common adjectives change completely rather than following either pattern.", "example": "good → better → the best; bad → worse → the worst."}, {"term": "Equality — as...as", "definition": "Used to say two things are the same in some quality.", "example": "\"This book is as useful as that one.\""}], "examples": ["This book is cheaper than that one.", "This is the most useful chapter.", "She is more confident than before."], "mistakes": ["Combining more with -er: 'more cheaper' should be either 'cheaper' or 'more expensive'.", "Forgetting the after a superlative: 'This is most useful chapter' should be 'This is the most useful chapter'.", "Missing than after a comparative: 'This is cheaper that one' should be 'This is cheaper than that one'."], "tip": "Count the syllables: one (or short two) → add -er/-est; two or more → use more/most. Check irregular adjectives separately.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["This is more cheap than that.", "This is cheaper than that.", "This is cheapest than that.", "This is cheap than that."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the superlative form of 'important'.", "options": ["importantest", "more important", "most important", "the more important"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g15", "day": 15, "level": "B1", "title": "Prepositions of Time and Place", "summary": "Use in, on, at and other prepositions precisely.", "plainExplanation": "The three most common time prepositions — at, on, in — each cover a different 'zoom level' of time, from most specific to most general. Use 'at' for precise points in time: clock times ('at 9 o'clock'), and a few fixed expressions ('at night', 'at the weekend' in British English, 'at Christmas'). Use 'on' for days and dates: specific days ('on Monday'), dates ('on July 4th'), and specific single occasions ('on my birthday'). Use 'in' for longer, broader periods: months, years, seasons, and centuries ('in July', 'in 2024', 'in summer', 'in the 21st century'). Place prepositions follow a similar logic but for space instead of time: 'at' for a specific point ('at the bus stop'), 'on' for a surface ('on the table'), and 'in' for an enclosed space or area ('in the box', 'in London'). Beyond these three, many prepositional phrases are simply fixed idioms that don't follow a predictable rule and need to be learned as whole chunks — 'at home', 'in the corner', 'on the way' — because trying to derive them logically often leads you astray.", "illustration": "🕐 TIME PREPOSITIONS — ZOOM FROM SPECIFIC TO GENERAL\nAT   → exact point in time     at 9 o'clock · at night · at the weekend\nON   → days and dates          on Monday · on July 4th · on my birthday\nIN   → longer periods          in July · in 2024 · in summer · in the 21st century\n\n📍 PLACE PREPOSITIONS — SAME LOGIC, FOR SPACE\nAT   → a point           at the bus stop\nON   → a surface         on the table\nIN   → an enclosed space in the box · in London", "steps": ["For time, use 'at' for precise clock times and short periods (at 6pm, at night).", "Use 'on' for specific days and dates (on Monday, on July 1st).", "Use 'in' for longer periods — months, years, seasons, centuries (in May, in 2024, in summer).", "For place, use 'at' for a specific point (at the bus stop), 'on' for a surface (on the table), and 'in' for an enclosed space (in the box, in London).", "Memorise common fixed expressions that don't follow the general pattern (in time, on time, at the weekend/on the weekend)."], "rule": "Prepositions of time and place connect a noun to the rest of the sentence by showing when or where something happens. The three most common time prepositions follow a size pattern — at for a precise point, on for a day or date, and in for a longer period — and the same logic extends loosely to place: at for a point, on for a surface, and in for an enclosed or larger space.", "structure": "at 8:00 | on Monday | in July | in 2026", "breakdown": [{"term": "at — precise point", "definition": "Used for exact times and specific points in a place.", "example": "\"The class starts at 9.\" / \"Meet me at the entrance.\""}, {"term": "on — day, date, surface", "definition": "Used for specific days, dates, and physical surfaces.", "example": "\"I study on Monday.\" / \"The book is on the table.\""}, {"term": "in — period, enclosed space", "definition": "Used for longer periods (months, years, seasons) and enclosed or larger areas.", "example": "\"She arrived in June.\" / \"The keys are in the drawer.\""}, {"term": "Movement prepositions — to / into / onto", "definition": "To shows direction toward a destination; into and onto show movement to the inside/surface of something.", "example": "\"He walked into the room and put the bag onto the shelf.\""}], "examples": ["The class starts at 9.", "I study on Monday.", "She arrived in June."], "mistakes": ["Mixing up at/on/in for time: 'at Monday' should be 'on Monday'.", "Using in for years incorrectly: 'on 2026' should be 'in 2026'.", "Confusing in and into: 'He walked in the room' (static meaning) is better as 'He walked into the room' when describing movement."], "tip": "Think small to big for time: at (clock time) → on (day/date) → in (month/year/period).", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct phrase.", "options": ["at Monday", "on Monday", "in Monday", "by Monday at every case"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct phrase for a year.", "options": ["at 2026", "on 2026", "in 2026", "by in 2026"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g16", "day": 16, "level": "B1", "title": "Gerunds and Infinitives", "summary": "Know when verbs are followed by -ing or to + verb.", "plainExplanation": "Gerunds (verb + -ing used as a noun, like 'swimming') and infinitives (to + base verb, like 'to swim') both let you use a verb's action as if it were a noun — but which one follows a particular verb isn't predictable from meaning alone, so it has to be learned verb by verb. Some verbs are always followed by a gerund: avoid, enjoy, finish, suggest, mind, consider, practise ('She enjoys reading', 'They suggested leaving early'). Others are always followed by an infinitive: want, decide, plan, hope, promise, agree, manage ('He wants to leave', 'They decided to wait'). A tricky middle group changes meaning depending on which one follows: 'stop' + gerund means ending an activity ('I stopped smoking' — I no longer smoke), while 'stop' + infinitive means pausing to do something else ('I stopped to smoke' — I paused another activity in order to smoke). One more wrinkle: after a preposition (including 'to' when it's genuinely a preposition, not part of an infinitive), always use a gerund — 'I look forward to meeting you' uses the gerund 'meeting' because this 'to' is a preposition meaning 'towards', not the infinitive marker.", "illustration": "🔀 GERUND (-ing as noun) vs INFINITIVE (to + base verb)\nALWAYS + GERUND      → avoid · enjoy · finish · suggest · mind · practise\n                        \"She enjoys reading.\"\nALWAYS + INFINITIVE  → want · decide · plan · hope · promise · agree\n                        \"He wants to leave.\"\nMEANING CHANGES:\n   stop + -ing  = end a habit     \"I stopped smoking.\" (quit)\n   stop + to    = pause to do X   \"I stopped to smoke.\" (paused for a cigarette)\nAFTER A PREPOSITION → always -ing:  \"I look forward to meeting you.\"", "steps": ["Identify the main verb in the sentence that controls the pattern (enjoy, want, decide, avoid, etc.).", "Check whether that verb is followed by a gerund (enjoy swimming), an infinitive (want to swim), or both with a meaning change (stop to rest vs stop resting).", "Use a gerund after prepositions (interested in learning, good at cooking).", "Use the infinitive to express purpose (I called to ask a question).", "When in doubt with a new verb, look up or memorise its typical pattern rather than guessing from translation."], "rule": "Some verbs are followed by a gerund (verb-ing) acting as a noun, others by a to-infinitive, and some allow both — sometimes with a change in meaning. There is no simple rule for which pattern a verb takes, so the safest approach is to learn common verbs in groups.", "structure": "verb + verb-ing | verb + to + base", "breakdown": [{"term": "verb + gerund", "definition": "Verbs like enjoy, avoid, finish, mind, suggest and practise are followed by -ing.", "example": "\"I enjoy reading.\" / \"They avoided making mistakes.\""}, {"term": "verb + to-infinitive", "definition": "Verbs like decide, want, hope, plan, promise and agree are followed by to + base verb.", "example": "\"She decided to leave early.\""}, {"term": "verb + either, same meaning", "definition": "Verbs like like, love, start, begin and continue can take either form with little or no change in meaning.", "example": "\"He likes swimming.\" = \"He likes to swim.\""}, {"term": "verb + either, different meaning", "definition": "Verbs like stop, remember and try change meaning depending on the form used.", "example": "\"He stopped smoking\" (quit) vs. \"He stopped to smoke\" (paused in order to smoke)."}], "examples": ["I enjoy reading.", "She decided to leave.", "They avoided making mistakes."], "mistakes": ["Using to after a gerund-only verb: 'I enjoy to read' should be 'I enjoy reading'.", "Using -ing after an infinitive-only verb: 'She decided leaving' should be 'She decided to leave'.", "Mixing up the stop + gerund / stop + infinitive contrast, which changes the meaning of the sentence entirely."], "tip": "When unfamiliar with a verb's pattern, notice the pattern in real examples and learn it as a fixed pair, rather than guessing from a general rule.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the natural sentence.", "options": ["I enjoy to read.", "I enjoy reading.", "I enjoying read.", "I enjoy readed."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct form: 'She decided ___ early.'", "options": ["leave", "leaving", "to leave", "left"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g17", "day": 17, "level": "B1", "title": "Relative Clauses", "summary": "Combine information using who, which, that and where.", "plainExplanation": "Relative clauses add extra information about a noun using connector words like who, which, that, whose, where, and when — essentially letting you combine two short sentences into one smoother one. 'Who' and 'that' refer to people ('the man who called', or informally 'the man that called'); 'which' and 'that' refer to things ('the book which/that I read'); 'whose' shows possession for people or things ('the woman whose car was stolen'); 'where' replaces a place ('the city where I was born'); 'when' replaces a time ('the year when we met'). There are two very different types of relative clause, and mixing them up changes meaning. A defining (restrictive) clause is essential — it tells you exactly which person or thing you mean, has no commas, and can use 'that': 'The students who studied passed the exam' (only the ones who studied — the clause is necessary to know which students). A non-defining (non-restrictive) clause just adds extra, non-essential detail, always sits between commas, and can never use 'that': 'My brother, who lives in Spain, is visiting' (I have one brother; the clause is just bonus information, not needed to identify him).", "illustration": "🔗 RELATIVE CLAUSES — CONNECT TWO IDEAS INTO ONE\nwho → people   |   which → things   |   whose → possession   |   where → place   |   when → time\n\nDEFINING (essential, no commas, 'that' OK):\n  \"The students who studied passed the exam.\"  ← tells us WHICH students\n\nNON-DEFINING (extra info, commas required, NEVER 'that'):\n  \"My brother, who lives in Spain, is visiting.\"  ← bonus detail, brother already identified", "steps": ["Find the noun you want to describe further.", "Choose the right relative pronoun: who/that for people, which/that for things, whose for possession, where for places, when for times.", "Decide if the clause is defining (essential, no commas) or non-defining (extra, needs commas) — 'The man who called is my boss' vs 'My boss, who called earlier, is busy.'", "In defining clauses, 'that' can replace who/which, and the pronoun can sometimes be dropped if it's the object (The book (that) I read).", "Never drop the relative pronoun in a non-defining clause, and never use 'that' after a comma."], "rule": "A relative clause adds extra information about a noun without starting a new sentence. Who typically refers to people, which typically refers to things, that can replace who or which in defining (essential) clauses, whose shows possession, and where/when refer to place and time. Defining clauses identify which noun we mean and use no commas; non-defining clauses add extra, non-essential information and are separated by commas.", "structure": "noun + relative word + clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "who — people", "definition": "Refers back to a person as subject or object of the clause.", "example": "\"The student who won is my friend.\""}, {"term": "which — things", "definition": "Refers back to an object, animal or idea.", "example": "\"The book which I bought is useful.\""}, {"term": "that — people or things (defining only)", "definition": "Can replace who or which in essential, defining clauses, but not in non-defining ones.", "example": "\"This is the phone that I lost.\""}, {"term": "whose — possession", "definition": "Shows that something belongs to the person or thing just mentioned.", "example": "\"That's the writer whose book won the prize.\""}, {"term": "where / when — place / time", "definition": "Refer back to a location or a time already mentioned.", "example": "\"This is the place where we met.\" / \"That was the year when I graduated.\""}], "examples": ["The student who won is my friend.", "The book that I bought is useful.", "This is the place where we met."], "mistakes": ["Using that in a non-defining clause: 'My father, that is a doctor, works nights' should use who: 'My father, who is a doctor, works nights.'", "Forgetting commas around non-defining (extra) information: 'My brother who lives in Yangon called' (implies more than one brother) vs. 'My brother, who lives in Yangon, called' (one brother, extra detail).", "Using whose for things without ownership sense: check that the relationship is genuinely possessive before using whose."], "tip": "Ask: does the clause identify which one (no commas, that/who/which OK), or just add extra detail (commas, no that)?", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct relative word for a person.", "options": ["who", "where", "whicher", "what"], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["The book who I bought is new.", "The book which I bought is new.", "The book where I bought is new.", "The book whom I bought is new."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g18", "day": 18, "level": "B1", "title": "Conditionals 0 and 1", "summary": "Express general truths and real future possibilities.", "plainExplanation": "Zero and first conditionals both talk about real, possible situations, but at different levels of certainty. The zero conditional expresses a general truth or scientific fact that's always true, using present simple in both halves: 'If you heat ice, it melts' (this is always true, every single time). The first conditional talks about a specific, realistic future possibility, using present simple in the 'if' clause and 'will' + base verb in the result clause: 'If it rains tomorrow, we will stay home' (a real possibility about a specific future situation, not a guaranteed law of nature). The easiest way to tell them apart: could you replace 'if' with 'when' or 'whenever' without changing the meaning? If yes, it's zero conditional, because it's describing something that always happens ('When you heat ice, it melts' still makes perfect sense). If replacing 'if' with 'when' would sound strange or overly certain, it's first conditional, because you're describing one specific possible future, not a universal rule.", "illustration": "🌡️ ZERO CONDITIONAL — always true, a fact\nIf + present simple  ,  present simple\n\"If you heat ice, it melts.\"        (swap 'if' → 'when' — still true!)\n\n🌦️ FIRST CONDITIONAL — a real future possibility\nIf + present simple  ,  will + base verb\n\"If it rains tomorrow, we will stay home.\"   (one specific possible future)", "steps": ["Zero conditional: use if + present simple, present simple — for facts that are always true (If you heat ice, it melts).", "First conditional: use if + present simple, will + base verb — for a realistic future possibility (If it rains, I will stay home).", "Keep the 'if' clause in the present tense even when talking about the future — never use 'will' in the if-clause itself.", "The order of clauses can be reversed; add a comma only when 'if' comes first.", "Replace 'if' with 'when' in the zero conditional if the result is a certainty, not just a possibility."], "rule": "Zero and first conditionals both describe things that are real or likely, not imaginary. The zero conditional (if/when + present, present) expresses facts and general truths that are always true. The first conditional (if + present, will + base) expresses a specific, realistic possibility in the future and its likely result. Unless means 'if...not' and often replaces if + negative.", "structure": "If + present, present | If + present, will + base", "breakdown": [{"term": "Zero conditional — general truths", "definition": "Both clauses in the present simple; true every time, like a scientific fact or fixed rule.", "example": "\"If you heat ice, it melts.\""}, {"term": "Zero conditional — instructions/rules", "definition": "Can also express habitual instructions or fixed procedures.", "example": "\"If the light turns red, drivers stop.\""}, {"term": "First conditional — real future possibility", "definition": "If + present simple in the condition, will + base verb in the result — a realistic future situation.", "example": "\"If it rains, we will stay home.\""}, {"term": "unless = if...not", "definition": "Introduces the one exception that would stop the result from happening.", "example": "\"We will be late unless we leave now.\" (= if we don't leave now)"}], "examples": ["If you heat ice, it melts.", "If I finish early, I will call you.", "If it rains, we will stay home."], "mistakes": ["Using will in the if-clause: 'If it will rain, we will stay home' should be 'If it rains, we will stay home'.", "Mixing conditional types: 'If I study, I would pass' mixes first conditional's if-clause with second conditional's result — it should be 'If I study, I will pass'.", "Misusing unless with a double negative: 'Unless you don't hurry, you'll be late' should simply be 'Unless you hurry, you'll be late'."], "tip": "Zero conditional = always true. First conditional = a real, likely future chance. Both if-clauses stay in the present tense.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which is a zero conditional?", "options": ["If you heat ice, it melts.", "If I study, I will pass.", "If I studied, I would pass.", "If I had studied, I would have passed."], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Choose the first conditional sentence.", "options": ["If she studies, she will improve.", "If she studied, she would improve.", "If she had studied, she would have improved.", "If she studies, she improved yesterday."], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g19", "day": 19, "level": "B1", "title": "Passive Voice", "summary": "Focus on an action or result rather than the doer.", "plainExplanation": "The passive voice shifts the spotlight from who does an action to what happens to something or someone — you flip the sentence so the object of the action becomes the subject. Build it with a form of 'be' plus the past participle: 'The cake was baked' instead of 'She baked the cake'. Use the passive when the person doing the action is unknown, unimportant, or obvious from context ('The window was broken' — we don't know or care who did it), when you want to sound more formal or objective, especially in scientific, academic, or news writing ('The results were analysed'), or when the action's receiver is simply more important to the story than the doer ('The Mona Lisa was painted in the 16th century' — the painting matters more than naming Leonardo again). If you do need to mention who performed the action in a passive sentence, add 'by' plus the doer at the end: 'The cake was baked by my grandmother.' A useful check: only verbs that take a direct object (transitive verbs) can be made passive — you can't passivise 'arrive' or 'happen', because there's no object to promote to subject position.", "illustration": "🔄 PASSIVE VOICE — FLIP THE SPOTLIGHT\nACTIVE:   [She]        [baked]         [the cake].\n           subject/doer   verb          object/receiver\n              ↓ flip ↓\nPASSIVE:  [The cake]   [was baked]     (by her).\n           new subject   be + p.p.      doer (optional, often dropped)\n\nUse passive when: doer is unknown/unimportant · formal/scientific tone · the result matters more than who did it", "steps": ["Find the object of the active sentence — this becomes the new subject in the passive.", "Use the correct form of be + past participle (is made, was written, has been sent).", "Match the tense of 'be' to the tense of the original active sentence (write → is written; wrote → was written).", "Only add 'by + agent' if it's genuinely important to say who did the action.", "Avoid overusing the passive — it can sound vague or evasive if used when the doer actually matters."], "rule": "Passive voice shifts the focus from who performs an action to the action itself or the person/thing affected by it. It is formed with the correct tense of be plus the past participle of the main verb, and the tense information is carried entirely by be. Passive voice is common when the doer is unknown, unimportant, obvious, or when the writer wants to sound more objective, as in formal or academic writing.", "structure": "Object + be (correct tense) + past participle", "breakdown": [{"term": "Basic form", "definition": "be (in the required tense) + past participle.", "example": "\"The essay is checked every day.\""}, {"term": "Doer unknown or unimportant", "definition": "Used when we do not know, or do not need to say, who performed the action.", "example": "\"The window was broken last night.\""}, {"term": "Adding the agent with by", "definition": "If the doer is important enough to mention, add by + agent at the end.", "example": "\"The bridge was built by a local company in 2010.\""}, {"term": "Passive across tenses", "definition": "Each tense has its own passive form, always built from be + past participle.", "example": "\"The results will be announced tomorrow.\" (future passive)"}], "examples": ["The essay is checked every day.", "The bridge was built in 2010.", "The results will be announced tomorrow."], "mistakes": ["Forgetting the past participle: 'The bridge was build in 2010' should be 'The bridge was built in 2010'.", "Using the wrong form of be for the tense: 'The essay checked every day' is missing is: 'The essay is checked every day.'", "Overusing by + agent when the doer is unimportant: 'The window was broken by someone' is usually better simply as 'The window was broken.'"], "tip": "Passive = object of the active sentence + correct be + past participle. Only add by + agent if naming the doer really matters.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the passive sentence.", "options": ["Students check the essays.", "The essays are checked.", "Students are checking essays.", "Students checked essays."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Complete: 'The results ___ tomorrow.'", "options": ["will announce", "will be announced", "are announce", "announced be"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g20", "day": 20, "level": "B2", "title": "Reported Speech", "summary": "Report what someone said while controlling tense, pronouns and time expressions.", "plainExplanation": "Reported speech (also called indirect speech) is how you retell what someone said without quoting them word for word. When the reporting verb is in the past tense ('She said...', 'He told me...'), the tense inside the quote usually shifts one step further into the past — this is called 'backshift'. Present simple becomes past simple ('I work here' → she said she worked there), present continuous becomes past continuous ('I am working' → she said she was working), present perfect becomes past perfect ('I have finished' → she said she had finished), and 'will' becomes 'would' ('I will call' → she said she would call). Time and place words shift too, since you're often reporting from a different time and place than the original: 'today' becomes 'that day', 'tomorrow' becomes 'the next day', 'here' becomes 'there', 'this' becomes 'that'. Pronouns also change to match the new speaker's perspective: if Maria says 'I am tired', reporting it becomes 'Maria said she was tired', not 'I was tired'. One important exception: if the reported statement is still true or a general fact at the time of reporting, backshift is optional — 'She said she lives in Rome' is fine if she still lives there.", "illustration": "⏪ REPORTED SPEECH — SHIFT ONE STEP BACK IN TIME\nDIRECT: \"I am working.\"     →  REPORTED: She said she was working.\nDIRECT: \"I work here.\"      →  REPORTED: She said she worked there.\nDIRECT: \"I have finished.\"  →  REPORTED: She said she had finished.\nDIRECT: \"I will call.\"      →  REPORTED: She said she would call.\n\nTIME/PLACE WORDS ALSO SHIFT:\ntoday → that day   |   tomorrow → the next day   |   here → there   |   this → that", "steps": ["Identify the original direct speech and the reporting verb (said, told, asked).", "Shift the tense back one step: present simple → past simple, present perfect → past perfect, will → would.", "Change pronouns to match the new speaker's perspective (I → she/he).", "Change time and place words if needed (today → that day, here → there, tomorrow → the next day).", "For reported questions, use normal word order (not question order) and no question mark: She asked where I lived, not She asked where did I live."], "rule": "Reported (indirect) speech reports what someone said without quoting their exact words. When the reporting verb is in the past (said, told), present tenses in the original statement typically shift one step back into the past, and pronouns and time/place expressions often change to fit the new speaker and time. Reported questions and commands follow their own word-order and structure rules.", "structure": "reporting verb + (that) + reported clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "Statements — tense shift", "definition": "Present tenses in direct speech usually shift back one tense in reported speech.", "example": "\"I am tired\" → She said (that) she was tired."}, {"term": "Reported yes/no questions", "definition": "Use if/whether, and change the question word order back to statement order (no auxiliary inversion).", "example": "\"Are you ready?\" → He asked if I was ready."}, {"term": "Reported wh-questions", "definition": "Keep the question word, but use statement word order, not question word order.", "example": "\"Where do you live?\" → She asked where I lived."}, {"term": "Reported commands", "definition": "Use tell/ask + object + to + base verb, instead of quoting the imperative directly.", "example": "\"Sit down!\" → He told them to sit down."}, {"term": "Time/place word changes", "definition": "Words like now, today, here and tomorrow often change to fit the new time and place of reporting.", "example": "\"I'll call tomorrow\" → She said she would call the next day."}], "examples": ["She said that she was busy.", "He told me that he needed help.", "They said they would return later."], "mistakes": ["Keeping question word order in reported questions: 'She asked where did I live' should be 'She asked where I lived'.", "Forgetting that/if/whether or misplacing the object: 'He told that me he was tired' should be 'He told me that he was tired'.", "Leaving the tense unshifted when the reporting verb is past: 'She said she is tired' is usually better as 'She said she was tired'."], "tip": "Turn the reported clause into a normal statement first (subject + verb, no question order), then shift the tense one step back if the reporting verb is past.", "quiz": [{"q": "Direct: 'I am busy.' Reported: She said that she ___ busy.", "options": ["is", "was", "be", "has"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct reporting pattern.", "options": ["He told that me he was tired.", "He told me that he was tired.", "He said me that he was tired.", "He told me he tired was."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g21", "day": 21, "level": "B2", "title": "Conditionals 2 and 3", "summary": "Discuss hypothetical present/future situations and unreal past situations.", "plainExplanation": "Second and third conditionals both talk about unreal, hypothetical situations — but at different points in time. The second conditional imagines an unreal present or future — something that isn't true now, or is unlikely to happen: 'If I won the lottery, I would travel the world' (I haven't won it, and probably won't). Structure: if + past simple, would + base verb. The third conditional imagines an unreal past — something that didn't actually happen, and you're speculating about what would have been different if it had: 'If I had studied harder, I would have passed the exam' (I didn't study harder, and I didn't pass — both parts are now impossible to change, since the moment has passed). Structure: if + past perfect, would have + past participle. A quick way to keep them straight: second conditional imagines a different present/future ('would' + base verb), while third conditional imagines a different past that can never be changed now ('would have' + past participle) — the extra 'have' is your signal that you've gone one step further back in imagined time.", "illustration": "💭 SECOND CONDITIONAL — unreal present/future\nIf + past simple  ,  would + base verb\n\"If I won the lottery, I would travel the world.\"   (haven't won — unlikely/imaginary)\n\n⏳ THIRD CONDITIONAL — unreal past (can't be changed now)\nIf + had + past participle  ,  would have + past participle\n\"If I had studied harder, I would have passed.\"   (didn't study — too late now)\n\nExtra 'have' = one more step back into imagined time", "steps": ["Second conditional: if + past simple, would + base verb — for something unlikely or imaginary now (If I won the lottery, I would travel).", "Third conditional: if + past perfect, would have + past participle — for an imagined different past (If I had studied, I would have passed).", "Remember: the 'if' clause tense does not match its real-world time — past simple in the second conditional still refers to the present/future.", "Use 'were' instead of 'was' for all subjects in formal second conditional style (If I were you...).", "Don't mix real tenses into these — 'If I would win' is a common error; keep 'would' only in the result clause."], "rule": "Second and third conditionals both describe unreal or hypothetical situations. The second conditional (if + past simple, would + base) talks about an imaginary present or future — something unlikely, impossible, or purely hypothetical right now. The third conditional (if + past perfect, would have + past participle) talks about an unreal past — something that did not actually happen, often expressing regret or a different imagined outcome.", "structure": "If + past, would + base | If + had + pp, would have + pp", "breakdown": [{"term": "Second conditional — hypothetical present/future", "definition": "If + past simple, would + base verb, for something imaginary or unlikely now.", "example": "\"If I had more time, I would study more.\""}, {"term": "'were' for all persons", "definition": "In formal or careful English, were (not was) is used with I/he/she/it in the if-clause of hypothetical sentences.", "example": "\"If I were you, I would apologise.\""}, {"term": "Third conditional — unreal past", "definition": "If + past perfect, would have + past participle, for a different past that did not happen.", "example": "\"If they had left earlier, they would have arrived on time.\""}, {"term": "Mixed conditional (brief note)", "definition": "Occasionally an unreal past condition produces a present result: if + past perfect, would + base.", "example": "\"If she had studied medicine, she would be a doctor now.\""}], "examples": ["If I had more time, I would study more.", "If she were here, she would help.", "If they had left earlier, they would have arrived on time."], "mistakes": ["Using would in the if-clause: 'If I would have time, I would go' should be 'If I had time, I would go'.", "Mixing past simple with third conditional's result: 'If I had had time, I would go' should be 'I would have gone' to match the past.", "Using was instead of were in formal hypothetical statements: 'If I was you' is common in speech, but 'If I were you' is the traditional, more formal form."], "tip": "Count how far from reality the idea is: unreal now/future → second conditional. Unreal in the past → third conditional.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence is second conditional?", "options": ["If I have time, I will go.", "If I had time, I would go.", "If I had had time, I would have gone.", "If I have had time, I went."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which form belongs in a third conditional if-clause?", "options": ["past simple", "present simple", "past perfect", "future simple"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g22", "day": 22, "level": "B2", "title": "Complex Sentences and Subordination", "summary": "Build precise relationships between ideas using dependent clauses.", "plainExplanation": "A complex sentence combines one independent clause (a complete thought that could stand alone as its own sentence) with one or more dependent clauses (an incomplete thought that needs the main clause to make sense), joined by a subordinating conjunction like because, although, since, while, if, when, or unless. The dependent clause can come before or after the main clause, but punctuation changes depending on the order: if the dependent clause comes first, put a comma after it ('Although it was raining, we went for a walk'); if the main clause comes first, no comma is needed ('We went for a walk although it was raining'). A very common error is the sentence fragment — writing a dependent clause on its own, as if it were a complete sentence: 'Because I was tired.' is not a full sentence, because it leaves the reader waiting for the rest of the thought; it needs a main clause attached, like 'I went to bed early because I was tired.' Subordination is what allows you to show relationships between ideas — cause and effect, contrast, condition, time — rather than just listing simple sentences one after another.", "illustration": "🧩 COMPLEX SENTENCE = MAIN CLAUSE + DEPENDENT CLAUSE\n[Although it was raining]₁, [we went for a walk]₂.\n     dependent (needs help)     independent (stands alone)\n     ↑ comma required when dependent clause comes FIRST\n\n[We went for a walk]₂ [although it was raining]₁.\n     independent first — NO comma needed\n\n⚠️ FRAGMENT ERROR: \"Because I was tired.\"  ← incomplete, needs a main clause attached", "steps": ["Identify your main idea — this becomes the independent clause.", "Identify the supporting idea and its relationship to the main one (reason, time, contrast, condition).", "Choose a subordinating conjunction that matches: because/since (reason), although/even though (contrast), when/while (time), if/unless (condition).", "Attach the dependent clause before or after the main clause — add a comma only when the dependent clause comes first.", "Check that the dependent clause cannot stand alone as a full sentence — if it can, you may have written two independent clauses instead."], "rule": "Complex sentences combine a main (independent) clause with one or more dependent clauses using subordinating conjunctions. The choice of subordinator signals the exact relationship between ideas: cause (because, since, as), contrast (although, whereas, even though), condition (unless, provided that), or time (while, as soon as, once). Precise subordination makes writing clearer and more sophisticated than simply joining ideas with and.", "structure": "Main clause + subordinator + dependent clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "Cause — because / since / as", "definition": "Introduces the reason for the action in the main clause.", "example": "\"Because she practised, her writing improved.\""}, {"term": "Contrast — although / whereas / even though", "definition": "Shows an unexpected or opposite relationship between two ideas.", "example": "\"Although it was expensive, the course was useful.\""}, {"term": "Condition — unless / provided that", "definition": "Introduces a condition, similar to if, often with a negative or restrictive sense.", "example": "\"Unless you revise, you may forget the rule.\""}, {"term": "Time — while / as soon as / once", "definition": "Places the main clause's action in relation to a time frame.", "example": "\"As soon as the lesson ends, I will practise.\""}], "examples": ["Although it was expensive, the course was useful.", "Because she practised, her writing improved.", "Unless you revise, you may forget the rule."], "mistakes": ["Adding but after although: 'Although it rained, but we went out' should be 'Although it rained, we went out'.", "Choosing the wrong logical connector: using because when the relationship is actually contrast, or although when it is actually cause.", "Leaving a dependent clause standing alone as a full sentence: 'Because she practised.' is incomplete without a main clause."], "tip": "Identify the logical relationship first — cause, contrast, condition or time — then pick the subordinator that matches it exactly.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which connector shows contrast?", "options": ["because", "although", "therefore", "so that"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["Although it rained, we went out.", "Although it rained, but we went out.", "Although rained, we went out.", "Although it raining, we went out."], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g23", "day": 23, "level": "B2", "title": "Modals of Deduction", "summary": "Express logical certainty and possibility about present and past situations.", "plainExplanation": "Modals of deduction let you express how certain you are about something, based on evidence or logic, without directly claiming to know for a fact. In the present, 'must' shows strong positive certainty ('The lights are off — he must be asleep'), 'can't' or 'couldn't' shows strong negative certainty (the opposite of must — 'She can't be at home, her car isn't here'), and 'might', 'may', or 'could' show a weaker, less certain possibility ('He might be running late'). For the past, you add 'have' plus the past participle to each modal: 'must have' for strong certainty about something that already happened ('She must have left already — her coat is gone'), 'can't have' or 'couldn't have' for strong certainty that something didn't happen ('He can't have finished already — it's only been five minutes'), and 'might have'/'may have'/'could have' for a weaker possibility about the past ('They might have missed the train'). Notice there's no 'mustn't have' for negative deduction — English uses 'can't have' or 'couldn't have' instead, which is a common and understandable point of confusion for learners.", "illustration": "🔍 MODALS OF DEDUCTION — HOW SURE ARE YOU?\nPRESENT:\n  must    → strongly sure YES   \"He must be asleep.\" (lights off = evidence)\n  can't   → strongly sure NO    \"She can't be home.\" (car's gone = evidence)\n  might/may/could → not sure    \"He might be late.\"\n\nPAST: add 'have' + past participle\n  must have    → \"She must have left.\"\n  can't have   → \"He can't have finished already.\"   (⚠️ NOT 'mustn't have')\n  might have   → \"They might have missed the train.\"", "steps": ["Assess how confident you are in your guess, based on the evidence you have.", "For strong positive certainty, use must + base verb (present) or must have + past participle (past): He must be tired / He must have forgotten.", "For possibility, use might/could/may + base verb or + have + past participle: She might be at work / She might have left already.", "For strong negative certainty, use can't/couldn't + base verb or + have + past participle — never 'mustn't' for this meaning: That can't be true.", "Match the modal + verb form to whether you are deducing about now/general (base verb) or the past (have + past participle)."], "rule": "Modals of deduction let a speaker express how certain they are about something, based on evidence or logic, rather than stating a fact directly. Must expresses strong positive certainty, can't expresses strong negative certainty, and might/may/could express possibility without certainty. To make a deduction about the past, add have + past participle after the modal.", "structure": "must/might/can't + base | must/might/can't + have + pp", "breakdown": [{"term": "must — strong positive deduction", "definition": "Used when the evidence strongly points to one logical conclusion.", "example": "\"She must be tired — she worked all night.\""}, {"term": "can't — strong negative deduction", "definition": "Used when something is logically almost impossible, based on evidence.", "example": "\"They can't be home — the lights are off.\""}, {"term": "might / may / could — possibility", "definition": "Used when something is possible but not certain either way.", "example": "\"He might be at home.\""}, {"term": "Past deduction — modal + have + past participle", "definition": "Adds have + past participle to make a deduction about a past situation.", "example": "\"They can't have forgotten the meeting.\" / \"She must have left early.\""}], "examples": ["She must be tired.", "He might be at home.", "They can't have forgotten the meeting."], "mistakes": ["Using must not instead of can't for strong negative deduction: 'She must not be at home' (prohibition-like meaning) should usually be 'She can't be at home' (deduction).", "Wrong form after the modal in past deduction: 'She must left' should be 'She must have left'.", "Using an -ing form after have: 'She must have leaving' should be 'She must have left'."], "tip": "Strength of evidence decides the modal: certain positive → must, certain negative → can't, unsure either way → might/may/could.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which modal shows strong positive deduction?", "options": ["must", "might", "can't", "wouldn't"], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Choose the past deduction.", "options": ["She must have left.", "She must leaving.", "She must left.", "She must to leave."], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g24", "day": 24, "level": "B2", "title": "Noun Clauses", "summary": "Use clauses as subjects or objects to express complex ideas.", "plainExplanation": "A noun clause is an entire clause that does the job of a single noun in a sentence — it can be the subject, the object of a verb, or the object of a preposition, wherever a noun could normally go. Noun clauses are commonly introduced by 'that' ('I believe that she is right'), question words like what, where, why, how, who ('I don't know what he wants'), or 'whether'/'if' for yes-no questions embedded inside a sentence ('I wonder whether/if she's coming'). A distinctive and important rule: once a question becomes a noun clause embedded inside a larger sentence, it loses its question word order — the subject and verb go back to normal statement order, and the question mark disappears (unless the whole sentence is itself a question). So 'Where does she live?' becomes 'I don't know where she lives' (not 'where does she live'), and 'What time is it?' becomes 'Can you tell me what time it is?' (not 'what time is it'). This shift trips up even advanced learners because it feels counterintuitive to remove the inverted word order from something that started life as a question.", "illustration": "📦 NOUN CLAUSE = A WHOLE CLAUSE ACTING AS ONE NOUN\n[That she is right] surprised everyone.     ← noun clause as SUBJECT\nI believe [that she is right].              ← noun clause as OBJECT\nI don't know [what he wants].               ← question word, but...\n\n⚠️ NO QUESTION WORD ORDER INSIDE!\n\"Where does she live?\"  →  \"I don't know where she lives.\"  (NOT \"where does she live\")\n\"What time is it?\"      →  \"Can you tell me what time it is?\"  (NOT \"what time is it\")", "steps": ["Find the clause that is acting as the subject, object, or complement of the main verb.", "Check that it starts with a noun-clause marker: that, what, whether, if, why, how, who, etc.", "Use normal statement word order inside the noun clause, even if it looks like a question (I don't know what he wants, not I don't know what does he want).", "'That' can often be omitted when the noun clause is the object (I think (that) she's right).", "Use 'whether' (not 'if') when the noun clause follows a preposition or starts a sentence (The question of whether we should go...)."], "rule": "A noun clause is a dependent clause that acts as a noun — as the subject, object or complement of a sentence. Noun clauses commonly begin with that, whether, if, or a wh-word such as what, why, how or where, depending on the type of information being expressed.", "structure": "Main verb + noun clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "that-clauses", "definition": "Report a fact, belief or statement, often after verbs like know, think, believe or say.", "example": "\"I know that she is honest.\""}, {"term": "whether / if-clauses", "definition": "Report a yes/no question or uncertainty.", "example": "\"We do not know whether they will agree.\""}, {"term": "wh-clauses", "definition": "Report a specific piece of information using a wh-word, and can act as subject or object.", "example": "\"What he said surprised me.\" (subject) / \"I don't understand why she left.\" (object)"}, {"term": "Noun clause as subject", "definition": "An entire clause can occupy the subject position of a sentence.", "example": "\"What we need is more time.\""}], "examples": ["I know that she is honest.", "What he said surprised me.", "We do not know whether they will agree."], "mistakes": ["Using question word order inside the noun clause: 'I don't know where is she' should be 'I don't know where she is'.", "Dropping whether/if in reported yes/no situations: 'I don't know she will come' should be 'I don't know whether/if she will come'.", "Confusing a noun clause with a relative clause — a noun clause replaces an entire noun phrase, while a relative clause describes a specific noun already mentioned."], "tip": "Test it: can you replace the whole clause with 'it' or 'something' and the sentence still makes sense? If so, it's a noun clause.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence contains a noun clause?", "options": ["I know that she is honest.", "She is honest and kind.", "The honest student smiled.", "She smiled honestly."], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Complete: 'We don't know ___ he will come.'", "options": ["whether", "because", "although", "than"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g25", "day": 25, "level": "B2", "title": "Adverb Clauses", "summary": "Use clauses to express time, reason, contrast and condition.", "plainExplanation": "Adverb clauses are dependent clauses that behave like a single adverb, adding detail about when, why, how, where, or under what condition the main action happens — and they're introduced by subordinating conjunctions that signal exactly which of those relationships is at play. Time: when, while, before, after, until, as soon as ('I'll call you when I arrive'). Reason/cause: because, since, as ('She left early because she felt ill'). Contrast/concession: although, even though, while, whereas ('Although it was expensive, we bought it'). Condition: if, unless, provided that ('You can stay unless you're too tired'). Purpose: so that, in order that ('She saved money so that she could travel'). Just like other dependent clauses, punctuation depends on position: a comma follows the adverb clause when it comes first in the sentence, but no comma is needed when the main clause comes first. Using a rich variety of these connectors — rather than relying on 'and', 'but', and 'so' over and over — is one of the clearest signs of more sophisticated, fluent writing, because it lets you show precisely how your ideas relate to each other instead of just placing them side by side.", "illustration": "🕸️ ADVERB CLAUSES — SIGNAL THE RELATIONSHIP\nTIME       → when · while · before · after · until · as soon as\nREASON     → because · since · as\nCONTRAST   → although · even though · while · whereas\nCONDITION  → if · unless · provided that\nPURPOSE    → so that · in order that\n\n[Although it was expensive]₁, [we bought it]₂.   ← comma when clause comes FIRST\n[We bought it]₂ [although it was expensive]₁.    ← no comma when it comes LAST", "steps": ["Identify what kind of extra information you want to add: time, reason, condition, contrast, or purpose.", "Choose the matching conjunction: when/while/before/after (time), because/since/as (reason), if/unless (condition), although/whereas (contrast), so that/in order that (purpose).", "Place the adverb clause before or after the main clause, adding a comma only if it comes first.", "Make sure the adverb clause has its own subject and verb, but still depends on the main clause to be complete.", "Vary conjunctions to avoid overusing 'because' or 'when' — precise choice improves clarity."], "rule": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies the main clause, functioning like an adverb — showing time, reason, contrast, purpose or condition. It begins with a subordinating conjunction and can come before or after the main clause; when it comes first, a comma usually separates it from the main clause.", "structure": "Subordinator + subject + verb, main clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "Time — when / while / before / after", "definition": "Shows the time relationship between the two actions.", "example": "\"When the lesson ends, I will practise.\""}, {"term": "Reason — because / since / as", "definition": "Explains why the action in the main clause happens.", "example": "\"Because I was tired, I went home.\""}, {"term": "Contrast — although / even though", "definition": "Shows an unexpected relationship between the two clauses.", "example": "\"Although it was difficult, she continued.\""}, {"term": "Condition — if / unless", "definition": "Shows a condition that must be true for the main clause to happen.", "example": "\"If it rains, we will stay inside.\""}], "examples": ["When the lesson ends, I will practise.", "Because I was tired, I went home.", "Although it was difficult, she continued."], "mistakes": ["Using future tense inside a time clause about the future: 'When I will arrive, I call you' should be 'When I arrive, I will call you'.", "Missing the comma when the adverb clause comes first: 'Because I was tired I went home' should be 'Because I was tired, I went home'.", "Confusing while (time) with although (contrast) when the intended meaning is actually contrast."], "tip": "The subordinator tells you the job of the clause — time, reason, contrast or condition — so choose it based on the exact relationship you want to express.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which connector introduces a reason?", "options": ["because", "although", "while", "unless"], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["When I arrive, I will call you.", "When I will arrive, I call you.", "When I arriving, I will call you.", "When arrive I, I will call you."], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g26", "day": 26, "level": "C1", "title": "Inversion for Emphasis", "summary": "Use controlled inversion in formal and emphatic structures.", "plainExplanation": "Inversion means swapping the normal subject-verb order to add emphasis, drama, or formality — instead of 'I have never seen such beauty', an inverted version might say 'Never have I seen such beauty', which sounds noticeably more literary and forceful. This happens after certain negative or restrictive adverbials placed at the start of a sentence: never, rarely, seldom, hardly, no sooner, not only, little — when one of these leads the sentence, the auxiliary verb must jump in front of the subject, mimicking question word order. 'Not only did she finish first, but she also broke the record' (compare the plain version: 'She not only finished first, but she also broke the record'). 'No sooner had he arrived than the phone rang' (compare: 'He had no sooner arrived than the phone rang'). If there's no existing auxiliary verb in the sentence, you must insert do/does/did to carry the inversion, just as you would in a question: 'Rarely do we see such dedication' (not 'Rarely we see'). This structure is a hallmark of formal, literary, and rhetorical English — it's grammatically optional but stylistically powerful, so it appears far more in writing and speeches than in everyday casual conversation.", "illustration": "🔀 INVERSION — SWAP FOR EMPHASIS (mimics question order)\nNORMAL:    \"I have never seen such beauty.\"\nINVERTED:  \"Never have I seen such beauty.\"       ← auxiliary jumps before subject\n\nTRIGGERS (negative/restrictive adverbials at the start):\nnever · rarely · seldom · hardly · no sooner · not only · little\n\n\"Not only did she finish first, but she also broke the record.\"\n\"No sooner had he arrived than the phone rang.\"\nNo auxiliary already there? → insert do/does/did:  \"Rarely do we see such dedication.\"", "steps": ["Identify the negative or limiting adverbial you want to emphasise by moving it to the front (never, rarely, seldom, not only, little).", "Move that word or phrase to the start of the sentence.", "Invert the subject and auxiliary verb, as in a question (Never have I seen such a mess).", "Add an auxiliary (do/does/did) if the main clause has no auxiliary verb already (Rarely does she complain).", "Reserve inversion for formal or emphatic writing/speech — it can sound unnatural in everyday casual conversation."], "rule": "Inversion reverses the normal subject-verb order (auxiliary + subject + verb, instead of subject + verb) to create emphasis in formal or literary English. It is triggered by putting a negative or limiting expression — such as never, rarely, only then, not only, or seldom — at the very beginning of the sentence.", "structure": "Negative/limiting expression + auxiliary + subject + verb", "breakdown": [{"term": "never / rarely + auxiliary inversion", "definition": "When these negative frequency words open the sentence, the auxiliary comes before the subject.", "example": "\"Never have I seen such a result.\" / \"Rarely does he make this mistake.\""}, {"term": "only then / only after", "definition": "Limiting time expressions beginning with only also trigger inversion in the following clause.", "example": "\"Only then did she understand the problem.\""}, {"term": "not only... but also", "definition": "When not only opens a sentence, the first clause is inverted; but also introduces the second idea normally.", "example": "\"Not only did he arrive late, but he also forgot the documents.\""}, {"term": "Other negative adverbials", "definition": "Words like seldom, little, no sooner and under no circumstances behave the same way.", "example": "\"Under no circumstances should you share your password.\""}], "examples": ["Never have I seen such a result.", "Rarely does he make this mistake.", "Only then did she understand the problem."], "mistakes": ["Forgetting to invert after the trigger word: 'Never I have seen this' should be 'Never have I seen this'.", "Adding an extra auxiliary: 'Rarely did he does this' should be 'Rarely does he do this'.", "Using inversion in ordinary, informal sentences where it sounds unnaturally formal or stilted."], "tip": "If the sentence starts with a negative/limiting word like never, rarely or only then, swap the subject and auxiliary that would normally follow.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct emphatic sentence.", "options": ["Never I have seen this.", "Never have I seen this.", "Never seen I have this.", "Never did I have seen this."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which structure follows 'Rarely' in formal inversion?", "options": ["does he", "he does", "he do", "did he does"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g27", "day": 27, "level": "C1", "title": "Participle Clauses", "summary": "Compress related information into concise academic sentences.", "plainExplanation": "Participle clauses use an -ing form (present participle) or an -ed/-en form (past participle) to compress a full clause into a shorter, more elegant phrase, which is a hallmark of sophisticated written English. A present participle clause typically replaces a clause where the subject is actively doing something: 'Feeling tired, she went to bed early' compresses 'Because she felt tired, she went to bed early' — the -ing form implies the reason without spelling out 'because'. A past participle clause typically replaces a clause with a passive meaning: 'Written in haste, the email contained several errors' compresses 'Because the email was written in haste, it contained several errors' — the -ed form implies the passive 'was written' without spelling it out. A critical rule to avoid a classic and often comic error, the 'dangling participle': the implied subject of the participle clause must be the same as the subject of the main clause that follows it. 'Walking to the station, the rain started' is wrong and unintentionally funny, because it grammatically implies the rain was walking to the station; the correct version needs a human subject immediately after the comma: 'Walking to the station, I got caught in the rain.'", "illustration": "✂️ PARTICIPLE CLAUSES — COMPRESS A CLAUSE INTO A PHRASE\n-ING (active meaning):  \"Feeling tired, she went to bed early.\"\n   = \"Because she felt tired, she went to bed early.\"\n-ED (passive meaning):  \"Written in haste, the email contained errors.\"\n   = \"Because the email was written in haste, it contained errors.\"\n\n⚠️ DANGLING PARTICIPLE — the implied subject MUST match the main clause subject!\n❌ \"Walking to the station, the rain started.\"     (rain can't walk!)\n✅ \"Walking to the station, I got caught in the rain.\"", "steps": ["Find two clauses that share the same subject and could be combined.", "Use an -ing participle clause for an active, ongoing action (Walking home, she saw a fox = As she walked home...).", "Use an -ed/-en participle clause for a passive meaning (Written in 1990, the book became a classic = The book, which was written...).", "Make sure the implied subject of the participle clause is the same as the subject of the main clause, to avoid a 'dangling participle'.", "Use participle clauses to replace clauses of time, reason, or result for a more concise, formal style."], "rule": "Participle clauses compress information into shorter, more sophisticated sentences, common in academic and formal writing. A present participle (-ing) clause often expresses an action happening at the same time as, or closely related to, the main clause. A past participle (-ed/-en) clause can create a reduced passive meaning when the relationship to the main clause is clear. Care is needed to avoid a 'dangling' participle that appears to describe the wrong subject.", "structure": "V-ing/V-ed phrase + main clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "Present participle — simultaneous/related action", "definition": "Describes an action happening at the same time as, or just before, the main clause's action.", "example": "\"Working carefully, he avoided errors.\""}, {"term": "having + past participle — completed before", "definition": "Shows that the participle clause's action was completed before the main clause's action.", "example": "\"Having finished the essay, she submitted it.\""}, {"term": "Reduced passive (past participle)", "definition": "A past participle clause can replace a longer passive clause when the subject is clear.", "example": "\"Based on the evidence, the report recommends change.\" (= Because it is based on...)"}, {"term": "Avoiding dangling participles", "definition": "The implied subject of the participle clause must match the subject of the main clause, or the sentence becomes illogical.", "example": "Incorrect: \"Walking to school, the rain started.\" Correct: \"Walking to school, I got caught in the rain.\""}], "examples": ["Having finished the essay, she submitted it.", "Based on the evidence, the report recommends change.", "Working carefully, he avoided errors."], "mistakes": ["Dangling participles: 'Having finished the essay, the printer broke' illogically implies the printer finished the essay — it should refer to the actual person.", "Using the wrong participle form: 'Finished having the essay, she submitted it' should be 'Having finished the essay, she submitted it'.", "Overusing participle clauses in informal writing, where a full clause with a conjunction is often clearer."], "tip": "Check that the subject of the main clause is also the logical 'doer' of the participle clause — otherwise, rewrite with a full clause.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct reduced form for 'After she finished the essay, she submitted it.'", "options": ["Having finished the essay, she submitted it.", "Having finish the essay, she submitted it.", "Finished having the essay, she submitted it.", "She having finished the essay submitted it."], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Which participle commonly creates a reduced passive phrase?", "options": ["based", "bases", "basingly", "base"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g28", "day": 28, "level": "C1", "title": "Cleft Sentences and Focus", "summary": "Highlight a specific part of a sentence for emphasis.", "plainExplanation": "Cleft sentences deliberately split a simple sentence into two parts in order to shine a spotlight on one specific piece of information — the word 'cleft' literally means 'split'. Instead of the plain 'Tom broke the window', an it-cleft reorganises it to emphasise a particular element: 'It was Tom who broke the window' (emphasising that it was specifically Tom, not someone else) or 'It was the window that Tom broke' (emphasising that it was specifically the window, not something else he broke). A wh-cleft (also called a pseudo-cleft) uses a question word, typically 'what', to front the focused information at the end of the sentence instead: 'What Tom broke was the window' or 'What surprised everyone was her calm reaction.' Both cleft structures exist purely to control emphasis and information flow — they don't add new factual content, but they let a writer or speaker direct the listener's attention exactly where they want it, which is especially valuable in spoken English (for correcting a misunderstanding: 'No, it was Sarah who called, not Maria') and in writing that needs to build toward a specific point for rhetorical effect.", "illustration": "🔦 CLEFT SENTENCES — SPLIT TO SPOTLIGHT ONE PART\nPLAIN:     \"Tom broke the window.\"\n\nIT-CLEFT (spotlight the doer):    \"It was Tom who broke the window.\"\nIT-CLEFT (spotlight the object):  \"It was the window that Tom broke.\"\nWH-CLEFT (spotlight at the end):  \"What Tom broke was the window.\"\n                                   \"What surprised everyone was her calm reaction.\"\n\n💡 Same facts, different emphasis — you choose where the spotlight lands.", "steps": ["Decide exactly which piece of information you want to highlight.", "For an it-cleft, use 'It is/was + [the emphasised part] + that/who + [the rest of the sentence]' (It was Maria who found the solution).", "For a wh-cleft, use 'What + [rest of sentence] + is/was + [the emphasised part]' (What we need is more time).", "Keep the verb tense in the cleft consistent with the original sentence's meaning.", "Use clefts sparingly for genuine emphasis — overusing them makes writing feel repetitive or overly dramatic."], "rule": "Cleft sentences reorganize normal word order to place special emphasis on one part of the sentence, which is useful for highlighting new or contrasting information in formal writing and speech. The two most common patterns are the it-cleft (It is/was + focus + that/who...) and the wh-cleft (What.../The reason.../All... + is + focus).", "structure": "It is/was + focus + that/who + clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "it-cleft", "definition": "Places the focused information right after it is/was, followed by that or who.", "example": "\"It was the final paragraph that impressed the examiner.\""}, {"term": "wh-cleft (what-clause)", "definition": "Uses a what-clause as the subject to highlight what is needed, wanted or done.", "example": "\"What I need is more practice.\""}, {"term": "all-cleft", "definition": "Uses all + clause to emphasise that only one thing matters.", "example": "\"All I want is a quiet place to study.\""}, {"term": "the reason why-cleft", "definition": "Highlights a cause or explanation as the focus of the sentence.", "example": "\"The reason why she succeeded was hard work.\""}], "examples": ["It was the final paragraph that impressed the examiner.", "What I need is more practice.", "It is accuracy that matters most here."], "mistakes": ["Incorrect word order in the it-cleft: 'It accuracy was that mattered' should be 'It was accuracy that mattered'.", "Missing is in the wh-cleft: 'What I need more practice' should be 'What I need is more practice'.", "Overusing cleft sentences, which can sound repetitive if every sentence in a paragraph starts with It is/What..."], "tip": "Decide exactly which single piece of information you want to spotlight, then build the cleft frame (It is/was..., or What... is...) around it.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct cleft structure.", "options": ["It was accuracy that mattered.", "It accuracy was that mattered.", "Accuracy it was mattered.", "It was that accuracy mattered."], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Which phrase can focus on a needed thing?", "options": ["What I need is...", "What need I is...", "I what need is...", "Need what I is..."], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g29", "day": 29, "level": "C1", "title": "Advanced Punctuation and Sentence Boundaries", "summary": "Control commas, semicolons and sentence boundaries in formal writing.", "plainExplanation": "Advanced punctuation marks — the semicolon, colon, and em dash — each do a distinct job in signalling relationships between ideas at the sentence-boundary level, and knowing when to use each one (rather than defaulting to a comma or full stop every time) is a hallmark of confident, sophisticated writing. The semicolon (;) joins two closely related independent clauses that could each stand alone as a full sentence, without needing a conjunction: 'The weather was perfect; we decided to go hiking.' It signals a tighter logical connection than a full stop would, while still respecting that both halves are complete thoughts. The colon (:) introduces something that explains, elaborates on, or lists items related to what came before it — the part before the colon must be a complete independent clause, and the colon essentially says 'here's what I mean' or 'here's the list': 'She had one goal: to win.' The em dash (—) creates a stronger, more dramatic interruption or emphasis than a comma, often setting off an explanatory or surprising addition: 'The results were clear — everyone had passed.' A very common error is the comma splice — using a comma alone to join two independent clauses ('The weather was perfect, we decided to go hiking') where a semicolon, a period, or a coordinating conjunction is actually required.", "illustration": "🎯 SEMICOLON vs COLON vs EM DASH\nSEMICOLON (;) → joins two related COMPLETE sentences, no conjunction needed\n  \"The weather was perfect; we decided to go hiking.\"\nCOLON (:)     → introduces an explanation or list (needs a complete clause before it)\n  \"She had one goal: to win.\"\nEM DASH (—)   → dramatic interruption or emphasis\n  \"The results were clear — everyone had passed.\"\n\n⚠️ COMMA SPLICE (error!): \"The weather was perfect, we decided to go hiking.\"\n   ← needs a semicolon, period, or conjunction instead of just a comma", "steps": ["Use a semicolon to join two closely related independent clauses without a conjunction (The exam was hard; everyone struggled).", "Use a colon to introduce a list, explanation, or elaboration after a complete independent clause (She had one goal: to pass).", "Use an em dash for an abrupt break or emphatic aside — sparingly, in more informal or emphatic writing.", "Avoid comma splices — never join two independent clauses with only a comma; use a semicolon, a conjunction, or a full stop instead.", "Check every sentence boundary: could it stand alone as a complete sentence? If two complete ideas are joined only by a comma, fix the punctuation."], "rule": "Advanced punctuation controls how ideas are grouped and separated in formal writing. A semicolon can join two closely related independent clauses without a conjunction. Commas separate introductory elements and non-essential (extra) information but must not join two full independent clauses on their own — doing so creates a comma splice. A colon introduces an explanation, list or elaboration of what came before it.", "structure": "Independent clause; independent clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "Semicolon joining independent clauses", "definition": "Connects two complete, closely related sentences without a conjunction like and or but.", "example": "\"The evidence was limited; the conclusion remained cautious.\""}, {"term": "Avoiding comma splices", "definition": "A comma alone cannot join two independent clauses — use a semicolon, a conjunction, or split into two sentences.", "example": "Incorrect: \"The evidence was limited, the conclusion was cautious.\" Correct: \"...limited; the conclusion...\" or \"...limited, so the conclusion...\""}, {"term": "Commas for non-essential information", "definition": "Extra information that could be removed without changing the core meaning is set off with commas.", "example": "\"The report, which was published in June, received attention.\""}, {"term": "Colon for explanation or elaboration", "definition": "Introduces a list, explanation or example that directly explains the preceding clause.", "example": "\"The result was clear: accuracy had improved significantly.\""}], "examples": ["The evidence was limited; the conclusion remained cautious.", "However, the results were consistent.", "The report, which was published in June, received attention."], "mistakes": ["Joining two full sentences with only a comma: 'The results were strong, the method was sound.' should use a semicolon or conjunction.", "Using a semicolon where a colon is needed: a semicolon should join two independent clauses, not introduce a list or explanation.", "Adding unnecessary commas around essential (defining) information that the sentence needs to make sense."], "tip": "Before using a semicolon, check that both sides could stand alone as full sentences — that confirms a semicolon is the right choice.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence correctly joins two independent clauses?", "options": ["The evidence was limited, the conclusion was cautious.", "The evidence was limited; the conclusion was cautious.", "The evidence was limited the conclusion was cautious.", "The evidence, was limited; the conclusion was cautious."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which punctuation can join two closely related independent clauses?", "options": ["semicolon", "apostrophe", "hyphen only", "quotation mark"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g30", "day": 30, "level": "C1", "title": "Grammar for Academic Writing", "summary": "Combine accurate grammar with formal control, cohesion and sentence variety.", "plainExplanation": "Academic writing has its own grammatical register, favouring precision, objectivity, and formality over the casual, personal tone common in everyday speech. Several grammatical habits distinguish it: preferring the passive voice or impersonal constructions to keep the focus on findings rather than the researcher ('The data were analysed' rather than 'I analysed the data'), avoiding contractions entirely (write 'do not' instead of 'don't', 'cannot' instead of 'can't'), using nominalisation to create more abstract, analytical noun phrases ('the implementation of the policy' rather than 'when they implemented the policy'), and hedging claims appropriately with cautious modal verbs and phrases ('the results suggest' rather than the overconfident 'the results prove') to reflect the genuine, often partial certainty that research evidence provides. Academic writing also tends to avoid first-person pronouns in many disciplines (though this varies — some fields now accept 'I argue that...'), favours precise, formal vocabulary over colloquial phrasing, and uses longer, more complex sentences built through subordination and coordination to show explicit logical relationships between ideas, rather than a string of short, simple sentences. Mastering this register is less about learning new grammar rules from scratch and more about consistently applying the more formal option at every choice point — passive over active, full form over contraction, nominalisation over plain verb, hedge over blunt claim — throughout an entire piece of writing.", "illustration": "🎓 ACADEMIC REGISTER — CONSISTENTLY CHOOSE THE FORMAL OPTION\nCASUAL                          →  ACADEMIC\n\"I analysed the data.\"          →  \"The data were analysed.\"        (passive)\n\"don't / can't\"                 →  \"do not / cannot\"                (no contractions)\n\"when they implemented it\"      →  \"the implementation of the policy\" (nominalisation)\n\"the results prove...\"          →  \"the results suggest...\"          (hedged claim)\n\n💡 Not new rules — just consistently picking the more formal option every time.", "steps": ["Replace contractions with full forms (don't → do not, it's → it is).", "Use the passive voice when the process or result matters more than who performed it (The data was analysed...).", "Turn action verbs into abstract nouns where it suits formal tone (analyse → analysis, decide → decision).", "Hedge claims with cautious language instead of blunt assertions (may suggest, appears to indicate, tends to).", "Avoid first-person opinion words like 'I think' in favour of evidence-based, impersonal phrasing (The findings suggest...)."], "rule": "Strong academic writing combines grammatical accuracy with purposeful complexity: precise tense choice, controlled passive voice for objectivity, logical subordination to connect ideas, cautious modal language (hedging) to avoid overstatement, and a variety of sentence structures to maintain reader interest. The goal is not complexity for its own sake, but clarity supported by careful control of grammar.", "structure": "Claim + explanation + evidence/example + controlled complex structure", "breakdown": [{"term": "Hedging language", "definition": "Cautious modals and phrases (may, might, suggest, appears to) avoid overstating claims not fully supported by evidence.", "example": "\"This may suggest that the method is effective.\""}, {"term": "Nominalisation", "definition": "Turning verbs/adjectives into nouns creates a more formal, compact academic style.", "example": "\"The team improved the method\" → \"The improvement of the method...\""}, {"term": "Passive voice for objectivity", "definition": "Used to focus on findings or processes rather than the researcher, common in formal academic reports.", "example": "\"The data were analysed using standard methods.\""}, {"term": "Cohesive devices", "definition": "Linking words and phrases (however, therefore, in contrast) guide the reader logically between ideas.", "example": "\"Although the sample was limited, the results were broadly consistent.\""}, {"term": "Sentence variety", "definition": "Mixing simple, compound and complex sentences avoids monotony and controls emphasis.", "example": "Combine a short claim sentence with a longer, evidence-supported complex sentence."}], "examples": ["The findings suggest that targeted practice can improve accuracy.", "Although the sample was limited, the results were broadly consistent.", "It is therefore important to interpret the evidence cautiously."], "mistakes": ["Overstating claims without hedging: 'This proves everything' should be softened to 'This may suggest...' or 'This indicates...'.", "Overusing passive voice until the writing becomes vague about who did what and why it matters.", "Writing only long, complex sentences without variety, which can make academic writing harder to follow rather than more sophisticated."], "tip": "After drafting, check each strong claim: is it fully supported by the evidence, or does it need a hedge like may, suggests, or appears to?", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence shows cautious academic language?", "options": ["This proves everything.", "This may suggest that the method is effective.", "This definitely proves all cases.", "This always proves the theory."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which quality is most important in advanced academic grammar?", "options": ["Complexity at any cost", "Accuracy and purposeful control", "Long sentences only", "Rare vocabulary in every sentence"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g31", "day": 31, "level": "B1", "title": "Quantifiers", "summary": "Choose the right quantifier for countable, uncountable and mixed nouns.", "plainExplanation": "Quantifiers are words that tell you 'how much' or 'how many' of something, and different quantifiers pair with countable nouns, uncountable nouns, or both. For countable plural nouns, use 'many', 'a few', 'several', 'few' ('many books', 'a few friends'). For uncountable nouns, use 'much', 'a little', 'little' ('much money', 'a little time'). For either type, 'some', 'any', 'a lot of', and 'lots of' all work fine ('some water' / 'some apples'; 'any advice' / 'any questions'). A subtle but important distinction is between 'a few'/'a little' versus plain 'few'/'little' — dropping the 'a' flips the meaning from positive to negative. 'A few' and 'a little' mean 'a small amount, but enough' (a mildly positive, reassuring tone): 'I have a few friends here' (enough to feel supported). Plain 'few' and 'little' mean 'almost none, not enough' (a negative, often disappointed tone): 'I have few friends here' (a shortage, feeling isolated). This tiny word 'a' completely reverses the emotional colour of the sentence, so it's worth paying close attention to.", "illustration": "📊 QUANTIFIERS — MATCH THE NOUN TYPE\nCOUNTABLE PLURAL  → many · a few · several · few\nUNCOUNTABLE        → much · a little · little\nEITHER TYPE         → some · any · a lot of · lots of\n\n⚠️ THE HIDDEN FLIP: 'a' changes positive ↔ negative!\nA FEW / A LITTLE  = small amount, but ENOUGH (positive)   \"I have a few friends.\" 🙂\nFEW / LITTLE      = almost none, NOT ENOUGH (negative)    \"I have few friends.\" 🙁", "steps": ["Check if the noun is countable (many books) or uncountable (much water).", "Use 'some' in affirmative sentences and offers/requests, and 'any' in negatives and most questions.", "Use 'many/few' with countable plural nouns and 'much/little' with uncountable nouns.", "Use 'a few' and 'a little' for a small but sufficient amount; use plain 'few' and 'little' for an almost negative, insufficient amount.", "Use 'a lot of' or 'lots of' informally with both countable and uncountable nouns in affirmative sentences."], "rule": "Quantifiers indicate approximate or exact amount. 'Many' and 'few' pair with countable plural nouns (many books, few chances); 'much' and 'little' pair with uncountable nouns (much time, little money). 'Some', 'any' and 'a lot of' work with both types, with 'any' typically used in questions and negatives.", "structure": "many/few + plural noun | much/little + uncountable noun | some/any/a lot of + either", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. many / few — countable plural", "definition": "Many and few pair with plural countable nouns. Many asks about a large number; few (without 'a') suggests almost none, often with a slightly negative tone.", "example": "\"Many students passed, but few finished early.\""}, {"term": "2. much / little — uncountable", "definition": "Much and little pair with uncountable nouns in the same way many/few pair with countables. Little (without 'a') also leans negative — 'barely enough'.", "example": "\"There is much interest but little funding.\""}, {"term": "3. a few / a little — a small positive amount", "definition": "Adding 'a' before few/little flips the tone to positive: a small but sufficient amount, rather than 'almost none'.", "example": "\"I have a few minutes — let's talk.\""}, {"term": "4. some / any — both types, positive/negative", "definition": "Some and any work with both countable and uncountable nouns. Some is typical in positive statements and offers; any is typical in questions and negatives.", "example": "\"Do you have any suggestions? Yes, I have some.\""}], "examples": ["There are many opportunities here.", "We have little information about the delay.", "Do you have any suggestions?"], "mistakes": ["Using 'much' with a countable plural noun, e.g. 'much people' instead of 'many people'.", "Confusing 'a few' (some, a small positive amount) with 'few' (almost none, often negative in tone).", "Using 'much' outside questions/negatives in informal speech: 'much' is common in questions and negatives, but 'a lot of' usually sounds more natural in positive statements."], "tip": "Ask first: is the noun countable or uncountable? Then choose many/few for countable, much/little for uncountable.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["There are much books on the shelf.", "There are many books on the shelf.", "There is many books on the shelf.", "There are many book on the shelf."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Complete: 'I have very ___ money with me.'", "options": ["many", "few", "little", "some"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g32", "day": 32, "level": "B1", "title": "Question Tags", "summary": "Add short tags to confirm information or invite agreement.", "plainExplanation": "A question tag is a mini-question tacked onto the end of a statement, usually to confirm information or invite agreement — like saying 'right?' or 'isn't that so?' in many other languages, but English has a specific grammatical formula. The core rule is simple: if the main statement is positive, the tag is negative; if the statement is negative, the tag is positive. This is called the 'opposite polarity' rule. The tag also copies the same auxiliary verb (or modal) used in the main sentence, and matches the same subject as a pronoun: 'She is coming, isn't she?' (positive statement → negative tag, copies 'is'), 'They haven't left, have they?' (negative statement → positive tag, copies 'have'). If the main sentence has no obvious auxiliary verb (as in present simple or past simple without an auxiliary), the tag borrows do/does/did: 'You like coffee, don't you?' 'She left early, didn't she?' A special, commonly memorised exception: 'I am' takes the irregular tag 'aren't I?' rather than the logical but non-existent 'amn't I?' — 'I'm right, aren't I?'", "illustration": "🔄 QUESTION TAGS — OPPOSITE POLARITY RULE\nPOSITIVE statement → NEGATIVE tag       \"She is coming, isn't she?\"\nNEGATIVE statement → POSITIVE tag       \"They haven't left, have they?\"\n\nNo auxiliary in the sentence? → borrow do/does/did\n\"You like coffee, don't you?\"     \"She left early, didn't she?\"\n\n⚠️ SPECIAL CASE: \"I am\" → \"aren't I?\"   (never \"amn't I?\")", "steps": ["Identify the auxiliary or modal verb in the main statement (is, have, can, will) — if there isn't one, use do/does/did.", "Reverse the polarity: positive statement gets a negative tag, negative statement gets a positive tag.", "Match the tag's subject to a pronoun that refers back to the main subject (Maria is late, isn't she?).", "Keep the tense of the tag consistent with the main verb (She worked hard, didn't she?).", "Use rising intonation for a genuine question, and falling intonation when you're fairly sure and just confirming."], "rule": "A question tag is a short question added to the end of a statement, usually to confirm information or invite agreement. It repeats the auxiliary verb of the main clause and reverses its polarity: a positive statement gets a negative tag, and a negative statement gets a positive tag. If the main clause has no auxiliary (like present simple or past simple), do/does/did is used in the tag.", "structure": "Positive statement, negative auxiliary + pronoun? | Negative statement, positive auxiliary + pronoun?", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Reversing polarity", "definition": "A positive statement takes a negative tag, and a negative statement takes a positive tag — the tag checks or confirms what was just said.", "example": "\"She's from Spain, isn't she?\""}, {"term": "2. Matching the auxiliary", "definition": "The tag repeats whichever auxiliary is already in the main clause (is, has, can, will) rather than introducing a new one.", "example": "\"They can swim, can't they?\""}, {"term": "3. do/does/did when there is no auxiliary", "definition": "If the main clause is a plain present or past simple verb with no helping verb, the tag borrows do/does/did to carry the reversal.", "example": "\"He plays tennis, doesn't he?\""}, {"term": "4. Irregular tag for 'I am'", "definition": "The expected tag for 'I am' would be 'am I not', which sounds stiff, so English uses the irregular form 'aren't I' instead.", "example": "\"I'm right, aren't I?\""}], "examples": ["It's a lovely day, isn't it?", "You don't smoke, do you?", "They went home early, didn't they?"], "mistakes": ["Repeating the same polarity instead of reversing it, e.g. 'You're coming, are you?' when agreement is intended (this changes the meaning to a genuine question).", "Using the wrong auxiliary, e.g. 'You can swim, do you?' instead of 'can't you'.", "Forgetting that 'nobody', 'no one' and 'nothing' count as negative, so they take a positive tag: 'Nobody called, did they?'"], "tip": "Positive statement → negative tag; negative statement → positive tag. The tag always mirrors the main clause's auxiliary.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct tag: 'He can swim, ___?'", "options": ["can he", "can't he", "does he", "doesn't he"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct tag: 'They aren't coming, ___?'", "options": ["are they", "aren't they", "do they", "don't they"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g33", "day": 33, "level": "B1", "title": "Phrasal Verbs", "summary": "Use common two- and three-word verbs accurately in context.", "plainExplanation": "Phrasal verbs combine a verb with one or two small particles (prepositions or adverbs like up, off, out, into) to create a new meaning that's often completely different from the individual words — you genuinely can't guess the meaning just by knowing 'give' and 'up' separately, which is why phrasal verbs have to be learned as complete vocabulary chunks. 'Give up' means to quit or stop trying, 'look after' means to take care of, 'run into' means to meet by chance, 'put off' means to postpone. A key grammar point is whether a phrasal verb is 'separable' or 'inseparable' when it takes an object. Separable phrasal verbs allow the object to sit either after the whole phrase or in between the verb and particle: 'turn off the light' or 'turn the light off' both work — but if the object is a pronoun (it, them, him, her), it must go in the middle: 'turn it off', never 'turn off it'. Inseparable phrasal verbs never allow anything to be inserted between verb and particle, regardless of whether the object is a noun or pronoun: you can only say 'look after the baby' or 'look after her', never 'look the baby after' or 'look her after'.", "illustration": "🧩 PHRASAL VERBS — MEANING ≠ SUM OF THE WORDS\ngive up = quit   |   look after = take care of   |   run into = meet by chance\n\nSEPARABLE (object can split the phrase, PRONOUN MUST split it):\n  \"turn off the light\"  OR  \"turn the light off\"   ✅ both work\n  \"turn it off\"  ✅        \"turn off it\"  ❌ (pronoun MUST go in the middle)\n\nINSEPARABLE (never split, noun or pronoun):\n  \"look after the baby\" / \"look after her\"   ✅\n  \"look the baby after\" / \"look her after\"   ❌ never split", "steps": ["Learn phrasal verbs as whole units, not word-by-word translations (give up ≠ 'give' + 'up' literally).", "Check if the phrasal verb is separable (turn the light off / turn off the light) or inseparable (look after the baby, not look the baby after).", "With separable phrasal verbs, a pronoun object must go between the verb and particle (turn it off, not turn off it).", "Note that some phrasal verbs are transitive (need an object) and others are intransitive (stand alone): give up smoking vs. she gave up.", "Build a personal list of high-frequency phrasal verbs and their meanings, since there is no shortcut except memorisation and exposure."], "rule": "Phrasal verbs combine a verb with one or two particles (prepositions/adverbs) to create a meaning that is often idiomatic. Separable phrasal verbs allow the object between the verb and particle or after it (turn off the light / turn the light off), though a pronoun object must go in the middle (turn it off, not turn off it). Inseparable phrasal verbs keep the object after the whole phrase (look after the children).", "structure": "verb + particle (+ object) | verb + object + particle (separable only)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Separable phrasal verbs", "definition": "The object can sit between the verb and the particle, or after both — either order is correct with a full noun object.", "example": "\"Turn off the light.\" / \"Turn the light off.\""}, {"term": "2. Pronoun objects with separable verbs", "definition": "When the object is a pronoun (it, him, her, them), it must go between the verb and the particle — it cannot follow the particle.", "example": "\"Turn it off.\" (not 'turn off it')"}, {"term": "3. Inseparable phrasal verbs", "definition": "Some phrasal verbs never split; the object always comes after the whole verb-plus-particle unit, pronoun or not.", "example": "\"She looks after the children.\" / \"She looks after them.\""}, {"term": "4. Three-word phrasal verbs", "definition": "Verbs with two particles (look forward to, put up with) are always inseparable and are often followed by a gerund rather than an infinitive.", "example": "\"I look forward to seeing you.\""}], "examples": ["Could you turn the music down, please?", "She takes after her mother.", "They put off the meeting until next week."], "mistakes": ["Placing a pronoun object after a separable phrasal verb, e.g. 'turn off it' instead of 'turn it off'.", "Trying to separate an inseparable phrasal verb, e.g. 'look the children after' instead of 'look after the children'.", "Using 'to' + base verb after a three-word phrasal verb ending in 'to', e.g. 'look forward to see you' instead of 'look forward to seeing you'."], "tip": "With a pronoun object (it, him, her, them), separable phrasal verbs must split: verb + pronoun + particle.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence with a separable phrasal verb.", "options": ["Turn off it.", "Turn it off.", "Turn off the it.", "Off turn it."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which phrasal verb means 'to meet by chance'?", "options": ["look after", "run into", "turn off", "give up"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g34", "day": 34, "level": "B2", "title": "Mixed Conditionals", "summary": "Combine different time frames to express realistic hypothetical relationships.", "plainExplanation": "Mixed conditionals combine a condition from one time period with a result in a different time period, unlike the standard second and third conditionals, which stay within a single time frame. The most common mix pairs a past condition with a present result: 'If I had studied medicine (unreal past), I would be a doctor now (unreal present)' — the structure is if + past perfect, would + base verb, showing how a past decision affects the present. The reverse mix pairs a present/general condition with a past result: 'If she weren't so shy (unreal, ongoing present trait), she would have applied for the job (unreal past action)' — structure is if + past simple, would have + past participle, showing how an ongoing characteristic explains a past outcome. The key to building these correctly is to think about each half of the sentence separately: figure out what time period the condition belongs to and what time period the result belongs to, then apply the past-perfect-for-past / past-simple-for-present rule to each half independently, rather than trying to force both halves into matching times.", "illustration": "🔀 MIXED CONDITIONALS — TWO DIFFERENT TIME FRAMES\nPAST condition → PRESENT result:\nIf + had + past participle  ,  would + base verb\n\"If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now.\"\n   (unreal past decision)      (unreal present consequence)\n\nPRESENT condition → PAST result:\nIf + past simple  ,  would have + past participle\n\"If she weren't so shy, she would have applied for the job.\"\n   (ongoing present trait)     (unreal past action)", "steps": ["Decide if your condition is in the past and its result continues into the present, or vice versa.", "For a past condition with a present result: if + past perfect, would + base verb (If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now).", "For a present/general condition with a past result: if + past simple, would have + past participle (If she weren't so shy, she would have spoken up).", "Keep the logic consistent — check that the time reference of each clause genuinely matches the meaning you intend.", "Use mixed conditionals sparingly and only when a standard second or third conditional doesn't capture your exact meaning."], "rule": "Mixed conditionals combine two different time frames within one sentence to express a realistic relationship between an unreal condition and its result. The two common patterns are: (1) past condition, present result — If + past perfect, would + base; and (2) present condition, past result — If + past simple (often 'were'), would have + past participle.", "structure": "If + past perfect, would + base (past cause, present result) | If + past simple, would have + past participle (present cause, past result)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Past condition, present result", "definition": "An unreal past action explains a present state: if + had + past participle in the condition, would + base verb in the result.", "example": "\"If I had taken that job, I would be living abroad now.\""}, {"term": "2. Present condition, past result", "definition": "An ongoing present trait or state explains a specific past outcome: if + past simple (often 'were') in the condition, would have + past participle in the result.", "example": "\"If she weren't so shy, she would have spoken up yesterday.\""}, {"term": "3. Identifying the two time frames separately", "definition": "Unlike standard second/third conditionals, the condition and the result do not share one time frame — each clause must be checked on its own.", "example": "\"If he had studied medicine, he would be a doctor now.\" (past condition, present result)"}, {"term": "4. Keeping the tense pairing consistent", "definition": "Once the time frame of each clause is identified, the whole clause must match that frame — mixing tenses within a single clause breaks the pattern.", "example": "\"If they had left earlier, they would be home by now.\" (not 'they would have been')"}], "examples": ["If I had accepted that job offer, I would be living abroad now.", "If he weren't so stubborn, he would have accepted our help last week.", "If she had trained harder, she would be competing at the championship now."], "mistakes": ["Using the same tense pattern as a standard second or third conditional when the time frames of the condition and result are actually different.", "Forgetting to shift 'would' to 'would have' when the result is specifically in the past, not the present.", "Using 'was' instead of 'were' in the hypothetical if-clause of a formal mixed conditional, e.g. 'If she wasn't so shy' instead of 'If she weren't so shy'."], "tip": "Identify the time of the condition and the time of the result separately — they don't have to match, and mixed conditionals exist exactly for when they don't.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence links a past condition to a present result?", "options": ["If I had left earlier, I would have caught the bus.", "If I had left earlier, I would be home now.", "If I leave earlier, I will be home now.", "If I left earlier, I would catch the bus."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct mixed conditional.", "options": ["If she weren't afraid of heights, she would have gone skydiving with us yesterday.", "If she isn't afraid, she would have gone skydiving.", "If she wasn't afraid, she goes skydiving yesterday.", "If she hadn't afraid, she would go skydiving yesterday."], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g35", "day": 35, "level": "B2", "title": "Determiners and Word Order", "summary": "Order determiners, quantifiers and adjectives correctly before a noun.", "plainExplanation": "Determiners are the small words that come before a noun to specify it — articles (a, an, the), possessives (my, your, his), demonstratives (this, that, these, those), and quantifiers (some, many, every, each) all belong to this category, and English is strict about the order they can appear in and how many can stack up. A key rule: you generally can't combine two determiners of the same broad type in front of one noun — you can't say 'the my book' or 'a the house', because 'the' and 'my' are both trying to do the same specifying job. To combine ownership and specificity, English uses a different structure instead: 'that book of mine' or 'a friend of mine', rather than stacking determiners directly. Word order more broadly in English noun phrases follows a fairly fixed sequence: determiner, then any general opinion adjective, then more specific descriptive adjectives (size, age, shape, colour, origin, material), then the noun itself — 'a beautiful old wooden Italian table' rather than a random shuffle of the same words. Breaking this order doesn't just sound odd; it can genuinely make a phrase harder to parse.", "illustration": "📐 DETERMINERS — ONE PER NOUN, IN A FIXED ORDER\n❌ \"the my book\"   ❌ \"a the house\"   ← can't stack two determiners\n✅ \"my book\"   ✅ \"that book of mine\"   ← use 'of mine' to combine ownership + specificity\n\n🎨 ADJECTIVE ORDER (before the noun):\ndeterminer → opinion → size → age → shape → colour → origin → material → NOUN\n\"a  beautiful   old   wooden   Italian   table\"\n   opinion      age   material  origin", "steps": ["Identify the noun and any words specifying it: articles, possessives, demonstratives, or quantifiers.", "Remember only one determiner from the same category can normally go before a noun (not 'the my book', just 'my book').", "Follow the general order: determiner → opinion adjective → size → age → shape → colour → origin → material → noun.", "Use 'of' to combine two determiners when needed (some of my friends, not some my friends).", "Double-check word order in longer noun phrases, since determiner order is one of the most common sources of unnatural-sounding English."], "rule": "English noun phrases follow a fairly fixed order of determiners and adjectives before the noun: determiner → quantity/opinion → size/age/shape → colour/origin/material → noun. Determiners (a, the, this, my) generally cannot combine directly before the same noun; a phrase like 'a beautiful old French chair' follows this predictable sequence.", "structure": "determiner + (opinion) + (size/age/shape) + (colour/origin) + noun", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Determiner first", "definition": "Articles, possessives and demonstratives (a, the, my, this) open the noun phrase and cannot be doubled up before the same noun.", "example": "\"her old car\" (not 'the her old car')"}, {"term": "2. Quantity or opinion next", "definition": "Numbers and quantity words come next, followed by opinion adjectives that express a subjective judgement.", "example": "\"three lovely paintings\""}, {"term": "3. Size, age and shape", "definition": "Objective, measurable qualities like size, age and shape sit closer to the noun than opinion words do.", "example": "\"a lovely old, round table\""}, {"term": "4. Origin and material last", "definition": "Colour, origin and material sit closest to the noun, since they describe what the thing fundamentally is.", "example": "\"a beautiful old French wooden chair\""}], "examples": ["a fascinating new documentary", "these three small wooden boxes", "her elegant black leather bag"], "mistakes": ["Placing adjectives in the wrong category order, e.g. 'a wooden small table' instead of 'a small wooden table'.", "Combining two determiners before one noun, e.g. 'the my car' instead of 'my car' or 'this car of mine'.", "Overloading a noun phrase with too many adjectives at once, which sounds unnatural even when the order is technically correct — two or three is usually the practical limit."], "tip": "When several adjectives appear together, opinion words usually come first, and adjectives closest to the noun describe material or origin.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correctly ordered phrase.", "options": ["a wooden small beautiful table", "a beautiful small wooden table", "a small beautiful wooden table", "wooden a small beautiful table"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["The my book is here.", "My the book is here.", "My book is here.", "The book my is here."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g36", "day": 36, "level": "B2", "title": "Linking Words and Cohesion", "summary": "Connect ideas smoothly using addition, contrast, cause and result devices.", "plainExplanation": "Linking words (also called cohesive devices or connectors) tie sentences and ideas together so writing flows logically instead of reading like a disconnected list of facts. Different linking words signal different logical relationships, and choosing the right category matters: addition (furthermore, moreover, in addition, also) adds a similar point on the same side of an argument; contrast (however, on the other hand, nevertheless, whereas) signals a shift or opposing point; cause and effect (therefore, consequently, as a result, thus) shows one thing led to another; and exemplification (for instance, for example, such as) introduces a specific illustration of a general claim. Punctuation matters here too — most of these connectors, when starting a new sentence, are followed by a comma ('However, the results were disappointing.'), and when joining two independent clauses within one sentence, they typically need a semicolon before them and a comma after: 'The results were promising; however, more research is needed.' Using 'however' with just a comma to join two full sentences ('The results were promising, however, more research is needed') creates a common error called a comma splice, because 'however' is not a coordinating conjunction like 'and' or 'but' — it's an adverb, and adverbs alone can't join two independent clauses with just a comma.", "illustration": "🔗 LINKING WORDS — SIGNAL THE RELATIONSHIP\nADDITION      → furthermore · moreover · in addition · also\nCONTRAST      → however · on the other hand · nevertheless · whereas\nCAUSE/EFFECT  → therefore · consequently · as a result · thus\nEXAMPLE       → for instance · for example · such as\n\n⚠️ PUNCTUATION TRAP:\n❌ \"The results were good, however, more research is needed.\"  (comma splice!)\n✅ \"The results were good; however, more research is needed.\"  (semicolon before, comma after)\n✅ \"The results were good. However, more research is needed.\"  (new sentence)", "steps": ["Identify the logical relationship between your two ideas: addition, contrast, cause/result, or sequence.", "Choose a linking word that matches: furthermore/in addition (addition), however/nevertheless (contrast), therefore/as a result (cause-result), then/subsequently (sequence).", "Place most sentence-connecting linking words at the start of a new sentence, followed by a comma (However, the results were unexpected.).", "Use a semicolon before and a comma after a linking word when joining two independent clauses in one sentence (The plan failed; however, we learned a lot.).", "Vary your linking words across a piece of writing instead of repeating the same one, to keep the text engaging."], "rule": "Cohesive linking words connect ideas across sentences and paragraphs, showing relationships such as addition (furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless), cause/result (therefore, as a result), and similarity (similarly, likewise). Choosing the right connector — and punctuating it correctly, usually with a comma when it opens a sentence — makes writing easier to follow.", "structure": "Sentence. Connector, sentence continues.", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Addition", "definition": "Words like furthermore, in addition and moreover add a further point in the same direction as the previous one.", "example": "\"The plan is cost-effective. Furthermore, it can be implemented quickly.\""}, {"term": "2. Contrast", "definition": "Words like however, nevertheless and on the other hand introduce an idea that goes against what came before.", "example": "\"The results were promising; however, the sample size was small.\""}, {"term": "3. Cause and result", "definition": "Words like therefore, as a result and consequently show that what follows happens because of what came before.", "example": "\"Demand fell sharply; as a result, prices dropped.\""}, {"term": "4. Punctuation with sentence-initial connectors", "definition": "A connective adverb opening a sentence is usually followed by a comma, and it needs a semicolon or full stop (not just a comma) before it to avoid a comma splice.", "example": "\"The budget was cut; nevertheless, the project succeeded.\""}], "examples": ["The results were inconclusive. Nevertheless, the team proceeded with the next phase.", "She missed several classes; as a result, she struggled with the final exam.", "The first method was slow. The second, however, produced faster results."], "mistakes": ["Using a comma alone to join two independent clauses instead of a semicolon or full stop before the connector.", "Choosing a connector that doesn't match the logical relationship, e.g. using 'therefore' where a contrast ('however') is actually needed.", "Forgetting the comma directly after a sentence-initial connective adverb, e.g. 'Therefore we changed our plan' instead of 'Therefore, we changed our plan'."], "tip": "Before choosing a connector, identify the exact logical relationship first — addition, contrast, cause, result, or comparison — then pick the word that matches it precisely.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which connector shows a result?", "options": ["however", "therefore", "although", "whereas"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.", "options": ["However the results were mixed.", "However, the results were mixed.", "However; the results were mixed.", "However: the results were mixed."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g37", "day": 37, "level": "B2", "title": "Hedging and Cautious Language", "summary": "Soften claims appropriately in formal or academic writing.", "plainExplanation": "Hedging is the deliberate use of cautious, softened language to avoid sounding overly certain or absolute — it's essential in academic, professional, and diplomatic writing, where blunt, 100%-certain claims can sound naive, arrogant, or simply inaccurate if the evidence doesn't fully support them. Common hedging tools include modal verbs of possibility (may, might, could — 'This may suggest a correlation'), tentative verbs (suggest, indicate, appear to, seem to — 'The data appears to indicate a trend'), adverbs of frequency or degree (often, generally, largely, to some extent — 'This is often the case'), and cautious quantifying phrases (a number of, some, in many cases). Compare 'Social media causes anxiety' (an absolute, unhedged claim that's hard to fully prove) with 'Social media may contribute to anxiety in some individuals' (a hedged claim that's more defensible and precise, because it acknowledges the limits of what the evidence actually shows). Learning to hedge appropriately isn't about being vague or wishy-washy for its own sake — it's about matching the strength of your language to the actual strength of your evidence, which is a hallmark of careful, credible academic and professional writing.", "illustration": "🛡️ HEDGING — MATCH YOUR CONFIDENCE TO YOUR EVIDENCE\nTOO STRONG:  \"Social media causes anxiety.\"        ← absolute claim, hard to fully prove\nHEDGED:      \"Social media may contribute to anxiety in some individuals.\"\n\nHEDGING TOOLS:\nMODALS       → may · might · could\nVERBS        → suggest · indicate · appear to · seem to\nADVERBS      → often · generally · largely · to some extent\nQUANTIFIERS  → a number of · some · in many cases", "steps": ["Identify a strong claim you want to soften to sound more careful and realistic.", "Use modal verbs of possibility (may, might, could) instead of will/must for uncertain claims.", "Use hedging verbs like 'suggest', 'indicate', 'appear to', and 'tend to' instead of stating something as fact.", "Add hedging adverbs like 'possibly', 'arguably', 'generally', or 'to some extent' to qualify a statement.", "Reserve fully confident language (clearly, definitely, undoubtedly) only for claims that are genuinely well-supported by strong evidence."], "rule": "Hedging language allows a writer to present claims with an appropriate level of certainty rather than stating everything as absolute fact. Common hedges include modal verbs (may, might, could), reporting/evidential verbs (appears to, seems to, tends to), and phrases like 'it is possible that' or 'to some extent'. This is especially important in academic and professional writing, where overstating certainty can undermine credibility.", "structure": "Subject + hedging verb/modal + claim", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Modal hedges", "definition": "Modal verbs like may, might and could soften a claim by presenting it as possible rather than certain.", "example": "\"This factor might explain the result.\""}, {"term": "2. Evidential/reporting verbs", "definition": "Verbs like appears to, seems to and tends to attribute a claim to observation or pattern rather than stating it as fact.", "example": "\"The method appears to reduce errors.\""}, {"term": "3. Hedging phrases", "definition": "Fixed phrases such as 'it is possible that' and 'to some extent' explicitly flag a claim as limited or uncertain.", "example": "\"It is possible that other factors also played a role.\""}, {"term": "4. Matching hedge strength to evidence", "definition": "The degree of hedging should reflect how strong the actual evidence is — strong evidence can carry firmer language, weak evidence needs more caution.", "example": "\"The data strongly suggests (not 'proves') a link.\""}], "examples": ["The data suggests a possible link, although further research is needed.", "This approach tends to produce more consistent results.", "It is possible that external factors also contributed to the outcome."], "mistakes": ["Overstating claims with words like 'proves', 'always', or 'definitely' when the evidence only supports a more limited conclusion.", "Over-hedging every single sentence, which can make writing sound vague or unconfident rather than precise.", "Using a hedge that doesn't match the actual confidence level, e.g. 'might possibly' (double hedge) when a single hedge would be clearer."], "tip": "Match your level of certainty to your actual evidence — strong evidence can support firmer language, while limited evidence calls for hedged language.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence uses hedging language?", "options": ["This always works.", "This proves the theory completely.", "This may explain the result.", "This never fails."], "answer": 2}, {"q": "Why is hedging often preferred in academic writing?", "options": ["It sounds more impressive.", "It avoids overstating certainty beyond the evidence.", "It makes sentences shorter.", "It replaces the need for evidence."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g38", "day": 38, "level": "B2", "title": "Collocations and Word Partnerships", "summary": "Combine words the way fluent speakers naturally pair them.", "plainExplanation": "Collocations are words that habitually pair together in English, even when there's no logical grammatical rule forcing that pairing — native speakers just expect certain combinations and find others jarring, even if the 'wrong' word means almost the same thing. You 'make a decision' and 'do your homework', not 'do a decision' or 'make your homework' — there's no grammatical reason for this split, it's simply convention built up through common usage. Common collocation patterns worth learning as fixed chunks include verb + noun ('make a mistake', 'take a break', 'pay attention', 'do damage'), adjective + noun ('heavy rain', 'strong coffee', 'deep sleep' — not 'strong rain' or 'heavy coffee'), and adverb + adjective ('deeply concerned', 'highly unlikely', 'utterly exhausted'). Because collocations resist logical prediction, the most effective strategy for learning them isn't trying to derive the 'correct' verb through reasoning, but instead memorising them as complete word-partnership chunks — the same way you'd learn a single piece of vocabulary — and paying close attention to which words naturally travel together when you read and listen to authentic English.", "illustration": "🤝 COLLOCATIONS — WORDS THAT TRAVEL TOGETHER (NO LOGICAL RULE!)\nVERB + NOUN:      make a decision (not 'do')   |   do homework (not 'make')\n                  take a break   |   pay attention   |   do damage\nADJECTIVE + NOUN: heavy rain (not 'strong')   |   strong coffee (not 'heavy')\n                  deep sleep\nADVERB + ADJECTIVE: deeply concerned   |   highly unlikely   |   utterly exhausted\n\n💡 Learn these as whole CHUNKS, not word-by-word logic.", "steps": ["Learn common verb + noun collocations as fixed pairs, not by translating word for word (make a mistake, take a break, do homework).", "Pay attention to adjective + noun collocations that native speakers use automatically (heavy rain, strong coffee, not big rain or powerful coffee).", "Notice adverb + adjective collocations that intensify meaning naturally (deeply concerned, highly unlikely).", "When reading or listening, actively record new collocations you notice rather than just single words.", "Test a combination that feels uncertain by searching how frequently native speakers actually use it, rather than assuming logical combinations are always correct."], "rule": "Collocations are combinations of words that speakers of a language naturally use together, even though other combinations might seem logically equally valid. Common types include verb + noun (make a decision, do damage), adjective + noun (heavy rain, strong opinion), and adverb + adjective (deeply concerned). Learning collocations as fixed chunks, rather than translating word-by-word, is key to sounding natural.", "structure": "verb + noun collocation | adjective + noun collocation", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Verb + noun collocations", "definition": "Certain verbs pair naturally with certain nouns even when a synonym would seem logically equal.", "example": "\"make a decision\" (not 'do a decision')"}, {"term": "2. Adjective + noun collocations", "definition": "Certain adjectives conventionally describe certain nouns, and swapping in a similar adjective can sound unnatural.", "example": "\"heavy rain\" (not 'big rain')"}, {"term": "3. Adverb + adjective collocations", "definition": "Some adverbs intensify specific adjectives more naturally than others do, even with near-identical meanings.", "example": "\"deeply concerned\" (not 'strongly concerned')"}, {"term": "4. Learning collocations as chunks", "definition": "Because collocations are not always predictable from logic, it is more reliable to memorise them as whole fixed phrases rather than translating word by word.", "example": "\"pay close attention\" learned as one unit, not built from 'pay' + 'close' + 'attention' separately."}], "examples": ["The company took a huge risk by launching early.", "She pays close attention to detail.", "It was a great pleasure to meet you."], "mistakes": ["Mixing up common verb-noun pairs, e.g. 'do a mistake' instead of 'make a mistake'.", "Using a logical but unnatural word pairing, e.g. 'big rain' instead of the conventional 'heavy rain'.", "Translating a collocation directly from another language, which often produces a combination that is grammatically correct but not how English speakers actually phrase it."], "tip": "When you learn a new noun, learn its common collocating verb and adjective at the same time, rather than each word in isolation.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the natural collocation.", "options": ["make homework", "do homework", "take homework", "have homework done always"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the natural collocation.", "options": ["do a decision", "make a decision", "take a decision only", "have a decision"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g39", "day": 39, "level": "C1", "title": "Ellipsis and Substitution", "summary": "Avoid repetition by omitting or replacing understood words.", "plainExplanation": "Ellipsis is the deliberate omission of words that are understood from context, allowing you to avoid clunky, unnecessary repetition — a natural feature of both spoken and written English, not a mistake. Substitution is a closely related technique: instead of dropping a word entirely, you replace it with a short placeholder word like 'one', 'do', or 'so'. Example of ellipsis: 'She can speak French, and he can [speak French] too' — the bracketed part is simply dropped because it's obvious from what came before. Example of substitution: 'I need a pen. Do you have one?' — 'one' substitutes for 'a pen' rather than repeating it, and 'She thinks it will rain, and I think so too' — 'so' substitutes for the entire clause 'it will rain'. Both techniques rely entirely on the previous context making the missing or replaced material unambiguous — if a reader genuinely couldn't reconstruct what's missing, the ellipsis has gone too far and creates confusion rather than economy. Skilled use of ellipsis and substitution is a mark of natural, economical writing; overusing full repetition, on the other hand, can make writing feel stiff, robotic, and unnecessarily long.", "illustration": "✂️ ELLIPSIS — DROP WHAT'S ALREADY CLEAR\n\"She can speak French, and he can [speak French] too.\"  ← bracket = safely dropped\n\n🔁 SUBSTITUTION — REPLACE WITH A SHORT PLACEHOLDER\n\"I need a pen. Do you have one?\"        ← 'one' replaces 'a pen'\n\"She thinks it will rain, and I think so too.\"  ← 'so' replaces 'it will rain'\n\n⚠️ Only works when the reader can easily reconstruct the missing/replaced idea.", "steps": ["Find words that are repeated or clearly understood from the previous clause.", "Omit them entirely if the meaning stays clear (ellipsis): She can swim and (she can) dive.", "Or replace them with a substitute word (substitution): I need a pen. Do you have one? (one = a pen).", "Use 'so' or 'not' to substitute a whole clause after verbs like think, hope, believe (I think so, I hope not).", "Check that the omitted or substituted information is still fully recoverable by the reader/listener — don't create ambiguity."], "rule": "Ellipsis is the omission of words that are clearly understood from context, helping avoid unnecessary repetition. Substitution replaces a repeated word or phrase with a shorter word such as 'one', 'do/does/did', 'so', or 'not'. Both devices are common in natural spoken and written English and contribute to cohesion.", "structure": "...omit repeated words... | ...substitute with one/do/so/not...", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Ellipsis — omitting predictable words", "definition": "A word or phrase that is fully understood from context can be dropped entirely, rather than repeated.", "example": "\"I wanted to go, but I couldn't [go].\""}, {"term": "2. Substitution with 'one'", "definition": "'One' replaces a singular countable noun already mentioned, avoiding repetition of the noun itself.", "example": "\"I need a pen; do you have one?\""}, {"term": "3. Substitution with do/does/did", "definition": "The auxiliary do stands in for a repeated verb phrase, especially after 'too' or 'so'.", "example": "\"He works hard, and she does too.\""}, {"term": "4. Substitution with 'so' and 'not'", "definition": "'So' replaces a whole repeated idea in a positive sense, and 'not' replaces it in a negative sense, both commonly after verbs like think, hope, believe.", "example": "\"Is it going to rain? I think so.\" / \"I hope not.\""}], "examples": ["I wanted to go, but I couldn't [go].", "A: Is it going to rain? B: I think so.", "He passed the test, and so did his sister."], "mistakes": ["Omitting words that are not actually clear from context, causing ambiguity for the reader.", "Using 'it' instead of 'one' when referring to a non-specific member of a category, e.g. 'I need a pen; do you have it?' instead of 'do you have one?'.", "Repeating the full phrase where substitution would sound more natural, which can make writing feel repetitive rather than concise."], "tip": "Only use ellipsis when the omitted word is completely predictable from the previous clause — if there's any doubt, keep the word in.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence uses substitution correctly?", "options": ["He likes tea, and she likes tea too.", "He likes tea, and she does too.", "He likes tea, and she like too.", "He likes tea, and she also likes."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which is an example of ellipsis?", "options": ["I can swim, and she can swim.", "I can swim, and she can too.", "I can swim, and she can [swim] too.", "I can swim, and she can swimming too."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g40", "day": 40, "level": "C1", "title": "Nominalisation for Formality", "summary": "Convert verbs and adjectives into nouns to create a more formal, academic tone.", "plainExplanation": "Nominalisation is the process of turning a verb or adjective into a noun form — for example, 'decide' (verb) becomes 'decision' (noun), 'analyse' becomes 'analysis', and 'important' (adjective) becomes 'importance'. This shift is one of the defining features of formal, academic, and professional writing, because it lets you package an entire action or quality into a compact noun phrase, which in turn allows for more sophisticated sentence structures: instead of 'They decided to implement the policy, and this affected morale' (two clauses, informal, verb-heavy), academic style often prefers 'The decision to implement the policy affected morale' (one clause, the decision itself becomes the grammatical subject, allowing a tighter, more analytical sentence). Compare more examples: 'They analysed the data carefully' (verb-centred, active, more conversational) versus 'A careful analysis of the data was conducted' (noun-centred, more formal, often passive). While nominalisation is a genuine hallmark of academic register, overusing it can make writing feel dense, abstract, and harder to follow — skilled academic writers use it strategically for key concepts and topic sentences, while still relying on direct verbs elsewhere to keep the writing readable.", "illustration": "🔄 NOMINALISATION — VERB/ADJECTIVE → NOUN\ndecide (verb)    → decision (noun)\nanalyse (verb)   → analysis (noun)\nimportant (adj)  → importance (noun)\n\nINFORMAL/VERB-CENTRED:  \"They decided to implement the policy, and this affected morale.\"\nFORMAL/NOUN-CENTRED:    \"The decision to implement the policy affected morale.\"\n                          ↑ compact noun phrase becomes the subject\n\n⚠️ Overuse = dense, hard-to-follow writing. Use strategically, not everywhere.", "steps": ["Find an active verb or descriptive adjective in an informal sentence.", "Convert it into its noun form using common suffixes: -tion, -sion, -ment, -ness, -ity (decide → decision, develop → development).", "Restructure the sentence around the new noun, usually with a 'light' verb like make, take, or conduct (make a decision, conduct an analysis).", "Use nominalisation to shift focus from a specific actor to a general process or result — well suited to reports and essays.", "Avoid overusing nominalisation in casual or conversational writing, where it can sound stiff or overly bureaucratic."], "rule": "Nominalisation converts a verb or adjective into its noun form (decide → decision, aware → awareness, apply → application). This is a hallmark of formal and academic writing because it allows writers to package actions and qualities as compact 'things' that can then be described, quantified, or made the subject of a sentence, creating a more objective, information-dense style.", "structure": "verb/adjective → noun form + supporting clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Verb-to-noun nominalisation", "definition": "A verb is converted into its noun form, allowing the action itself to become the subject or object of a sentence.", "example": "\"decide\" → \"decision\": \"The decision surprised everyone.\""}, {"term": "2. Adjective-to-noun nominalisation", "definition": "An adjective is converted into a noun form, allowing a quality to be discussed as a 'thing' in its own right.", "example": "\"aware\" → \"awareness\": \"Public awareness has grown.\""}, {"term": "3. Creating a denser, more formal style", "definition": "Packaging actions and qualities as nouns lets a writer add detail (adjectives, quantifiers) that would be clumsy attached directly to a verb.", "example": "\"a noticeable improvement in performance\" (denser than \"performance improved noticeably\")"}, {"term": "4. Balancing density with clarity", "definition": "Nominalisation should be used selectively to foreground key results or processes, not applied to every verb in a paragraph, or the writing becomes hard to follow.", "example": "One well-placed nominalisation per key sentence, not stacked in every clause."}], "examples": ["The implementation of the new policy caused some initial confusion.", "There was a noticeable improvement in performance after training.", "The team's analysis revealed several inconsistencies."], "mistakes": ["Overusing nominalisation to the point where sentences become clogged with abstract nouns and lose clarity.", "Creating an incorrect noun form, e.g. 'the applying of the rule' instead of 'the application of the rule'.", "Nominalising a verb that already reads naturally as a verb, adding unnecessary formality where a direct verb would be clearer."], "tip": "Use nominalisation strategically to foreground the result or process itself, but don't let every verb in a paragraph become a noun — clarity still comes first.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence uses nominalisation?", "options": ["They decided quickly.", "Their decision was quick.", "They quickly decide.", "Deciding, they were quick."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Why is nominalisation common in academic writing?", "options": ["It makes sentences shorter and less formal.", "It allows ideas to be presented more densely and objectively.", "It removes the need for verbs entirely.", "It is only used in spoken English."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g41", "day": 41, "level": "C1", "title": "Advanced Modality and Stance", "summary": "Signal the writer's attitude and level of commitment to a claim precisely.", "plainExplanation": "Advanced modality and stance markers let a writer show exactly how confident, cautious, or committed they are to a claim — a more nuanced toolkit than the basic modal verbs (can, might, must) covered earlier. Stance adverbs signal the writer's evaluation or degree of certainty directly: 'arguably' (this is debatable but defensible), 'undoubtedly' (very high confidence), 'presumably' (a reasonable assumption, not confirmed fact), 'admittedly' (conceding a point before countering it). Stance verbs and phrases work similarly: 'it is widely believed that...', 'the evidence strongly suggests...', 'one could argue that...' — each signals a different strength of commitment to the claim that follows. This kind of stance-marking is essential for persuasive and academic writing because it lets you position your own claims precisely along a spectrum from tentative to confident, and also lets you accurately represent how strong the existing evidence or consensus actually is, rather than flattening every claim into the same uniform level of certainty. A writer who never varies their stance markers — treating every claim as equally certain — often comes across as either naive (if genuinely certain about everything) or unpersuasive (if the reader senses the uniform confidence isn't actually justified by the evidence).", "illustration": "🎚️ STANCE MARKERS — A DIAL, NOT A SWITCH\nHIGH CONFIDENCE   → undoubtedly · clearly · it is evident that\nMODERATE          → arguably · the evidence suggests · it is widely believed that\nCAUTIOUS/TENTATIVE → presumably · one could argue that · it might be said that\nCONCEDING A POINT → admittedly · granted · while it is true that...\n\n💡 Vary the dial to match your actual confidence in each specific claim.", "steps": ["Identify exactly how certain, obligated, or critical you want to sound about a situation.", "Combine modals with perfect forms to talk about past possibilities or missed obligations (should have known, could have helped, needn't have worried).", "Combine modals with continuous forms to describe an ongoing state (must be sleeping, might be travelling).", "Use modal + passive constructions for formal, impersonal stance (This issue must be addressed).", "Choose your modal carefully to match the exact strength of your stance — must vs should vs could carry very different levels of force."], "rule": "Advanced modality and stance markers let a writer signal exactly how confident, tentative, or evaluative they are about a claim. Expressions range from strongly certain (undoubtedly, is bound to) to cautiously possible (arguably, might conceivably) to explicitly evaluative (it could be argued that, is likely to). Skilled writers vary these expressions deliberately to match the strength of their evidence.", "structure": "Stance expression + claim + (supporting justification)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Strongly certain markers", "definition": "Words like undoubtedly and 'is bound to' commit the writer to a very high level of confidence in the claim.", "example": "\"Undoubtedly, the reform changed public opinion.\""}, {"term": "2. Cautiously possible markers", "definition": "Words like arguably and 'might conceivably' present a claim as reasonable but genuinely open to debate.", "example": "\"Arguably, the short-term costs outweigh the long-term benefits.\""}, {"term": "3. Explicitly evaluative phrases", "definition": "Phrases like 'it could be argued that' and 'is likely to' frame a statement as the writer's judgement rather than an established fact.", "example": "\"It could be argued that the policy was premature.\""}, {"term": "4. Varying stance across an argument", "definition": "Skilled writers deliberately shift between stronger and weaker stance markers to match the actual strength of each individual claim, rather than using one expression throughout.", "example": "Using 'undoubtedly' for a well-evidenced point and 'arguably' for a more contestable one, in the same paragraph."}], "examples": ["Undoubtedly, the reform changed public opinion.", "Arguably, the short-term costs outweigh the long-term benefits.", "This trend is likely to continue if current conditions persist."], "mistakes": ["Using only one stance expression repeatedly, which flattens the nuance of an argument.", "Choosing an overly strong stance marker (e.g. 'undoubtedly') for a claim that is genuinely debatable or under-evidenced.", "Stacking two stance markers together unnecessarily, e.g. 'arguably, it might possibly be true', which weakens rather than strengthens the sentence."], "tip": "Before writing a claim, decide honestly how certain you actually are, then pick the stance expression that matches — don't inflate confidence for effect.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which phrase signals a debatable stance rather than a fact?", "options": ["It is a fact that...", "It could be argued that...", "It is proven that...", "It is certain that..."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which sentence shows strong, near-certain commitment?", "options": ["This might possibly help.", "This could perhaps help.", "This will undoubtedly help.", "This may or may not help."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g42", "day": 42, "level": "C1", "title": "Grammar for Persuasive Argument", "summary": "Combine advanced structures to build a controlled, convincing argument.", "plainExplanation": "Persuasive academic and professional writing relies on specific grammatical structures to build a convincing argument, beyond just having good ideas. Concession and rebuttal structures acknowledge an opposing view before countering it, which makes an argument feel balanced and credible rather than one-sided: 'While some argue that X, the evidence suggests that Y' or 'Although it is true that X, this overlooks the fact that Y.' Rhetorical questions can draw the reader into considering a point actively rather than passively: 'If not now, then when?' Parallel structure — repeating the same grammatical pattern across a series — creates rhythm and emphasis: 'We must act quickly, decisively, and collectively' (three adverbs in a row, matched grammatical form) rather than mixing forms awkwardly ('quickly, with decision, and as a group'). Cause-and-effect connectors (therefore, consequently, as a result) make the logical chain of an argument explicit rather than leaving the reader to infer it. Together, these structures do the grammatical heavy lifting that turns a list of opinions into a genuinely persuasive, well-organised piece of argumentative writing — the grammar itself becomes part of the persuasive strategy, not just a container for ideas.", "illustration": "🎯 PERSUASIVE GRAMMAR TOOLKIT\nCONCESSION + REBUTTAL:  \"While some argue X, the evidence suggests Y.\"\nRHETORICAL QUESTION:    \"If not now, then when?\"\nPARALLEL STRUCTURE:     \"We must act quickly, decisively, and collectively.\"\n                          ✅ adverb, adverb, adverb (matched form)\n                          ❌ \"quickly, with decision, and as a group\" (mismatched)\nCAUSE→EFFECT:           \"...; therefore, ...\" / \"...; consequently, ...\"", "steps": ["Use emphatic auxiliary verbs (do/does/did + base verb) to stress a point strongly (This does matter).", "Use rhetorical questions to engage the reader and imply an obvious answer (Who would argue against safety?).", "Use strong modal verbs like must and should to argue for necessity and obligation, not just possibility.", "Use concessive conjunctions (although, while) to acknowledge counterarguments before refuting them, which builds credibility.", "Use cleft sentences and fronting to place the most persuasive information first in the sentence for maximum impact."], "rule": "Persuasive academic argument combines several advanced tools at once: precise claims, hedging to stay credible, cohesive linking to guide the reader logically, and occasional emphatic structures (cleft sentences, inversion) to spotlight the most important point. The goal is controlled persuasion — convincing through clarity and calibrated confidence, not overstatement.", "structure": "Claim + hedged justification + cohesive link + (emphatic structure for key point)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Precise claims", "definition": "A persuasive argument starts from a specific, well-formed claim rather than a vague generalisation.", "example": "\"Early intervention reduces long-term costs.\" (not \"This is generally a good idea.\")"}, {"term": "2. Hedging for credibility", "definition": "Cautious language keeps a strong claim believable by acknowledging its limits, rather than overstating certainty.", "example": "\"This approach appears to reduce costs in most cases.\""}, {"term": "3. Cohesive linking", "definition": "Connectors guide the reader logically from one point to the next, making the argument easy to follow.", "example": "\"Although the evidence is not exhaustive, it strongly suggests early action is beneficial.\""}, {"term": "4. Emphatic structures, used sparingly", "definition": "Cleft sentences and inversion spotlight the single most important point in an argument, but lose their impact if overused.", "example": "\"What remains contested is its cost-effectiveness.\""}], "examples": ["It is widely accepted that this approach improves outcomes; what remains contested is its cost-effectiveness.", "Rarely does a single intervention address every aspect of the problem.", "Although the evidence is not exhaustive, it strongly suggests that early action is beneficial."], "mistakes": ["Overusing emphatic structures like cleft sentences or inversion, which can make writing feel exaggerated rather than persuasive.", "Neglecting hedging entirely in an effort to sound persuasive, which can make an argument seem overconfident and less credible.", "Stating the claim without ever linking it to supporting evidence or a logical connector, leaving the argument feeling disconnected."], "tip": "Build a persuasive paragraph in layers: state the claim, hedge it appropriately, link it to the next idea, and reserve emphatic structures for the single most important point.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence best balances a claim with appropriate caution?", "options": ["This always works for everyone.", "This appears to work well in most documented cases.", "This never fails under any circumstances.", "This is impossible to question."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which technique adds emphasis to a key point in persuasive writing?", "options": ["Random capitalisation", "A cleft sentence such as 'What matters most is...'", "Repeating the same word many times", "Removing all punctuation"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g43", "day": 43, "level": "A1", "title": "Negatives and Questions in Present and Past", "summary": "Form clear negative sentences and questions using do/does/did.", "plainExplanation": "Forming negatives and questions in the present and past both rely on the same helper verbs, called auxiliaries — do/does for present, did for past — which do the grammatical work so the main verb doesn't have to change form. For negatives, insert not right after the auxiliary: 'I do not (don't) like coffee', 'She does not (doesn't) like coffee', 'They did not (didn't) go.' For questions, move the auxiliary to the very front of the sentence, before the subject: 'Do you like coffee?', 'Does she like coffee?', 'Did they go?' Notice that in every case, the main verb stays in its plain base form — the auxiliary alone carries the tense and the person marking (the -s for third person, or the past tense meaning). This is genuinely one of the most distinctive features of English grammar compared to many other languages, where you'd typically just change the main verb itself to form a question or negative. The one major exception is the verb 'be' (am/is/are, was/were), which never needs do/does/did — it moves to the front for questions and takes 'not' directly after itself for negatives: 'Is she happy?', 'She is not happy', not 'Does she be happy?'", "illustration": "🔧 DO/DOES/DID — THE HELPER THAT DOES THE WORK\nPRESENT:  do/does + not + base verb     → \"I don't like coffee.\"\n          Do/Does + subject + base verb? → \"Does she like coffee?\"\nPAST:     did + not + base verb          → \"They didn't go.\"\n          Did + subject + base verb?     → \"Did they go?\"\n\n⚠️ EXCEPTION: the verb 'BE' never needs do/does/did\n  \"Is she happy?\"  /  \"She is not happy.\"   (NOT \"Does she be happy?\")", "steps": ["For present simple negatives, use don't/doesn't + base verb (I don't work, she doesn't work).", "For past simple negatives, use didn't + base verb, even for irregular verbs (I didn't go, not I didn't went).", "For present simple questions, move do/does before the subject (Do you work? Does she work?).", "For past simple questions, move did before the subject and use the base verb (Did you go? Did she see it?).", "Remember the verb 'be' never uses do/does/did — invert be and the subject directly instead (Is he here? He isn't here)."], "rule": "In English, present-simple negatives and questions are formed with do/does + not, and past-simple negatives and questions are formed with did + not, regardless of the subject. The auxiliary (do/does/did) carries the tense, so the main verb always stays in its base form.", "structure": "Subject + do/does/did + not + base verb | Do/Does/Did + subject + base verb?", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Present simple negatives", "definition": "Do/does + not forms the negative, and the main verb returns to its base form regardless of subject.", "example": "\"She doesn't like coffee.\" (not 'doesn't likes')"}, {"term": "2. Present simple questions", "definition": "Do/does moves to the front of the question, and the main verb again stays in its base form.", "example": "\"Does he study every day?\""}, {"term": "3. Past simple negatives", "definition": "Did + not forms the negative for any subject, and the main verb loses its past ending once did is present.", "example": "\"They didn't arrive until midnight.\" (not 'didn't arrived')"}, {"term": "4. Past simple questions", "definition": "Did moves to the front of the question, and the main verb stays in the base form, never the past form.", "example": "\"Did you visit Bagan last year?\""}], "examples": ["I don't understand this rule.", "Does she speak French?", "They didn't arrive until midnight."], "mistakes": ["Adding -s or -ed to the main verb as well as the auxiliary, e.g. 'She doesn't likes it' or 'Did you went there?'.", "Forgetting the auxiliary entirely in questions, e.g. 'You like coffee?' instead of 'Do you like coffee?' in written English.", "Using 'don't' with a third-person singular subject instead of 'doesn't', e.g. 'He don't know' instead of 'He doesn't know'."], "tip": "Once you've used do/does/did, the main verb never changes form — it stays as the plain base verb.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct negative sentence.", "options": ["He don't like tea.", "He doesn't likes tea.", "He doesn't like tea.", "He not like tea."], "answer": 2}, {"q": "Choose the correct past question.", "options": ["Did she went home?", "Did she goes home?", "Did she go home?", "Does she went home?"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g44", "day": 44, "level": "A2", "title": "Prepositions of Movement and Direction", "summary": "Describe motion and direction accurately using prepositions.", "plainExplanation": "Prepositions of movement and direction describe how something moves from one place to another, and each one paints a slightly different picture of that movement. 'To' shows movement toward a destination ('She walked to the station'). 'Into' shows movement toward the inside of an enclosed space ('He walked into the room' — crossing the threshold to the inside, different from just 'to the room', which only implies arriving near it). 'Onto' shows movement toward the top of a surface ('The cat jumped onto the table'). 'Through' shows movement from one side of something to the other, passing inside it along the way ('They walked through the tunnel'). 'Across' shows movement from one side to the other of a flat, open area or surface ('She swam across the lake'). 'Along' shows movement following the length of something, like a path or a line ('We walked along the beach'). 'Past' shows movement that goes near something and continues beyond it ('He drove past the school without stopping'). Getting these right matters because 'into' vs 'in', and 'onto' vs 'on', signal genuinely different meanings — 'in' and 'on' describe a static position (already there), while 'into' and 'onto' describe the movement of arriving at that position.", "illustration": "🧭 MOVEMENT PREPOSITIONS — PICTURE THE PATH\nTO      → toward a destination        \"She walked to the station.\"\nINTO    → toward the INSIDE (crossing a threshold)  \"He walked into the room.\"\nONTO    → toward the TOP of a surface  \"The cat jumped onto the table.\"\nTHROUGH → passing from one side to the other, INSIDE something  \"through the tunnel\"\nACROSS  → from one side to the other, over an open area  \"swam across the lake\"\nALONG   → following the length of something  \"walked along the beach\"\nPAST    → moving near something and continuing beyond it  \"drove past the school\"\n\n⚠️ into/onto = MOVEMENT to a place; in/on = STATIC position already there", "steps": ["Identify the starting point, path, and destination of the movement.", "Use 'to' for movement toward a destination (go to the station).", "Use 'into' for entering an enclosed space, and 'onto' for moving onto a surface (walk into the room, jump onto the table).", "Use 'through' for moving inside and out the other side of something, and 'across' for moving from one side to another over a surface.", "Use 'along' for movement following a line, like a road or river (walk along the beach)."], "rule": "Prepositions of movement describe the path or direction an action follows, unlike prepositions of static location (in, on, at). Common movement prepositions include to (destination), into/out of (entering/exiting an enclosed space), through (from one side of an enclosed space to the other), across (from one side of an open area to the other), along (following a length), and over/under (above/below something).", "structure": "verb of movement + preposition + place", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. to — destination", "definition": "Shows movement toward an end point, without specifying entry into an enclosed space.", "example": "\"She walked to the station.\""}, {"term": "2. into / out of — entering or exiting an enclosed space", "definition": "Marks the moment of entry or exit from a bounded area, distinct from simply being located inside it.", "example": "\"He walked into the room and out of the meeting shortly after.\""}, {"term": "3. through / across — crossing an area", "definition": "Through describes movement from one side of an enclosed space to the other; across describes movement over an open area.", "example": "\"They walked through the tunnel and across the field.\""}, {"term": "4. along / over / under — following or passing a path", "definition": "Along follows the length of something; over and under describe movement above or below an obstacle.", "example": "\"We walked along the river, over the bridge, and under the archway.\""}], "examples": ["The children ran across the playground.", "She climbed over the wall.", "We walked along the river for an hour."], "mistakes": ["Confusing static location prepositions with movement prepositions, e.g. 'He walked in the room' when 'into the room' is meant to show entry.", "Using 'through' for an open area instead of 'across', e.g. 'She walked through the field' when 'across the field' better fits an open space.", "Omitting the preposition entirely with a direction verb, e.g. 'He walked the room' instead of 'He walked into the room'."], "tip": "Ask whether the sentence describes a fixed position (use in/on/at) or a path/direction (use movement prepositions like into, through, across, along).", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["She walked in the room slowly opening the door.", "She walked into the room.", "She walked at the room.", "She walked on the room."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct preposition: 'The cat jumped ___ the fence.'", "options": ["over", "in", "at", "on top"], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g45", "day": 45, "level": "A2", "title": "Used to and Would for Past Habits", "summary": "Talk about past habits and states that are no longer true.", "plainExplanation": "'Used to' and 'would' both describe repeated past habits or states that are no longer true, but they're not fully interchangeable, and each has its own restrictions. 'Used to' + base verb works for both past habits/repeated actions and past states or situations: 'I used to play tennis every weekend' (repeated action) and 'I used to live in Berlin' (a past state). 'Would' + base verb works only for past habits or repeated actions — never for states, feelings, or situations: 'When I was a child, I would visit my grandmother every summer' (repeated action, fine), but 'I would live in Berlin' is wrong if you simply mean you lived there in the past as an ongoing state (you'd need 'I used to live in Berlin' instead). A helpful test: if the sentence describes a specific repeated event you can picture happening again and again, both 'used to' and 'would' typically work; if it describes a general fact, feeling, possession, or ongoing state (not a repeated event), only 'used to' works. Also watch the spelling and structure of the negative and question forms of 'used to', which are a common source of error: 'Did you use to...?' and 'I didn't use to...' (without the -d on 'use' in these forms), not 'Did you used to...?'", "illustration": "🔙 USED TO vs WOULD — PAST HABITS THAT AREN'T TRUE ANYMORE\nUSED TO  → repeated ACTIONS ✅  AND  past STATES ✅\n  \"I used to play tennis every weekend.\"  (action)\n  \"I used to live in Berlin.\"              (state)\nWOULD    → repeated ACTIONS ✅  ONLY — never states!\n  \"I would visit my grandmother every summer.\"  ✅\n  \"I would live in Berlin.\"  ❌ (wrong for a simple past state)\n\n⚠️ Question/negative: \"Did you USE to...?\" / \"I didn't USE to...\"  (no -d on 'use' here!)", "steps": ["Check if you're describing a repeated action or a past state (fact/situation).", "Use 'used to + base verb' for both habits and states (I used to smoke; I used to live in Paris).", "Use 'would + base verb' only for repeated actions, not states (I would visit my grandmother every summer — not 'I would live in Paris').", "Form negatives and questions of 'used to' with did (Did you use to smoke? I didn't use to smoke) — note the spelling drops the -d.", "Don't confuse 'used to + base verb' (past habit) with 'be used to + -ing' (be accustomed to something now) — they mean very different things."], "rule": "Both 'used to' and 'would' can describe repeated actions in the past that no longer happen, but only 'used to' can describe past states (not actions), such as living somewhere, having something, or feeling a certain way. 'Would' is reserved for repeated actions and is common in storytelling.", "structure": "used to + base verb | would + base verb (actions only)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. 'used to' for past states", "definition": "Used to describes a past state — living somewhere, having something, feeling a certain way — that is no longer true. 'Would' cannot replace it here.", "example": "\"I used to be afraid of the dark.\""}, {"term": "2. 'used to' for past habits", "definition": "Used to can also describe a repeated past action, alongside 'would', when the emphasis is on the fact it no longer happens.", "example": "\"She used to work at the hospital before she retrained.\""}, {"term": "3. 'would' for repeated past actions only", "definition": "Would describes a repeated action in the past, common in storytelling, but never describes a state.", "example": "\"Every Friday, we would order pizza and watch a film.\""}, {"term": "4. 'used to' vs 'be used to'", "definition": "'Used to' (past habit) is a completely different structure from 'be used to' (being accustomed to something), which uses a present tense of be plus a gerund.", "example": "\"I used to live in Yangon.\" vs \"I am used to living in Yangon now.\""}], "examples": ["I used to be afraid of the dark. (state — 'would' is not possible here)", "Every Friday, we would order pizza and watch a film. (action)", "She used to work at the hospital before she retrained."], "mistakes": ["Using 'would' with a state verb, e.g. 'I would have a dog when I was young' instead of 'I used to have a dog'.", "Confusing 'used to' (past habit) with 'be used to' (being accustomed to something), which has a completely different meaning and structure.", "Forgetting that 'used to' takes the base verb, not the past form, e.g. 'I used to went' instead of 'I used to go'."], "tip": "If the sentence describes a state (be, have, know, like), use only 'used to'. If it describes a repeated action, either 'used to' or 'would' can work.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence for a past state.", "options": ["I would have a bike when I was a child.", "I used to have a bike when I was a child.", "I use to have a bike when I was a child.", "I am used to have a bike."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which verb type does NOT normally work with 'would' for past habits?", "options": ["action verbs like visit, play", "state verbs like have, know", "verbs describing routines", "verbs describing repeated events"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g46", "day": 46, "level": "B1", "title": "So and Such for Emphasis", "summary": "Intensify adjectives, adverbs and nouns correctly.", "plainExplanation": "'So' and 'such' both intensify an adjective for emphasis, but they attach to different grammatical structures, which is the main thing to get right. 'So' goes directly before an adjective or adverb, with no noun involved: 'The film was so boring.' If a noun does follow, 'so' needs extra help from 'that' plus a full clause explaining the result, and even then, 'so' still goes right before the adjective, not the noun: 'It was so boring that I fell asleep.' 'Such' goes before a noun phrase (which may include an adjective before the noun): 'It was such a boring film' or simply 'It was such nonsense.' Both can be followed by a 'that'-clause showing a result or consequence: 'The film was so boring that I fell asleep' and 'It was such a boring film that I fell asleep' mean essentially the same thing, but the grammar around the noun differs — notice that with 'such', the article 'a' comes after 'such', not before it ('such a boring film', never 'a such boring film'). A simple way to choose: if there's no noun immediately in the intensified phrase, use 'so'; if there is a noun (with or without an adjective attached), use 'such'.", "illustration": "⚡ SO vs SUCH — DEPENDS ON WHETHER A NOUN FOLLOWS\nSO + adjective/adverb (NO noun)      → \"The film was so boring.\"\nSO + adj + that-clause               → \"It was so boring that I fell asleep.\"\n\nSUCH + (a/an) + adjective + NOUN     → \"It was such a boring film.\"\nSUCH + noun (no adjective)           → \"It was such nonsense.\"\n\n⚠️ Article order: \"such a boring film\"  NOT  \"a such boring film\"", "steps": ["Check what you're emphasising: an adjective/adverb alone, or a noun phrase.", "Use 'so + adjective/adverb' when there's no noun directly after (The exam was so difficult).", "Use 'such + a/an + adjective + noun' when a noun follows (It was such a difficult exam).", "With plural or uncountable nouns, drop the article: such difficult exams, such good advice.", "Add a 'that' clause after so/such to state the result of the extreme degree (It was so difficult that nobody finished)."], "rule": "'So' and 'such' both intensify meaning but attach to different word types. 'So' goes directly before an adjective or adverb with no noun (so tired, so fast). 'Such' goes before a noun phrase, with or without an adjective (such news, such a good idea). Both often introduce a result clause with 'that'.", "structure": "so + adjective/adverb | such (+ a/an) + (adjective) + noun", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. 'so' before adjectives/adverbs alone", "definition": "So intensifies an adjective or adverb directly, with no noun following.", "example": "\"The traffic was so bad.\""}, {"term": "2. 'such' before a noun phrase", "definition": "Such intensifies a noun phrase, with or without an adjective inside it, and requires a/an before a singular countable noun.", "example": "\"It was such a beautiful sunset.\""}, {"term": "3. 'such' with plural or uncountable nouns", "definition": "With plural or uncountable nouns, such is used without an article, since none is needed.", "example": "\"They are such kind people.\""}, {"term": "4. Result clauses with 'that'", "definition": "Both so and such commonly introduce a following result clause with that, showing the consequence of the intensified quality.", "example": "\"She spoke so quietly that I could barely hear her.\""}], "examples": ["The traffic was so bad that we missed the flight.", "It was such a beautiful sunset that everyone stopped to take photos.", "She spoke so quietly that I could barely hear her."], "mistakes": ["Using 'so' before a noun phrase, e.g. 'It was so good idea' instead of 'It was such a good idea'.", "Forgetting the article 'a/an' after 'such' with a singular countable noun, e.g. 'such good idea' instead of 'such a good idea'.", "Adding an unnecessary article after 'such' with a plural noun, e.g. 'such a kind people' instead of 'such kind people'."], "tip": "If a noun follows, use 'such' (with a/an for singular countable nouns); if only an adjective or adverb follows, use 'so'.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["It was so good film.", "It was such good film.", "It was such a good film.", "It was so a good film."], "answer": 2}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["He is so kind person.", "He is such kind.", "He is such a kind person.", "He is so a kind person."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g47", "day": 47, "level": "B1", "title": "Direct and Indirect Questions", "summary": "Ask questions politely using indirect question structures.", "plainExplanation": "Direct questions and indirect (or embedded) questions ask for the same information but use different word order and different tones — direct questions are more common in casual speech, while indirect questions sound noticeably more polite and formal, which is why they're often used to soften a request. A direct question inverts the subject and auxiliary verb, just like a normal question: 'Where is the station?' An indirect question embeds that same question inside a longer, more polite introductory phrase like 'Could you tell me...', 'Do you know...', or 'I was wondering...', and crucially, the embedded question reverts to normal statement word order — subject before verb — with no inversion: 'Could you tell me where the station is?' (not 'where is the station', even though that's a perfectly correct direct question on its own). For yes/no questions being embedded, add 'if' or 'whether': 'Do you know if she is coming?' rather than 'Do you know is she coming?' This shift from question word order to statement word order inside the embedded clause is the single most common error at this level, precisely because it feels counterintuitive — the sentence still ends with a question mark (since the whole utterance is a genuine question), but the internal grammar quietly switches back to normal statement order.", "illustration": "🤝 DIRECT vs INDIRECT QUESTIONS — POLITENESS CHANGES THE WORD ORDER\nDIRECT (casual):    \"Where is the station?\"          ← inverted (question order)\nINDIRECT (polite):  \"Could you tell me where the station is?\"  ← back to statement order!\n\nYES/NO QUESTIONS need 'if' or 'whether':\nDIRECT:    \"Is she coming?\"\nINDIRECT:  \"Do you know if she is coming?\"   (NOT \"do you know is she coming\")\n\n⚠️ Still ends with a question mark — only the INSIDE reverts to statement order.", "steps": ["Decide if you're asking directly or embedding the question politely within a longer sentence.", "For direct questions, invert the subject and auxiliary/be verb (Where is she? Do you know him?).", "For indirect questions, start with a polite frame (Could you tell me..., I wonder..., Do you know...).", "Switch back to normal statement word order inside the indirect question — no inversion (Could you tell me where she is, not where is she).", "Do not add a question mark to an indirect question that is embedded in a statement (I wonder where she is.), but do add one if the whole sentence is still a question (Could you tell me where she is?)."], "rule": "Indirect questions are used to ask something more politely, often introduced by phrases like 'Could you tell me...', 'Do you know...', or 'I wonder if...'. Unlike direct questions, the word order inside an indirect question stays like a normal statement (subject before verb), and yes/no questions use 'if' or 'whether' instead of an auxiliary at the start.", "structure": "Polite phrase + question word/if/whether + subject + verb", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Polite opening phrases", "definition": "Phrases like 'Could you tell me...', 'Do you know...' and 'I wonder if...' introduce a question more politely than a direct question form.", "example": "\"Could you tell me how much this costs?\""}, {"term": "2. Statement word order inside the clause", "definition": "Once the polite opener is used, the embedded clause keeps normal subject-before-verb order, not question order.", "example": "\"Do you know if the store is open today?\" (not 'is the store open')"}, {"term": "3. if / whether for embedded yes/no questions", "definition": "When the original question has a yes/no answer, if or whether introduces the embedded clause instead of an inverted auxiliary.", "example": "\"I wonder if she is coming.\""}, {"term": "4. wh-words for embedded information questions", "definition": "When the original question uses a wh-word, that word is kept, but the clause after it still follows statement order.", "example": "\"I wonder what she meant by that comment.\""}], "examples": ["Could you tell me how much this costs?", "Do you know if the store is open today?", "I wonder what she meant by that comment."], "mistakes": ["Keeping direct-question word order inside the embedded clause, e.g. 'Do you know where is the exit?' instead of 'where the exit is'.", "Omitting 'if' or 'whether' for embedded yes/no questions, e.g. 'I wonder she is coming' instead of 'I wonder if she is coming'.", "Adding a question mark and inverted order together, mixing the direct and indirect forms into one incorrect hybrid."], "tip": "After the polite opening phrase, mentally form a normal statement first (the exit is...), then attach it — don't invert the subject and verb.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct indirect question.", "options": ["Could you tell me where is the station?", "Could you tell me where the station is?", "Could you tell me the station where is?", "Could you tell me is the station where?"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct indirect question.", "options": ["Do you know what does he want?", "Do you know what he wants?", "Do you know what wants he?", "Do you know does he want what?"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g48", "day": 48, "level": "B1", "title": "Causative Verbs: Have and Get Something Done", "summary": "Describe actions arranged for someone else to perform.", "plainExplanation": "Causative verbs let you talk about arranging for someone else to do something for you, rather than doing it yourself — the structure shifts attention from who actually performs the action to who arranged for it to happen. 'Have something done' emphasises a service arranged, usually for a fee, often with a professional: 'I had my car repaired' (I arranged for someone, a mechanic, to repair it — I didn't do it myself). 'Get something done' means essentially the same thing but is slightly more informal and sometimes implies a bit more personal effort or persuasion involved in making it happen: 'I got my hair cut' or 'I need to get this fixed.' Both follow the same structural pattern: subject + have/get + object + past participle. Compare this to a similar-looking but different structure, 'have someone do something' (with a bare infinitive, not a past participle), which describes directly instructing or asking a specific person to do something, rather than describing a general arranged service: 'I had the mechanic check the brakes' (I directly asked/instructed the mechanic — note the bare infinitive 'check', and the mechanic is explicitly named) versus 'I had the brakes checked' (a more general, passive-focused statement about the service being arranged, without naming who did it).", "illustration": "🛠️ CAUSATIVE — ARRANGING FOR SOMEONE ELSE TO DO IT\nHAVE/GET + OBJECT + PAST PARTICIPLE  (service arranged, doer often unnamed)\n  \"I had my car repaired.\"     (a mechanic did it — I arranged it)\n  \"I got my hair cut.\"          (a hairdresser did it — I arranged it)\n\nCOMPARE: HAVE + PERSON + BASE VERB  (directly instructing a named person)\n  \"I had the mechanic check the brakes.\"   ← 'check' = bare infinitive, mechanic named", "steps": ["Identify that someone else performs the action for the subject, not the subject themselves.", "Use have/get + object + past participle (I had my car repaired, I got my hair cut).", "Choose 'have' for a slightly more neutral/formal tone and 'get' for a more casual tone — both are correct.", "Add 'by + agent' only if it's important to specify exactly who did the service (I had my car repaired by a local mechanic).", "Don't confuse this with the active structure where the subject does the action themselves (I repaired my car myself)."], "rule": "Causative structures with have or get describe an action performed by someone else on the subject's behalf, rather than by the subject personally. The pattern is have/get + object + past participle. 'Get' is generally more informal than 'have', and both can optionally add 'by + agent' to specify who performed the action.", "structure": "Subject + have/get + object + past participle", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. have/get + object + past participle", "definition": "The core causative pattern: the object comes first, followed by the past participle of the action performed on it by someone else.", "example": "\"I'm having my kitchen renovated.\""}, {"term": "2. 'have' vs 'get'", "definition": "Have is the more neutral, common choice; get is more informal and can also add a sense of effort or persuasion in arranging the action.", "example": "\"He got his suit tailored for the wedding.\""}, {"term": "3. Adding the agent with 'by'", "definition": "An optional 'by + agent' phrase can specify exactly who performed the action, when that detail matters.", "example": "\"We had the contract reviewed by a lawyer.\""}, {"term": "4. Causative vs simple active voice", "definition": "A causative structure signals that someone else performed the action for the subject, unlike a simple active sentence which implies the subject did it personally.", "example": "\"I had my hair cut\" (a hairdresser did it) vs \"I cut my hair\" (I did it myself)."}], "examples": ["I'm having my kitchen renovated next month.", "He got his suit tailored for the wedding.", "We had the contract reviewed by a lawyer."], "mistakes": ["Using the base form instead of the past participle, e.g. 'I had my car fix' instead of 'I had my car fixed'.", "Confusing the causative with a simple active sentence, e.g. saying 'I cut my hair' when actually a hairdresser did it — 'I had my hair cut' is more accurate.", "Placing the past participle before the object instead of after it, e.g. 'I had fixed my car' (wrong structure) instead of 'I had my car fixed'."], "tip": "If someone else performed the action for the subject, the verb after the object must be in the past-participle form, not the base form.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct causative sentence.", "options": ["I cut my hair yesterday at the salon by the stylist.", "I had my hair cut yesterday.", "I had my hair cutting yesterday.", "I had cut my hair yesterday by."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence.", "options": ["She is getting her car repair.", "She is getting her car repaired.", "She is getting repaired her car.", "She is get her car repaired."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g49", "day": 49, "level": "B2", "title": "Wish and If Only", "summary": "Express regrets and desires about present, past and future situations.", "plainExplanation": "'Wish' and 'if only' both express regret or a desire for something to be different from reality, and both pair with specific past-tense verb forms to signal that the wish is contrary to how things actually are, even though the meaning is often about the present or future, not literally the past. For an unreal present situation, use wish/if only + past simple: 'I wish I had more free time' (I don't have more free time right now, and I want that to be different). For an unreal past situation — regret about something that already happened and can't be undone — use wish/if only + past perfect: 'I wish I had studied harder' (I didn't study harder, and now it's too late to change that). For wishing someone else, or something, would change a present behaviour that's currently annoying or frustrating you, use wish + would + base verb: 'I wish it would stop raining' or 'I wish you would listen to me' (implying frustration with a current, ongoing, repeated pattern). 'If only' is essentially a more emphatic, more emotionally intense version of 'wish', following the exact same grammatical rules but conveying stronger, often more urgent or heartfelt regret.", "illustration": "💫 WISH / IF ONLY — REGRET, PAIRED WITH 'UNREAL' PAST TENSES\nUNREAL PRESENT:  wish/if only + PAST SIMPLE\n  \"I wish I had more free time.\"      (don't have it now)\n\nUNREAL PAST (regret, too late):  wish/if only + PAST PERFECT\n  \"I wish I had studied harder.\"      (didn't study — can't change it now)\n\nFRUSTRATION at someone's current behaviour:  wish + WOULD + base verb\n  \"I wish it would stop raining.\"     \"I wish you would listen to me.\"\n\n'IF ONLY' = same rules, more emotional intensity", "steps": ["Identify the time you're wishing were different: now, the past, or an annoying ongoing situation.", "For a present wish, use wish/if only + past simple (I wish I had more time).", "For a past regret, use wish/if only + past perfect (I wish I had studied harder).", "For wishing someone would change an annoying behaviour, use wish + would + base verb (I wish you would stop interrupting).", "Use 'if only' for a slightly stronger, more emotional version of the same structures as 'wish'."], "rule": "'Wish' and 'if only' both express that a situation is different from what the speaker wants, but the verb form depends on the time frame. Past simple after wish/if only expresses a present wish (I wish I knew); past perfect expresses a past regret (I wish I had known); and 'would' + base verb expresses annoyance at someone else's present behaviour or a situation that resists change (I wish it would stop raining).", "structure": "wish/if only + past simple (present wish) | + past perfect (past regret) | + would + base (annoyance)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. wish + past simple — present wish", "definition": "Describes a wish about a current situation that is not true, using the same past-simple form as a second conditional.", "example": "\"I wish I lived closer to my family.\""}, {"term": "2. wish + past perfect — past regret", "definition": "Describes regret about something in the past that cannot be changed, shifting one step further back than the present-wish form.", "example": "\"If only we had left earlier, we wouldn't have missed the start.\""}, {"term": "3. wish + would — annoyance at someone else", "definition": "Expresses irritation at another person's ongoing behaviour or a situation that resists change, not the speaker's own state.", "example": "\"I wish my neighbours would turn down their music.\""}, {"term": "4. 'if only' as a stronger alternative to 'wish'", "definition": "If only follows the same tense rules as wish but expresses the feeling more emphatically, often with a sense of genuine regret.", "example": "\"If only I hadn't sold my car.\""}], "examples": ["I wish I lived closer to my family.", "If only we had left earlier, we wouldn't have missed the start.", "I wish my neighbours would turn down their music."], "mistakes": ["Using present tense after wish for a present wish, e.g. 'I wish I have more time' instead of 'I wish I had more time'.", "Using 'would' to wish for a change in your own state, e.g. 'I wish I would be taller' instead of the correct 'I wish I were taller'.", "Using past simple instead of past perfect for a past regret, e.g. 'I wish I studied harder' (ambiguous) instead of 'I wish I had studied harder'."], "tip": "'Would' after wish is almost always about someone/something else's behaviour, not the speaker's own state — for your own present situation, use the past simple.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence expressing a past regret.", "options": ["I wish I study harder.", "I wish I studied harder.", "I wish I had studied harder.", "I wish I would study harder."], "answer": 2}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence expressing annoyance at someone else's habit.", "options": ["I wish he stops talking loudly.", "I wish he stopped talking loudly.", "I wish he would stop talking loudly.", "I wish he had stopped talking loudly."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g50", "day": 50, "level": "B2", "title": "Emphatic Structures with Do/Does/Did", "summary": "Add emphasis to a verb using the auxiliary do.", "plainExplanation": "Emphatic do/does/did adds extra stress and emotional force to a positive statement, used specifically to contradict a doubt, correct a misunderstanding, or add genuine emphasis — it's not needed grammatically (a plain positive sentence works fine on its own), but it adds a layer of insistence. Structure: do/does/did + base verb, exactly like the auxiliary used in negatives and questions, but here it appears in an otherwise positive, non-negative, non-question sentence purely for emphasis: 'I do like your idea' (stressing 'do' to counter an assumption that you don't), 'She does work hard' (countering someone's doubt that she's lazy), 'He did call, I promise' (insisting this happened, perhaps because someone claimed he didn't). In natural spoken English, the emphatic auxiliary is pronounced with heavy stress, which is really the whole point of the structure — in writing, italics or the surrounding context often signal that same emphasis. This structure is especially common in contexts of disagreement, reassurance, or correction: 'You never listen to me!' 'I do listen to you!' The main verb, just as in negatives and questions, stays in its plain base form, since do/does/did alone is carrying the tense.", "illustration": "💥 EMPHATIC DO/DOES/DID — INSIST, CORRECT, REASSURE\ndo/does/did + BASE VERB (in a POSITIVE sentence, for extra force)\n\n  \"I do like your idea.\"        (countering an assumed doubt)\n  \"She does work hard.\"          (correcting a wrong assumption)\n  \"He did call, I promise.\"      (insisting something really happened)\n\nTypical context: \"You never listen to me!\"  →  \"I DO listen to you!\"\n(spoken with heavy stress on do/does/did)", "steps": ["Identify the verb you want to emphasise.", "Insert do/does/did directly before the base form of that verb (I do like it, She does understand, We did finish).", "Match do/does/did to the correct tense and subject, just as with negatives and questions.", "Use this structure especially to contradict a previous negative statement (You don't like it. — I do like it!).", "Add stress in speech on the emphatic do/does/did to make the contrast clear to a listener."], "rule": "Emphatic 'do/does/did' adds stress to an affirmative sentence, often to contradict a doubt, correct a misunderstanding, or simply emphasise sincerity. The auxiliary carries the tense while the main verb returns to its base form, similar to negative and question formation.", "structure": "Subject + do/does/did + base verb", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Emphatic 'do' in the present", "definition": "Do/does adds stress to a present-simple affirmative sentence, with the main verb returning to its base form.", "example": "\"She does know the answer.\""}, {"term": "2. Emphatic 'did' in the past", "definition": "Did adds stress to a past-simple affirmative sentence in the same way, and the main verb again stays in the base form.", "example": "\"We did warn them about the risks.\""}, {"term": "3. Contradicting a doubt", "definition": "Emphatic do is especially natural when directly countering something the listener doubted or denied.", "example": "\"I do appreciate your help, even if I didn't say so at the time.\""}, {"term": "4. Spoken stress as the cue", "definition": "In speech, the auxiliary itself carries extra stress; in writing, the word order alone signals the emphasis.", "example": "\"I DO agree with you.\" (stress on 'do')"}], "examples": ["I do appreciate your help, even if I didn't say so at the time.", "She does know the answer; she's just shy about speaking up.", "We did warn them about the risks in advance."], "mistakes": ["Keeping the main verb in its inflected form alongside the auxiliary, e.g. 'I do loves this song' instead of 'I do love this song'.", "Using emphatic do in a context where no emphasis or contrast is intended, which can sound unnatural or overly insistent.", "Forgetting to shift the main verb back to base form in the past, e.g. 'He did tried' instead of 'He did try'."], "tip": "Emphatic 'do' works best when contradicting doubt or correcting a misunderstanding — read the sentence aloud with stress on 'do/does/did' to check it fits.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the emphatic sentence.", "options": ["I like this plan.", "I do like this plan.", "I liked this plan.", "I am like this plan."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct emphatic past sentence.", "options": ["She did completed the project.", "She did complete the project.", "She does completed the project.", "She completed did the project."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g51", "day": 51, "level": "B2", "title": "Word Formation: Prefixes and Suffixes", "summary": "Build and adapt vocabulary using common word parts.", "plainExplanation": "Prefixes attach to the beginning of a word and typically change its meaning (often to a negative or opposite meaning), while suffixes attach to the end and typically change its grammatical category (turning a verb into a noun, an adjective into an adverb, and so on) — together, understanding both lets you decode unfamiliar words and build your vocabulary far more efficiently than memorising every word individually. Common negative prefixes include un- (unhappy, unable), dis- (disagree, dishonest), in-/im- (inappropriate, impossible — im- appears before words starting with m, p, or b), and non- (nonsense, non-existent) — there's no single reliable rule for which prefix goes with which word, so pairing (like collocations) often needs to be learned individually, though patterns do exist. Common noun-forming suffixes include -tion/-sion (decision, action), -ment (development, agreement), -ness (happiness, kindness), and -ity (creativity, ability); common adjective-forming suffixes include -ful (helpful, careful), -less (hopeless, careless — note these are near-opposites of each other despite the same base word), -able/-ible (readable, flexible), and -ous (dangerous, famous); the adverb-forming suffix -ly typically attaches to an adjective to describe how an action is performed (quickly, carefully). Recognising these patterns lets you often guess both the meaning and the part of speech of an unfamiliar word just by examining its structure.", "illustration": "🧱 PREFIXES (meaning) + SUFFIXES (word type) = WORD BUILDING BLOCKS\nNEGATIVE PREFIXES:  un- · dis- · in-/im- · non-\n  happy → unhappy   |   agree → disagree   |   possible → impossible\n\nNOUN SUFFIXES:      -tion/-sion · -ment · -ness · -ity\n  decide → decision   |   develop → development   |   happy → happiness\n\nADJECTIVE SUFFIXES:  -ful · -less · -able/-ible · -ous\n  help → helpful   |   hope → hopeless   |   read → readable\n\nADVERB SUFFIX:       -ly   →  quick → quickly", "steps": ["Identify the base word and what change in meaning or part of speech you need.", "Add a prefix to change meaning: un-/dis- (opposite: happy → unhappy), re- (again: do → redo), mis- (wrongly: understand → misunderstand).", "Add a suffix to change part of speech: -tion/-ment (verb → noun: decide → decision), -ful/-less (noun → adjective: care → careful/careless), -ly (adjective → adverb: quick → quickly).", "Watch for spelling changes when adding suffixes (happy → happiness, not happyness).", "Build word families deliberately — learn the noun, verb, adjective, and adverb forms of key vocabulary together."], "rule": "Word formation lets you expand vocabulary systematically by adding prefixes (which usually change meaning, e.g. un-, re-, mis-, dis-) or suffixes (which usually change word class, e.g. -tion/-ment for nouns, -ful/-less for adjectives, -ly for adverbs, -ise/-ify for verbs). Recognising these patterns helps with both understanding and producing new vocabulary.", "structure": "prefix + base word | base word + suffix", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Prefixes that reverse meaning", "definition": "Prefixes like un-, dis- and in- typically create the opposite meaning of the base word.", "example": "\"happy\" → \"unhappy\""}, {"term": "2. Prefixes that add 'wrongly' or 'again'", "definition": "Mis- typically means 'wrongly' or 'badly', while re- typically means 'again' or 'back'.", "example": "\"understand\" → \"misunderstand\"; \"build\" → \"rebuild\""}, {"term": "3. Suffixes that form nouns", "definition": "Suffixes like -tion, -ment and -ness convert a verb or adjective into its noun form.", "example": "\"develop\" → \"development\""}, {"term": "4. Suffixes that form adjectives and adverbs", "definition": "Suffixes like -ful and -less form adjectives, often with opposite meanings, while -ly typically converts an adjective into an adverb.", "example": "\"care\" → \"careful\" / \"careless\"; \"quiet\" → \"quietly\""}], "examples": ["The manager's decision was widely criticised as short-sighted.", "The company aims to simplify its outdated procedures.", "His careless attitude toward deadlines caused ongoing frustration."], "mistakes": ["Attaching a prefix or suffix that doesn't actually combine with that particular base word, producing a non-existent word.", "Confusing similar prefixes with different meanings, e.g. using 'dis-' where 'mis-' is needed, or vice versa.", "Doubling a negative prefix unnecessarily, e.g. 'un-disorganised' instead of simply 'disorganised' or 'unorganised'."], "tip": "When you learn a new word, also learn its common related forms (noun, verb, adjective, adverb) — this builds a much larger vocabulary from a single root.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which prefix means 'again'?", "options": ["un-", "re-", "dis-", "mis-"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which suffix typically forms a noun?", "options": ["-ful", "-less", "-tion", "-ly"], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g52", "day": 52, "level": "C1", "title": "Discourse Markers for Spoken Fluency", "summary": "Use natural spoken connectors to organise ideas and manage conversation.", "plainExplanation": "Discourse markers are small words and phrases that organise spoken conversation and signal what a speaker is about to do — change topic, add a thought, buy thinking time, or wrap up an idea — and using them naturally (rather than dead silence or an overused 'um') is one of the clearest signs of genuine spoken fluency. 'Well' often signals a thoughtful pause or a slightly hesitant, softened response ('Well, I'm not sure that's right'). 'Actually' signals a correction or a mild contrast with what was just said or assumed ('Actually, I think it's the other way around'). 'You know' checks for shared understanding or buys a moment to think ('It's, you know, complicated'). 'Anyway' signals a return to a previous topic after a digression, or a shift to wrap things up ('Anyway, back to what I was saying'). 'I mean' introduces a clarification or rephrasing of what was just said ('I mean, it's not that bad'). These words carry very little literal meaning on their own — their real job is entirely functional, managing the flow and structure of live conversation — which is why they're largely absent from formal written English but essential for sounding natural, relaxed, and fluent in spoken English.", "illustration": "🗣️ DISCOURSE MARKERS — MANAGE THE FLOW OF SPEECH\nWELL       → thoughtful pause, softened response   \"Well, I'm not sure.\"\nACTUALLY   → gentle correction or contrast          \"Actually, it's the opposite.\"\nYOU KNOW   → checking shared understanding          \"It's, you know, complicated.\"\nANYWAY     → returning to topic / wrapping up        \"Anyway, back to my point...\"\nI MEAN     → clarifying or rephrasing                \"I mean, it's not that bad.\"\n\n💡 Almost no literal meaning — their job is purely to organise the conversation.", "steps": ["Use 'well' to pause, soften a response, or introduce a slightly different opinion.", "Use 'so' to introduce a conclusion, a new topic, or a story.", "Use 'actually' to correct information or introduce a surprising fact politely.", "Use 'I mean' to clarify or rephrase something you just said.", "Use these markers naturally and sparingly in speech — overusing any single one can sound like a verbal tic rather than fluent speech."], "rule": "Discourse markers are words and short phrases that organise spoken language, manage turn-taking, and soften statements, without adding core grammatical or factual content. Common examples include well (to begin or hedge a response), anyway (to return to or change topic), I mean (to clarify), you know (to check shared understanding), and actually (to introduce a correction or contrast).", "structure": "discourse marker + clause", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. 'well' to begin or hedge a response", "definition": "Well often opens a spoken answer, signalling a pause for thought or a slightly cautious response.", "example": "\"Well, that's one way to look at it.\""}, {"term": "2. 'anyway' to return to or change topic", "definition": "Anyway signals a shift back to a previous topic or a move on to a new one after a digression.", "example": "\"Anyway, back to what we were discussing.\""}, {"term": "3. 'I mean' to clarify", "definition": "I mean introduces a rephrasing or clarification of what the speaker just said.", "example": "\"I mean, it's fine, but it's not exactly what I expected.\""}, {"term": "4. 'you know' to check shared understanding", "definition": "You know softens a statement and checks that the listener follows or agrees, without adding new factual content.", "example": "\"It's just not ideal, you know?\""}], "examples": ["Well, that's one way to look at it.", "Anyway, back to what we were discussing.", "I mean, it's fine, but it's not exactly what I expected."], "mistakes": ["Overusing discourse markers to the point that speech sounds unfocused or hesitant.", "Using formal written connectors (furthermore, notwithstanding) in casual spoken contexts, which can sound unnaturally stiff.", "Relying on a single discourse marker (like 'like' or 'so') so heavily that it becomes a distracting verbal habit rather than a natural connector."], "tip": "Discourse markers are for spoken or very informal written English — match them to context, and don't substitute them for formal connectors in academic writing.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which discourse marker is typical of informal spoken English?", "options": ["Furthermore", "You know", "Nonetheless", "Notwithstanding"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which sentence uses a discourse marker to soften a statement?", "options": ["The plan failed completely.", "I mean, the plan didn't quite go as expected.", "The plan is a total failure.", "The plan failed, full stop."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g53", "day": 53, "level": "C1", "title": "Register: Formal vs Informal Grammar Choices", "summary": "Adapt grammatical choices to suit formal and informal contexts.", "plainExplanation": "Register is the level of formality a speaker or writer chooses, and English offers genuinely different grammatical structures — not just different vocabulary — for formal versus informal contexts, so switching register correctly means adjusting your grammar, not just swapping a few words. Formal register favours full forms over contractions ('do not' instead of 'don't'), passive voice for objectivity ('The report was submitted' instead of 'I submitted the report'), formal connectors ('however', 'furthermore' instead of 'but', 'also'), and more complex, subordinated sentence structures. Informal register favours contractions ('I'm', 'don't', 'can't'), active voice and direct personal pronouns, phrasal verbs over their more formal single-word equivalents ('find out' instead of 'discover', 'put off' instead of 'postpone'), and shorter, more loosely coordinated sentences joined with 'and' or 'so'. Choosing the wrong register for a context is a real communication problem, not just a stylistic preference — an email to a close colleague written in stiff, over-formal academic register can feel cold or distant, while a formal report or cover letter written in casual, contraction-heavy language can seem unprofessional or careless, regardless of how grammatically correct each sentence is on its own.", "illustration": "🎭 REGISTER — SAME MEANING, DIFFERENT GRAMMATICAL CHOICES\n                   FORMAL                    INFORMAL\nContractions   →  do not, cannot        →  don't, can't\nVoice          →  The report was sent   →  I sent the report\nVocabulary     →  discover, postpone    →  find out, put off\nConnectors     →  however, furthermore  →  but, also\nSentences      →  complex, subordinated →  short, joined with 'and'/'so'\n\n⚠️ Wrong register ≠ wrong grammar, but it can still damage communication.", "steps": ["Identify your audience and purpose — a friend, a colleague, or a formal report each require different register.", "In formal register, avoid contractions (do not instead of don't) and phrasal verbs (investigate instead of look into).", "In informal register, contractions and phrasal verbs are natural and expected (I'll check it out).", "Use passive voice and nominalisation more in formal writing; use active voice and simple verbs more in informal speech.", "Stay consistent — mixing very formal and very informal grammar in the same piece of writing can sound jarring or unintentional."], "rule": "Register refers to how formal or informal language is, and this affects grammar choices as much as vocabulary. Formal register typically uses full forms (do not instead of don't), passive voice, longer sentences, and formal connectors, and avoids phrasal verbs and casual discourse markers. Informal register favours contractions, active voice, phrasal verbs, and a more conversational structure.", "structure": "context → choice of vocabulary, contraction, voice and connector", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Full forms vs contractions", "definition": "Formal register uses full forms (do not, cannot); informal register favours contractions (don't, can't).", "example": "\"We regret to inform you...\" (formal) vs \"Sorry, but...\" (informal)"}, {"term": "2. Passive vs active voice", "definition": "Formal writing leans on passive voice to sound objective; informal writing prefers direct, active voice.", "example": "\"The proposal was rejected.\" (formal) vs \"We rejected the proposal.\" (informal)"}, {"term": "3. Formal vs phrasal vocabulary", "definition": "Formal register favours single-word Latinate verbs; informal register favours everyday phrasal verbs.", "example": "\"investigate\" (formal) vs \"look into\" (informal)"}, {"term": "4. Consistency across a whole text", "definition": "Once a register is chosen, every grammar choice throughout the piece — contractions, passive voice, connectors — should match it consistently.", "example": "A formal email uses 'do not hesitate to contact us' throughout, not mixed with 'just let us know'."}], "examples": ["Formal: The board has decided not to proceed with the proposal.", "Informal: The board decided not to go ahead with it.", "Formal: Please do not hesitate to contact us should you have any questions."], "mistakes": ["Mixing registers within the same piece of writing, e.g. using contractions in an otherwise formal report.", "Using overly formal language in casual contexts, which can sound stiff or even sarcastic.", "Switching from passive to active voice inconsistently within a single formal document, undermining its objective tone."], "tip": "Before writing, decide on the register the whole piece needs, then keep grammar choices (contractions, passive voice, connectors) consistent with that choice throughout.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence is more formal?", "options": ["We looked into the problem.", "We investigated the problem.", "We checked out the problem.", "We looked at the problem."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which feature is typical of informal register?", "options": ["Passive voice", "Contractions and phrasal verbs", "Formal connectors like 'furthermore'", "Avoiding contractions"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g54", "day": 54, "level": "C2", "title": "Mastery: Integrating Advanced Structures Naturally", "summary": "Blend advanced grammar seamlessly and appropriately across registers.", "plainExplanation": "At the mastery (C2) level, the goal shifts from learning new grammar rules to blending everything you've already learned so fluidly that no single structure calls attention to itself — advanced structures like inversion, cleft sentences, nominalisation, and sophisticated hedging should appear naturally, woven into ordinary communication, rather than as showy, deliberate insertions that feel like a student performing a grammar exercise. A C2 speaker or writer chooses the right structure for genuine communicative effect at exactly the right moment: using inversion for real emphasis when a point truly deserves it, reaching for a cleft sentence only when there's a genuine contrast worth spotlighting, and varying sentence length and complexity to control pacing and rhythm rather than defaulting to maximum complexity everywhere, which would actually be a sign of over-eagerness rather than true mastery. This level of natural integration only comes through extensive exposure to real, authentic English — reading widely, listening actively, and noticing how skilled native writers and speakers deploy advanced structures sparingly and purposefully rather than constantly — combined with genuine practice producing your own English in situations where you're focused on the message, not on performing a specific grammar point.", "illustration": "🎼 C2 MASTERY — GRAMMAR AS INVISIBLE TOOL, NOT PERFORMANCE\nLower levels:  learn a structure → practise it deliberately → use it correctly\nC2 mastery:    the RIGHT structure appears naturally, at the RIGHT moment,\n               for a GENUINE communicative reason — never for show\n\n💡 The test: does an advanced structure serve the message, or is it just\n   there to demonstrate that you know it?", "steps": ["Review a piece of your own writing and identify where you default to simple structures out of habit rather than choice.", "Deliberately vary sentence length and structure — mix simple, compound, and complex sentences for natural rhythm.", "Layer in advanced structures (inversion, cleft sentences, participle clauses) only where they genuinely add clarity or emphasis, not just to look impressive.", "Check tense and register consistency across a whole passage, not just sentence by sentence.", "Read your work aloud — if a structure feels forced or unnatural when spoken, revise it toward simpler, more natural phrasing."], "rule": "True grammatical mastery is not about maximising complexity in every sentence, but about selecting the structure that best serves the purpose, audience, and register of a piece of writing or speech. Skilled writers move fluidly between simple and complex structures, using advanced grammar (inversion, cleft sentences, nominalisation, hedging) only where it genuinely adds clarity or emphasis.", "structure": "purpose + audience + register → naturally selected structure", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Selecting structure by purpose", "definition": "Mature writing chooses the grammatical structure that best fits the specific point being made, not the most impressive-sounding option.", "example": "A short, simple sentence for a key takeaway; a complex one for a nuanced point."}, {"term": "2. Fluid movement between simple and complex", "definition": "Skilled writers alternate naturally between short and long, simple and complex sentences rather than defaulting to one style throughout.", "example": "\"The proposal, though promising, still requires further testing.\" followed by a short one: \"It is not yet ready.\""}, {"term": "3. Advanced grammar only where it adds value", "definition": "Inversion, cleft sentences and nominalisation are used only when they genuinely sharpen clarity or emphasis, not simply to show range.", "example": "\"What made the difference wasn't talent, but consistency.\""}, {"term": "4. Matching structure to register", "definition": "Even advanced structures must still fit the expected formality of the context — a highly formal structure can feel out of place in casual writing.", "example": "Reserving inversion for formal essays, not casual emails."}], "examples": ["Had the team communicated earlier, the misunderstanding could have been avoided.", "What made the difference wasn't talent, but consistency.", "The proposal, though promising, still requires further testing."], "mistakes": ["Stacking multiple advanced structures into a single sentence just to demonstrate range, which reduces clarity and can sound unnatural.", "Applying highly formal or academic structures in contexts (casual conversation, informal email) where they don't fit the expected register.", "Avoiding all complex structures out of caution, which limits expressiveness just as much as overusing them does."], "tip": "Ask whether an advanced structure genuinely improves clarity or emphasis in this specific sentence — if not, a simpler structure is usually the better, more mature choice.", "quiz": [{"q": "What best defines grammatical mastery at this level?", "options": ["Using the most complex structure in every sentence", "Choosing the structure that best fits purpose, audience and register", "Avoiding all simple sentences", "Using passive voice exclusively"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which sentence shows natural, purposeful use of an advanced structure rather than forced complexity?", "options": ["Rarely, extremely, undoubtedly, and arguably, the plan worked perfectly in every single conceivable way.", "What ultimately determined the result was not funding, but timing.", "The the plan worked was arguably rarely undoubtedly good.", "Very very complex structures are always better in every sentence."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g55", "day": 55, "level": "B2", "title": "IELTS/TOEFL Task 2: Grammar for Opinion Essays", "summary": "Use the grammatical patterns examiners expect in opinion and argumentative essays.", "plainExplanation": "IELTS and TOEFL Task 2 opinion essays are scored partly on 'grammatical range and accuracy' — meaning examiners are specifically looking for a genuine mix of simple and complex sentence structures used correctly, not just one repeated pattern, however correct that pattern might be. Strong opinion essays typically combine several grammar tools: complex sentences with subordination to show cause-effect and contrast ('Although some argue X, the evidence suggests Y'), a clear, consistent stance expressed through appropriate hedging language rather than absolute claims ('This is likely to result in...' rather than the riskier 'This will definitely result in...'), cohesive linking devices to connect paragraphs and ideas smoothly (moreover, consequently, in contrast), and varied sentence openings rather than starting every sentence with 'I think' or the essay's subject noun repeatedly. A common and costly mistake is relying entirely on short, simple sentences throughout, which caps the achievable score regardless of how accurate those simple sentences are — examiners are explicitly assessing range, not just correctness, so a essay entirely built from short, safe, but grammatically simple sentences will score lower than one that attempts (and mostly succeeds at) more complex structures, even if the more ambitious essay contains a few minor slips.", "illustration": "📝 IELTS/TOEFL TASK 2 — EXAMINERS SCORE 'RANGE', NOT JUST ACCURACY\n✅ Mix simple AND complex sentences (subordination, conditionals, relative clauses)\n✅ Hedge claims appropriately: \"is likely to\" not \"will definitely\"\n✅ Use varied linking words: moreover · consequently · in contrast\n✅ Vary sentence openings — don't start every sentence with \"I think\"\n\n❌ All short, simple sentences = capped score, even if 100% error-free", "steps": ["State your opinion clearly using confident but appropriately hedged language (I believe that..., It seems clear that...).", "Use a range of complex sentences (relative clauses, adverb clauses) rather than only short simple sentences, to demonstrate grammatical range.", "Support your opinion with conditional sentences to explore hypothetical consequences (If this policy were adopted, ...).", "Use cohesive linking words to connect paragraphs and ideas logically (Furthermore, However, As a result).", "Proofread specifically for subject-verb agreement and article errors, which are heavily penalised in exam scoring."], "rule": "IELTS/TOEFL opinion (Task 2) essays are graded partly on grammatical range and accuracy, which examiners assess through the structures used to state and defend a position. A strong essay opens with a clear, specific thesis (not a vague restatement of the question), develops ideas using complex sentences with subordination, and evaluates claims with appropriate hedging rather than absolute statements.", "structure": "Thesis statement + reason clause + example + hedged evaluation", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. A clear, specific thesis", "definition": "The opening should state a precise position on the question, not a vague restatement of the topic.", "example": "\"This essay will argue that investing in early education produces the greatest long-term social benefit.\""}, {"term": "2. Complex sentences with subordination", "definition": "Ideas are developed using subordinators (although, because, while) rather than only short, disconnected simple sentences.", "example": "\"Although critics highlight the initial costs, the long-term benefits are likely to outweigh them.\""}, {"term": "3. Hedged evaluation", "definition": "Claims are presented with appropriate caution rather than as absolute, unqualified statements.", "example": "\"This policy tends to be effective, although exceptions clearly exist.\""}, {"term": "4. A signalled, balanced conclusion", "definition": "The conclusion uses a clear formal marker, briefly acknowledges the other side, and restates the writer's final position.", "example": "\"In conclusion, while both perspectives have merit, the evidence favours stronger regulation.\""}], "examples": ["This essay will argue that public investment in renewable energy should be prioritised over short-term subsidies.", "Although critics highlight the initial costs, the long-term environmental benefits are likely to outweigh them.", "It could be argued that a balanced approach, combining both strategies, offers the most sustainable outcome."], "mistakes": ["Writing a vague or overly general thesis statement that doesn't commit to a specific position, which examiners penalise for lack of clarity.", "Using only simple sentences throughout the essay, or only very long, unpunctuated sentences, both of which limit the grammatical range score.", "Ending the essay without a clear, formally signalled conclusion, leaving the argument feeling unresolved."], "tip": "Before writing, decide your exact position in one specific sentence — this becomes your thesis, and everything else in the essay should support or nuance it.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence is a strong IELTS/TOEFL-style thesis statement?", "options": ["Some people think one thing, some people think another thing.", "This essay will argue that investing in public transport is the most effective solution to urban congestion.", "I don't really know what to think about this topic.", "There are many opinions about many topics in the world."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which sentence shows the balanced, hedged evaluation examiners reward?", "options": ["This is definitely the only correct answer.", "This may be one of the more effective approaches, although exceptions exist.", "Everyone agrees with this completely.", "This is obviously true for every single case."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g56", "day": 56, "level": "B2", "title": "Exam Error Correction: Tense Consistency", "summary": "Identify and correct common tense-consistency errors under exam conditions.", "plainExplanation": "Tense consistency errors happen when a piece of writing shifts tense without a real reason to do so — jumping between past and present, for instance, in a way that confuses the reader about when events are actually happening. The core principle: once you establish a tense for narrating a sequence of events, stay in that tense unless there's a genuine, logical reason to shift (like moving to an earlier point via past perfect, or referencing a timeless fact that stays in present tense regardless of the surrounding narrative tense). A classic exam error-correction pattern looks like this: 'She walked into the room and sees her friend waiting' — this incorrectly mixes past ('walked') with present ('sees') for two actions that are part of the same single narrative sequence; the correction should keep both actions in past tense: 'She walked into the room and saw her friend waiting.' However, a shift is genuinely correct when the logic calls for it: 'She said that water boils at 100 degrees' correctly keeps 'boils' in present tense despite the past-tense reporting verb 'said', because it's stating a timeless scientific fact that remains true regardless of when it was mentioned. Exam error-correction tasks specifically test whether you can tell the difference between an unjustified, confusing tense shift (an error) and a justified, meaningful one (correct usage).", "illustration": "⏱️ TENSE CONSISTENCY — STAY IN ONE TENSE UNLESS THERE'S A REASON TO SHIFT\n❌ \"She walked into the room and sees her friend waiting.\"\n✅ \"She walked into the room and saw her friend waiting.\"   (same narrative sequence)\n\n✅ JUSTIFIED SHIFT — timeless fact keeps present tense:\n\"She said that water boils at 100 degrees.\"   (said=past, boils=present fact, both correct)\n\n💡 Exam tests: can you spot an UNJUSTIFIED shift vs a JUSTIFIED one?", "steps": ["Read the whole passage first to identify its main tense (usually established in the first sentence).", "Check every verb against that established tense — does it match, or has it shifted without a clear time signal?", "Confirm any tense shift is justified by an explicit time change (a date, 'now', 'later') — not just a random switch.", "Correct any verb that has drifted into the wrong tense so it matches the surrounding context.", "Pay special attention to reported speech and conditional sentences, which have their own internal tense logic separate from the surrounding paragraph."], "rule": "Exam error-correction tasks frequently test tense consistency: whether all verbs in a sentence or short passage correctly reflect the same established time frame, unless a specific word (already, by the time, before) signals a genuine shift. The most common trap is a sentence that starts in one tense and unintentionally drifts into another.", "structure": "Identify the established time frame → keep all related verbs consistent with it, unless a clear time-shift word appears", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Establishing one time frame per sentence", "definition": "All verbs in a sentence should reflect the same established time frame unless a specific signal word marks a genuine shift.", "example": "\"She studied all night and passed the exam.\" (both past simple)"}, {"term": "2. Signal words that justify a shift", "definition": "Words like already, by the time and before can legitimately trigger a tense shift (often to the past perfect) within the same sentence.", "example": "\"By the time the guests arrived, dinner was already ready.\""}, {"term": "3. The most common trap: drifting mid-sentence", "definition": "A sentence that begins in one tense and unintentionally shifts to another, without any signal word justifying the change, is a tense-consistency error.", "example": "Incorrect: \"She left the office and calls a taxi.\" Correct: \"She left the office and called a taxi.\""}, {"term": "4. Sequencing two past events", "definition": "When two past events are both mentioned, the earlier one often needs past perfect to clearly mark it as happening first.", "example": "\"By the time I got home, my sister had already left.\""}], "examples": ["She left the office and calls a taxi. → She left the office and called a taxi.", "While he was studying, his phone rings. → While he was studying, his phone rang.", "By the time I got home, my sister already leaves. → By the time I got home, my sister had already left."], "mistakes": ["Switching from past to present mid-sentence without any signal that time has genuinely changed.", "Forgetting to use past perfect for the earlier of two past events when both are mentioned in the same sentence.", "Treating a specific past time word (yesterday) as compatible with present perfect, producing an inconsistent tense mix."], "tip": "Read the whole sentence first to establish the time frame, then check every verb against that same time frame before moving on.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["He worked all morning and then goes home for lunch.", "He worked all morning and then went home for lunch.", "He works all morning and then went home for lunch.", "He working all morning and then went home for lunch."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["When she arrived, the meeting already starts.", "When she arrives, the meeting had already started.", "When she arrived, the meeting had already started.", "When she arrived, the meeting already start."], "answer": 2}]},
{"id": "g57", "day": 57, "level": "B2", "title": "Exam Error Correction: Subject-Verb Agreement Traps", "summary": "Spot the tricky agreement errors that commonly appear in exam texts.", "plainExplanation": "Subject-verb agreement traps go beyond the basic he/she/it + -s rule and involve situations specifically designed to mislead you about which word is the true grammatical subject. Common traps include: a long phrase inserted between subject and verb, where the noun right before the verb is not actually the subject ('The list of complaints is long' — 'list' is the true singular subject, not the plural 'complaints' sitting right next to the verb); collective nouns that describe a group but are usually treated as singular in most English varieties ('The team is confident', not 'are confident', though British English does sometimes allow plural treatment when emphasising the individual members); indefinite pronouns that sound plural but are grammatically singular ('Everyone has arrived', not 'have arrived'); and subjects joined by 'or'/'nor', where the verb must agree with whichever subject is physically closest to it, not the first one mentioned ('Neither the manager nor the employees were informed' — 'were' agrees with the closer, plural 'employees', not the further, singular 'manager'). Exam error-correction tasks deliberately construct these tricky patterns because they expose whether a learner truly understands agreement, or is just pattern-matching on whatever noun happens to sit closest to the verb.", "illustration": "🪤 SUBJECT-VERB AGREEMENT TRAPS\nLONG PHRASE IN BETWEEN:  \"The list of complaints is long.\"    ← 'list' (sing.) is the real subject\nCOLLECTIVE NOUNS:         \"The team is confident.\"              ← usually singular\nINDEFINITE PRONOUNS:      \"Everyone has arrived.\"                ← sounds plural, IS singular\nOR/NOR — MATCH THE CLOSER SUBJECT:\n  \"Neither the manager nor the employees WERE informed.\"  ← matches 'employees' (closer)\n  \"Neither the employees nor the manager WAS informed.\"   ← matches 'manager' (closer)", "steps": ["Find the true subject of the verb, ignoring any prepositional phrases in between (The list of items is long — 'list' is the subject, not 'items').", "Check for collective nouns (team, family, government) and decide if they're acting as one unit (singular) or as individuals (plural).", "Watch for indefinite pronouns like each, everyone, and neither, which always take a singular verb.", "Check subjects joined by 'or'/'nor' — the verb agrees with the nearer subject.", "Reread the sentence with only the true subject and verb isolated, to check the agreement sounds correct without the distracting phrase."], "rule": "Subject-verb agreement errors in exams are often disguised by a phrase or clause sitting between the true subject and its verb, or by subjects that look plural but are grammatically singular (each, everyone, the number of, collective nouns like team, committee, group). The verb must always agree with the true subject, not the nearest noun.", "structure": "Find the true subject (ignore the words in between) → match the verb to that subject", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Ignoring interrupting phrases", "definition": "A phrase or clause sitting between the subject and the verb does not change what the verb should agree with.", "example": "\"The results of the survey are surprising.\" (subject: results, plural)"}, {"term": "2. Words that look plural but are singular", "definition": "Each, everyone, everybody, nobody and neither are grammatically singular, even though they refer to more than one person.", "example": "\"Neither of the answers is fully correct.\""}, {"term": "3. Collective nouns as one unit", "definition": "Words like team, committee and group are usually treated as singular when acting together as a single unit.", "example": "\"The committee is meeting tomorrow.\""}, {"term": "4. 'The number of' vs 'a number of'", "definition": "'The number of' is treated as singular, while 'a number of' (meaning 'several') is treated as plural.", "example": "\"The number of errors has decreased.\" vs \"A number of errors were found.\""}], "examples": ["The results of the survey are surprising. (subject: results, plural)", "The impact of these changes is still unclear. (subject: impact, singular)", "Neither of the answers is fully correct. (Neither = singular)"], "mistakes": ["Matching the verb to the nearest noun instead of the true subject, e.g. 'The list of names are long' instead of 'is long'.", "Treating 'each', 'everyone', 'neither', or 'the number of' as plural because a plural noun follows them.", "Forgetting that collective nouns can occasionally take a plural verb when members are acting individually rather than as one unit."], "tip": "Mentally cross out any phrase between the subject and the verb, then check agreement using only what remains.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["The box of documents were left on the desk.", "The box of documents was left on the desk.", "The box of documents are left on the desk.", "The box of documents being left on the desk."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["Everyone in the room agree with the plan.", "Everyone in the room agrees with the plan.", "Everyone in the room are agreeing with the plan.", "Everyone in the room have agreed with the plan."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g58", "day": 58, "level": "C1", "title": "IELTS/TOEFL Task 2: Grammar for Discussion and Balanced Essays", "summary": "Present both sides of an argument using precise comparative and contrastive grammar.", "plainExplanation": "IELTS and TOEFL discussion or balanced essays require you to present both sides of an issue fairly before giving your own view, which places specific grammatical demands beyond what a simple one-sided opinion essay needs. You need contrast and concession structures to present the two sides evenhandedly ('On the one hand... on the other hand...', 'While proponents argue X, critics contend Y'), hedged, cautious language throughout to represent each side's position fairly and without exaggeration ('Some believe that... it could be argued that...'), and often a shift to a more assertive, hedged-but-clear personal stance in the conclusion, where you finally state and defend your own view after presenting both sides ('In my view, the evidence more strongly supports...'). A common structural weakness in weaker essays is spending equal grammatical energy and complexity on one side of the argument while treating the other side dismissively or in overly simple language, which examiners read as a lack of genuine balance regardless of what the essay's content claims to be doing — true balance means both sides deserve equally sophisticated grammar and equally serious treatment, even if you ultimately favour one side in your conclusion.", "illustration": "⚖️ DISCUSSION/BALANCED ESSAY — GRAMMAR FOR FAIR TREATMENT\nCONTRAST/CONCESSION:  \"On the one hand... on the other hand...\"\n                      \"While proponents argue X, critics contend Y.\"\nHEDGED, FAIR LANGUAGE for BOTH sides:  \"Some believe...\" / \"It could be argued...\"\nCONCLUSION — your own stance, still hedged but clear:\n  \"In my view, the evidence more strongly supports...\"\n\n⚠️ Both sides need EQUALLY sophisticated grammar — don't shortchange one side.", "steps": ["Present the first viewpoint using neutral, objective language (Some people argue that...).", "Present the opposing viewpoint using contrast conjunctions to transition smoothly (However, others believe that...).", "Use hedging language throughout to maintain a balanced, non-biased tone until your conclusion (This may suggest..., It could be argued...).", "Use comparative structures to weigh the two sides against each other (While X has some merit, Y is arguably more significant because...).", "Save your personal opinion and stronger, more direct language for the concluding paragraph, where a clear stance is expected."], "rule": "Discussion-type Task 2 essays require presenting two viewpoints fairly before reaching a conclusion. This demands specific grammatical signals: 'on the one hand... on the other hand' to frame two balanced sides, 'whereas'/'while' to contrast within a sentence, and clear evaluative language in the conclusion. Overusing 'but' or repeating 'I think' throughout weakens both coherence and grammatical range scores.", "structure": "On the one hand + view A. On the other hand + view B. Overall + balanced conclusion.", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Framing two balanced sides", "definition": "'On the one hand... on the other hand' explicitly signals that both viewpoints will be presented fairly before any conclusion.", "example": "\"On the one hand, urban living offers greater career opportunities; on the other hand, it often comes at the cost of a slower pace of life.\""}, {"term": "2. Contrasting within a single sentence", "definition": "'Whereas' and 'while' let a writer directly contrast two viewpoints inside one sentence rather than across two separate ones.", "example": "\"While some students thrive in large lecture halls, others benefit more from smaller classes.\""}, {"term": "3. Varying contrastive language", "definition": "Using a range of contrast connectors (whereas, in contrast, on the other hand) instead of repeating 'but' keeps the argument grammatically varied.", "example": "\"Supporters emphasise economic growth, whereas critics highlight rising inequality.\""}, {"term": "4. A clear, evaluated conclusion", "definition": "After presenting both sides, the conclusion should weigh them and state a considered final judgement, not just restate that 'there are two views'.", "example": "\"Having considered both sides, it seems reasonable to conclude that a hybrid approach best serves most learners.\""}], "examples": ["On the one hand, urban living offers greater career opportunities; on the other hand, it often comes at the cost of a slower pace of life.", "While some students thrive in large lecture halls, others benefit more from smaller, interactive classes.", "Having considered both sides, it seems reasonable to conclude that a hybrid approach best serves most learners."], "mistakes": ["Presenting only one side of the argument in a 'discuss both views' essay, which directly fails the task requirement.", "Using 'but' repeatedly instead of varying contrastive language (whereas, while, in contrast, on the other hand).", "Repeating 'I think' throughout instead of using more varied, examiner-preferred evaluative language in the conclusion."], "tip": "Plan the essay in three explicit sections before writing: view A, view B, and your evaluated conclusion — this naturally produces the grammar structures examiners expect.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence best signals a balanced discussion structure?", "options": ["I think this and also I think that.", "On the one hand, costs may rise; on the other hand, quality may improve.", "This is true and this is also true and so is this.", "Everything about this topic is complicated and unclear."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which connector is most appropriate for contrasting two viewpoints in one sentence?", "options": ["Similarly", "Whereas", "In addition", "As well as"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g59", "day": 59, "level": "C1", "title": "Exam Error Correction: Article and Preposition Traps", "summary": "Correct the subtle article and preposition errors examiners frequently test.", "plainExplanation": "Article and preposition traps are some of the most persistent error sources even for advanced learners, precisely because these small words often follow convention rather than logic, and no amount of grammatical reasoning alone can substitute for direct exposure and memorisation. Classic article traps include: using 'the' with a general plural or uncountable noun when no article is needed ('the information I need' is fine if specific, but 'The information is valuable' as a general statement about information itself should just be 'Information is valuable'), and omitting 'the' with a genuinely specific, previously identified noun ('I read book' instead of 'I read the book' or 'I read a book'). Classic preposition traps include fixed expressions that simply don't follow logical rules across languages: 'interested in' (not 'interested for' or 'interested on'), 'married to' (not 'married with'), 'good at' (not 'good in'), 'depend on' (not 'depend of' or 'depend from'), and 'arrive at/in' (not 'arrive to' — arrive takes 'at' for smaller places and 'in' for larger areas like cities and countries, but never 'to'). Exam error-correction tasks target exactly these fixed, convention-based patterns because they can't be solved by applying a general rule — they require having actually encountered and absorbed the specific correct combination before.", "illustration": "🪤 ARTICLE & PREPOSITION TRAPS — CONVENTION, NOT LOGIC\nARTICLES:\n  \"Information is valuable.\" (general, NO article)  vs  \"The information I need is here.\" (specific, 'the')\n  \"I read a book.\" / \"I read the book.\"   ← never bare \"I read book.\"\n\nPREPOSITIONS (fixed, must be memorised):\n  interested IN (not for/on)   |   married TO (not with)\n  good AT (not in)              |   depend ON (not of/from)\n  arrive AT/IN (not TO — ever!)", "steps": ["Check every noun for whether it needs a/an, the, or no article at all, based on whether it's specific, general, or first-mentioned.", "Watch for common exceptions: no article before most singular proper nouns, but 'the' before countries with plural names (the Netherlands) or organisations (the United Nations).", "Check every preposition against fixed expressions you've memorised (interested in, good at, married to) rather than translating literally.", "Pay close attention to prepositions after adjectives and verbs, since these combinations are often idiomatic and don't follow a general rule.", "When unsure, recall a memorised example sentence using that exact word, rather than trying to apply a general rule."], "rule": "Articles and prepositions are among the most commonly tested error types because native-language interference often causes learners to add unnecessary articles, drop necessary ones, or use a logical-but-incorrect preposition. Key patterns to memorise: general/abstract nouns (poverty, education, crime) usually take no article; many adjectives and verbs have one fixed, non-negotiable preposition (responsible for, interested in, depend on, capable of).", "structure": "Check: does this noun need a/an/the, or no article at all? → Check: does this verb/adjective pair with a fixed preposition?", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. No article with general/abstract nouns", "definition": "Abstract nouns used in a general sense (poverty, education, crime) normally take no article at all.", "example": "\"Poverty remains a significant challenge.\" (not 'The poverty')"}, {"term": "2. Fixed adjective + preposition pairs", "definition": "Many adjectives have one non-negotiable preposition that must be memorised rather than guessed logically.", "example": "\"responsible for\", \"interested in\", \"capable of\""}, {"term": "3. Fixed verb + preposition pairs", "definition": "Many verbs similarly pair with one fixed preposition, which can differ from what direct translation might suggest.", "example": "\"depend on\", \"consist of\", \"apply for\""}, {"term": "4. Testing for a logical but incorrect substitute", "definition": "A preposition that seems logically reasonable can still be wrong if it isn't the one native speakers conventionally use.", "example": "\"different from\" (standard) rather than \"different than\" or \"different to\" in careful formal writing."}], "examples": ["Poverty remains a significant challenge in many regions. (no article — general concept)", "She is responsible for managing the entire team.", "Success often depends on consistent effort over time."], "mistakes": ["Adding 'the' before an abstract noun used in a general sense, e.g. 'The poverty is a problem' instead of 'Poverty is a problem'.", "Substituting a logical-sounding preposition for the fixed one, e.g. 'responsible of' instead of 'responsible for'.", "Adding an unnecessary article before a general plural noun, e.g. 'the crimes are rising' when a general statement is intended, instead of 'crime is rising'."], "tip": "Build a personal list of adjective/verb + fixed preposition pairs as you encounter them — these must be memorised individually, since they aren't always predictable from logic.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["The success depends of hard work.", "Success depends on hard work.", "The success depend on hard work.", "Success is depend on hard work."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["She is interested about ancient history.", "She is interested for ancient history.", "She is interested on ancient history.", "She is interested in ancient history."], "answer": 3}]},
{"id": "g60", "day": 60, "level": "C1", "title": "Exam Error Correction: Parallel Structure", "summary": "Fix broken parallel structure in lists and comparisons, a frequent high-level exam error.", "plainExplanation": "Parallel structure means that items joined together in a list, comparison, or pair must share the same grammatical form — all nouns, all verbs in the same form, all adjectives, or all matching phrase types — rather than mixing different grammatical categories in the same series. A broken example: 'She enjoys reading, to write, and swimming' mixes a gerund (reading), an infinitive (to write), and another gerund (swimming); the fix is to make all three match: 'She enjoys reading, writing, and swimming.' Parallel structure errors are especially common in lists after a colon, in comparisons using 'than' or 'as', and in correlative pairs like 'not only...but also' and 'either...or', where each half of the pair needs to match the other's grammatical form: 'Not only was she talented, but also hardworking' is broken (a clause vs. an adjective); the fix aligns both halves: 'Not only was she talented, but she was also hardworking' or 'She was not only talented but also hardworking.' This error often escapes notice on a first read because each individual item sounds fine in isolation — the problem only becomes visible when you check that every item in the series follows the identical grammatical pattern as its neighbours.", "illustration": "⚖️ PARALLEL STRUCTURE — MATCH THE GRAMMATICAL FORM ACROSS A LIST\n❌ \"She enjoys reading, to write, and swimming.\"\n     gerund      infinitive    gerund   ← mismatched!\n✅ \"She enjoys reading, writing, and swimming.\"\n     gerund       gerund        gerund   ← all match\n\nCORRELATIVE PAIRS must match too:\n❌ \"Not only was she talented, but also hardworking.\"   (clause vs. adjective)\n✅ \"She was not only talented but also hardworking.\"     (adjective, adjective)", "steps": ["Identify a list, a pair joined by 'and/or', or a comparison in the sentence.", "Check that every item in the list uses the same grammatical form (all gerunds, all infinitives, or all nouns).", "Fix any item that breaks the pattern so it matches the others (I like reading, writing, and to swim → I like reading, writing, and swimming).", "Apply the same check to paired conjunctions like both...and, either...or, not only...but also.", "Read the sentence aloud, focusing only on the listed items, to hear whether the rhythm and form feel consistent."], "rule": "Parallel structure requires that items joined by and, or, or compared with than/as...as all share the same grammatical form — all nouns, all gerunds, or all infinitives, and never a mix. This error is especially common in longer exam sentences where the writer loses track of the original grammatical pattern partway through a list.", "structure": "Identify the joined items → make each item match the same grammatical pattern", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Matching grammatical form across a list", "definition": "Every item joined by and, or, or compared with than/as...as must share the same grammatical form.", "example": "\"clear, concise, and persuasive\" (all adjectives)"}, {"term": "2. All gerunds or all infinitives, never mixed", "definition": "A list of actions must consistently use either the -ing form or the to-infinitive form throughout, not a mix of both.", "example": "\"swimming, running, and cycling\" (not \"to swim, running, and cycling\")"}, {"term": "3. Matching verb forms after paired connectors", "definition": "Structures like 'not only... but also' and 'both... and' require the same grammatical form on each side of the connector.", "example": "\"He not only apologised but also offered compensation.\""}, {"term": "4. Checking substitutability", "definition": "A reliable test for parallel structure is whether any item in the list could grammatically replace any other item without breaking the sentence.", "example": "\"analytical, a strong communicator, and a good team player\" — each item can swap positions and still work."}], "examples": ["The training focuses on planning, organising, and delegating. (all gerunds)", "The report was thorough, accurate, and well-structured. (all adjectives)", "She not only led the project but also mentored two junior colleagues. (both verb phrases match)"], "mistakes": ["Starting a list with one grammatical form and switching partway through, e.g. 'clear, concise, and it flows well' instead of 'clear, concise, and fluent'.", "Mismatching verb forms after paired connectors like 'not only... but also' or 'both... and'.", "Mixing a noun phrase with a full clause in the same list, e.g. 'creativity, patience, and to communicate well' instead of three matching nouns."], "tip": "After writing a list or comparison, go back and check that every single item could grammatically replace any other item in the list without breaking the sentence.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["The course improved my writing, speaking, and to listen.", "The course improved my writing, speaking, and listening.", "The course improved my writing, to speak, and listening.", "The course improved to write, speaking, and listening."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["The manager is organised, reliable, and works hard.", "The manager is organised, reliable, and hardworking.", "The manager organised, is reliable, and hardworking.", "The manager is organise, reliable, and hardworking."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g61", "day": 61, "level": "C1", "title": "IELTS/TOEFL Task 1: Grammar for Data and Trend Description", "summary": "Use precise tense and comparative grammar to describe graphs, charts and processes accurately.", "plainExplanation": "IELTS and TOEFL Task 1 (describing data, charts, graphs, or trends) requires a specific grammatical toolkit that's quite different from the opinion-essay grammar needed for Task 2, since the goal here is precise, objective, factual description rather than argument. Trend vocabulary needs matching grammatical partners: verbs describing change (rise, increase, fall, decline, fluctuate) paired with adverbs of degree and manner (rise sharply, increase gradually, fall dramatically, remain relatively stable) and matching noun forms for the same ideas (a sharp rise, a gradual increase, a dramatic fall). Comparative structures are essential for comparing different categories or time periods within the same data set ('Sales were significantly higher in 2020 than in 2019', 'Category A saw the steepest decline'). Time-based prepositions and clauses situate changes precisely ('between 2015 and 2020', 'from January to March', 'over the following decade'). Passive voice often suits process descriptions (for diagrams showing how something is made or done): 'The raw material is first collected, then transported to the factory.' A very common weakness in Task 1 responses is repeating the same one or two 'change' verbs (usually just 'increase' and 'decrease') throughout the entire description, which caps the vocabulary and grammatical range score — strong responses deliberately vary both the verbs and the sentence structures used to describe similar trends across different parts of the data.", "illustration": "📈 TASK 1 — DESCRIBING DATA & TRENDS\nCHANGE VERBS + ADVERBS:  rise sharply · increase gradually · fall dramatically\nMATCHING NOUN FORMS:     a sharp rise · a gradual increase · a dramatic fall\nCOMPARISON:              \"Sales were significantly higher in 2020 than in 2019.\"\nTIME PRECISION:          between 2015 and 2020 · from January to March\nPASSIVE for PROCESSES:   \"The material is first collected, then transported...\"\n\n⚠️ Don't just repeat 'increase/decrease' throughout — vary verbs AND structures.", "steps": ["Identify the overall trend first (increase, decrease, fluctuation, stability) before describing individual data points.", "Use appropriate trend verbs and their matching nouns (rise/a rise, fall/a fall, fluctuate/a fluctuation).", "Use the past simple for a graph showing completed historical data, and the present simple for a graph showing a general or current-state trend.", "Use comparative and superlative structures to compare specific data points (higher than, the largest increase).", "Use precise adverbs and prepositions to describe the rate and degree of change (sharply, gradually, by 10%, to 50%)."], "rule": "IELTS/TOEFL Task 1 (or Integrated Writing) requires precise grammar to describe visual data or processes objectively. Completed past trends need past-simple trend verbs (rose, fell, peaked, fluctuated) with precise adverbs (sharply, gradually, slightly) and prepositions (by 15%, from X to Y, between 2015 and 2020). General repeated processes use present simple passive, since the focus is on the action, not who performs it.", "structure": "Subject + trend verb (past) + adverb/degree phrase + prepositional phrase (by/from/to)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Past-simple trend verbs for completed trends", "definition": "A specific, completed past trend uses past-simple trend verbs like rose, fell and peaked, since the data describes a finished period.", "example": "\"The population rose steadily before peaking in 2019.\""}, {"term": "2. Precise adverbs and prepositions of degree", "definition": "Trend descriptions pair trend verbs with precise adverbs (sharply, gradually) and exact prepositions (by 15%, from X to Y) to quantify the change.", "example": "\"Exports fell sharply, from $2m to $1.2m, between 2018 and 2020.\""}, {"term": "3. Present simple passive for general processes", "definition": "A repeated, general process (not a one-off past event) is described with present simple passive, focusing on the action rather than the agent.", "example": "\"Raw materials are first collected, then processed, and finally distributed.\""}, {"term": "4. Future forms for projections", "definition": "A predicted future trend uses appropriate future language such as 'is projected to' or 'is expected to', matched consistently throughout the description.", "example": "\"Demand is expected to rise over the next decade.\""}], "examples": ["The population rose steadily before peaking in 2019 and declining thereafter.", "Raw materials are first collected, then processed, and finally distributed to retailers.", "Unemployment is projected to fall gradually over the coming years."], "mistakes": ["Using present tense to describe a specific, completed past trend, e.g. 'Sales rise sharply in 2020' instead of 'Sales rose sharply in 2020'.", "Using active voice inconsistently when describing a general process, losing the objective, procedural tone expected in Task 1 process descriptions.", "Mixing past-trend language with a general-process description in the same sentence, blurring whether the data is a one-off event or a repeated pattern."], "tip": "Identify first whether you're describing a specific past trend (use past simple), a general repeated process (use present simple passive), or a future projection (use appropriate future forms) — then keep that choice consistent throughout.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the correct sentence for describing a completed past trend.", "options": ["Profits raised by 15% last year.", "Profits rose by 15% last year.", "Profits rise by 15% last year.", "Profits rising by 15% last year."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the correct sentence for describing a general process.", "options": ["The mixture is heated to 200 degrees before it is cooled.", "The mixture heats to 200 degrees before it cools it.", "The mixture heating to 200 degrees before cooled.", "The mixture was heat to 200 degrees before cool."], "answer": 0}]},
{"id": "g62", "day": 62, "level": "C1", "title": "Exam Error Correction: Conditional and Modal Slips", "summary": "Correct the mixed-up conditional and modal forms that commonly appear in exam error-spotting tasks.", "plainExplanation": "Conditional and modal slips are a frequent source of exam errors because these structures involve several interacting parts — an 'if' clause, a result clause, and sometimes a modal verb — and getting even one part wrong (mismatched tense, missing 'have', wrong modal choice) breaks the whole sentence's logic. Common slip patterns include: mixing conditional types within a single sentence, using 'would' in the if-clause itself instead of the required plain past or past perfect ('If I would have known, I would have come' instead of the correct 'If I had known, I would have come'), forgetting the extra 'have' in third-conditional result clauses ('I would helped' instead of 'I would have helped'), and confusing deduction modals with obligation modals, especially the negative forms ('You mustn't have taken it' when the intended meaning was the deduction 'You can't have taken it', since 'mustn't' expresses prohibition, not improbability). Exam error-correction tasks at this level often present a conditional or modal sentence with exactly one of these interacting parts altered, testing whether you can trace back through the whole structure to identify precisely which single piece is broken and how the rest of the sentence should logically constrain the fix.", "illustration": "🪤 CONDITIONAL & MODAL SLIPS — ONE WRONG PIECE BREAKS THE LOGIC\n❌ \"If I would have known, I would have come.\"\n✅ \"If I had known, I would have come.\"          (never 'would' in the if-clause)\n\n❌ \"I would helped if I had known.\"\n✅ \"I would have helped if I had known.\"          (don't forget 'have')\n\n❌ \"You mustn't have taken it.\" (meaning: it's unlikely you took it)\n✅ \"You can't have taken it.\"    ('mustn't' = forbidden, not 'unlikely')", "steps": ["Identify the conditional type being used (zero, first, second, or third) based on its intended meaning.", "Check that both clauses use the tense pattern that matches that conditional type consistently.", "Watch for the common error of using 'would' in the if-clause itself — this is almost always incorrect.", "Check every modal verb is followed by the correct verb form: base verb for present/future modals, have + past participle for past modals.", "Reread the sentence's intended meaning (real vs. unreal, present vs. past) and confirm the grammar actually expresses that meaning."], "rule": "Exam error-correction tasks frequently combine conditional and modal mistakes in the same sentence: mismatched conditional tense pairings (using 'will' in an if-clause, or 'would' instead of 'will' in a first-conditional result), and modals incorrectly followed by 'to' or an inflected verb form. Modals (can, could, must, should, would, might) are always followed directly by the base verb.", "structure": "if + correct tense pairing | modal + base verb (never modal + to + verb)", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. First conditional tense pairing", "definition": "The if-clause uses present simple, and the result clause uses will + base verb — never 'will' in the if-clause itself.", "example": "\"If it rains tomorrow, we will postpone the event.\""}, {"term": "2. Second conditional tense pairing", "definition": "The if-clause uses past simple (often 'were' for all persons in formal use), and the result clause uses would + base verb.", "example": "\"If I were in charge, I would change the policy.\""}, {"term": "3. Third conditional tense pairing", "definition": "The if-clause uses past perfect, and the result clause uses would have + past participle, both referring to an unreal past.", "example": "\"If they had left earlier, they would have caught the train.\""}, {"term": "4. Modals take the plain base verb", "definition": "Modal verbs (can, could, must, should, would, might) are always followed directly by the base verb, with no 'to' and no added ending.", "example": "\"You should consult a specialist.\" (not 'should to consult')"}], "examples": ["If it rains tomorrow, we will postpone the event. (first conditional: present + will)", "If I were in charge, I would change the policy immediately. (second conditional: were + would)", "You should consult a specialist before making a decision. (modal + base verb, no 'to')"], "mistakes": ["Using 'will' in the if-clause of a first conditional, e.g. 'If it will rain, we will postpone' instead of 'If it rains'.", "Inserting 'to' after a modal verb, e.g. 'She must to finish' instead of 'She must finish'.", "Using 'would' instead of 'will' in the result clause of a first conditional, e.g. 'If she studies, she would improve' instead of 'she will improve'."], "tip": "For conditionals, check the if-clause and result-clause tenses as a matched pair; for modals, check that the very next word is always the plain base verb, with no 'to' and no added ending.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["If it rains, we will cancel the trip.", "If it will rain, we will cancel the trip.", "If it rains, we would cancel the trip always.", "If it will rains, we cancel the trip."], "answer": 0}, {"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["You should to see a doctor.", "You should see a doctor.", "You should seeing a doctor.", "You should saw a doctor."], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g63", "day": 63, "level": "C1", "title": "IELTS/TOEFL Speaking and Writing: Grammatical Range Signals", "summary": "Deploy a range of structures examiners specifically credit for higher grammar-range scores.", "plainExplanation": "In IELTS and TOEFL speaking and writing, examiners are specifically trained to notice 'grammatical range' — deliberate signals that you can handle structures beyond the safe, basic ones — and weaving these signals in naturally, at the right moments, measurably raises your score even if your overall vocabulary and pronunciation stay the same. High-value signals include: at least one clearly correct conditional (especially second or third, which are harder to produce accurately under pressure), a relative clause used to add detail smoothly rather than as a separate, choppy sentence, a passive construction where it genuinely fits the content (not forced in artificially), an example of appropriate hedging language showing you can express nuanced certainty rather than only flat statements, and correctly used complex sentences with subordinating conjunctions showing cause, contrast, or condition. Importantly, quality matters more than quantity here — one confidently correct third conditional woven naturally into your answer is worth far more to your score than three attempted conditionals that come out with mixed-up tenses. A realistic strategy for exam speaking specifically is to consciously plan, before or during your response, at least one moment to naturally deploy a more complex structure you're confident about, rather than leaving your entire response to safe, simple sentences by default under time pressure.", "illustration": "🎯 SPEAKING/WRITING — SIGNAL YOUR GRAMMATICAL RANGE\nHigh-value signals examiners look for:\n  ✓ one clearly CORRECT conditional (2nd or 3rd — harder, so worth more)\n  ✓ a relative clause woven in smoothly (not a separate choppy sentence)\n  ✓ a passive construction where it genuinely fits\n  ✓ hedging language (\"this might suggest...\") showing nuance\n  ✓ subordination showing cause/contrast/condition\n\n💡 ONE confident, correct complex structure > THREE attempted ones with mistakes.", "steps": ["Consciously include at least one complex sentence with a subordinate clause in every few sentences you speak or write.", "Use a mix of tenses appropriate to the topic, rather than defaulting only to the present simple.", "Include at least one conditional sentence when discussing hypothetical situations or recommendations.", "Use passive voice occasionally where it's natural, to show range beyond only active-voice sentences.", "Self-correct minor errors smoothly when speaking rather than freezing — examiners reward natural self-correction as a sign of genuine competence."], "rule": "In IELTS/TOEFL speaking and writing, the grammatical range criterion specifically rewards using a variety of structures accurately, not just accuracy alone. This includes varying sentence openings (not starting every sentence with 'I' or 'This'), mixing simple and complex sentences, and including at least some advanced structures — passive voice, relative clauses, conditionals, or inversion — where they fit naturally.", "structure": "Vary sentence openings + mix simple/complex sentences + include at least one advanced structure per paragraph", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Varying sentence openings", "definition": "Grammatical range is credited when sentences don't all begin the same way (especially not always with 'I' or 'This').", "example": "\"Having considered the alternatives, I believe this approach is the most practical.\""}, {"term": "2. Mixing simple and complex sentences", "definition": "A response that combines short, direct sentences with longer, subordinated ones demonstrates control rather than just complexity.", "example": "\"The results were positive. Although some limitations remain, they suggest a promising direction.\""}, {"term": "3. Including advanced structures naturally", "definition": "Passive voice, relative clauses, conditionals or inversion should appear where they genuinely fit, rather than being forced in.", "example": "\"The proposal, which was initially controversial, has since gained wide support.\""}, {"term": "4. Accuracy still outweighs range", "definition": "An advanced structure used inaccurately can lower the accuracy score more than the range score improves, so correctness comes first.", "example": "Preferring a simple, correct sentence over an ambitious but flawed complex one."}], "examples": ["Having considered the alternatives, I believe this approach is the most practical.", "The proposal, which was initially controversial, has since gained wide support.", "Rarely does a single policy address every aspect of a complex issue."], "mistakes": ["Relying on the same sentence pattern (often 'I think that...') for nearly every sentence, which caps the grammatical range score even if each sentence is accurate.", "Attempting advanced structures inaccurately just to include them, which can lower the accuracy score more than it raises the range score.", "Overloading a single sentence with multiple advanced structures at once, which can reduce clarity instead of showcasing genuine range."], "tip": "Aim to include at least one complex sentence and one advanced structure per paragraph, but only where it fits naturally — accuracy still matters more than forced complexity.", "quiz": [{"q": "Which paragraph would likely score higher for grammatical range?", "options": ["I think this is good. I think this helps people. I think it works well.", "Although imperfect, this policy has helped many people; its long-term effects remain to be seen.", "This is good. This helps. This works.", "People think this is good. People think this helps. People think this works."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Which feature specifically signals strong grammatical range to an examiner?", "options": ["Repeating the exact same sentence structure throughout", "Mixing simple sentences with occasional complex and advanced structures", "Only ever using simple present tense", "Avoiding all subordinate clauses"], "answer": 1}]},
{"id": "g64", "day": 64, "level": "C1", "title": "Exam Timed Error-Correction Drill: Mixed Review", "summary": "Practise spotting and fixing a mix of common exam grammar errors under realistic time pressure.", "plainExplanation": "This final review pulls together the most frequently tested error patterns across the entire course, because timed error-correction exams draw from all these categories interchangeably and expect you to recognise each one quickly under time pressure, without being told in advance which category a given error belongs to. Key patterns to have fully internalised: subject-verb agreement traps (long phrases between subject and verb, collective nouns, indefinite pronouns, or/nor proximity rules), tense consistency (no unjustified shifts within one narrative, but correct shifts for timeless facts or earlier past events), conditional and modal structure (never 'would' in an if-clause, never forgetting 'have' in third-conditional or past-modal result clauses, 'can't have' rather than 'mustn't have' for negative past deduction), article and preposition conventions (general vs. specific noun reference, fixed prepositional collocations that must be memorised rather than derived), and parallel structure (matching grammatical form across every item in a list, comparison, or correlative pair). The single most valuable exam skill this course builds isn't memorising more individual rules, but developing a fast, reliable process: read the full sentence first for overall logic, then systematically check subject-verb agreement, then tense consistency, then any conditional/modal structure, then article/preposition choices, then parallel structure — moving through this checklist quickly becomes automatic with enough deliberate practice, turning error-correction from guesswork into a reliable, repeatable process.", "illustration": "🏁 MIXED REVIEW — YOUR FAST ERROR-CHECKING SEQUENCE\n1️⃣ Subject-verb agreement    → find the TRUE subject, ignore inserted phrases\n2️⃣ Tense consistency          → any unjustified shift within one narrative?\n3️⃣ Conditional/modal logic    → no 'would' in if-clause; 'have' not forgotten\n4️⃣ Article/preposition        → general vs specific; fixed collocations\n5️⃣ Parallel structure         → does every list item share the same form?\n\n💡 Run this sequence on every error-correction sentence — speed comes with practice.", "steps": ["Read the whole passage once quickly to understand its overall meaning before hunting for errors.", "Go sentence by sentence, checking subject-verb agreement first, since it's the most frequent error type.", "Check every article and preposition against memorised patterns rather than instinct alone.", "Check tense consistency across the whole passage, not just within individual sentences.", "Do a final read focused only on parallel structure in any lists or comparisons, since this is often the last error type test-takers notice."], "rule": "Real exam error-correction passages often combine several error types — tense, agreement, articles, prepositions, and parallel structure — within the same short text, and time pressure makes it easy to miss more than one error per sentence. The most reliable strategy is to check systematically: first the verb tense and sequencing, then subject-verb agreement, then articles and prepositions, then parallel structure in any lists.", "structure": "Read sentence → identify the single most likely error type → check → correct", "breakdown": [{"term": "1. Step 1 — check verb tense and sequencing", "definition": "Start by confirming every verb reflects the correct, consistent time frame, and that any two-event sentence sequences past perfect before past simple where needed.", "example": "\"Each of the reports contain...\" → check tense/agreement together: \"contains\""}, {"term": "2. Step 2 — check subject-verb agreement", "definition": "Next confirm the verb matches the true subject, watching for interrupting phrases and singular-looking-plural words like each and neither.", "example": "\"Conclusions was drawn\" → \"conclusions were drawn\" (plural subject)"}, {"term": "3. Step 3 — check articles and prepositions", "definition": "Then check for missing or unnecessary articles and for any fixed preposition pairing that has been substituted incorrectly.", "example": "\"capable to lead\" → \"capable of leading\""}, {"term": "4. Step 4 — check parallel structure in lists", "definition": "Finally confirm that any list or comparison uses one consistent grammatical form throughout, with no item breaking the pattern.", "example": "\"managing a team, communicating clearly, and solve problems\" → \"...and solving problems\""}], "examples": ["Each of the reports contain a summary of key findings. → Each of the reports contains a summary of key findings.", "She is capable to lead the project independently. → She is capable of leading the project independently.", "The data was analysed, and conclusions was drawn from it. → The data was analysed, and conclusions were drawn from it."], "mistakes": ["Fixing the first error found in a sentence and moving on, without checking whether a second, unrelated error is also present.", "Running out of time by over-analysing early sentences instead of applying a consistent, quick systematic check to each one.", "Applying the four checks out of order inconsistently, which makes it easy to miss an error type under time pressure."], "tip": "Build a fixed mental checklist (tense → agreement → articles/prepositions → parallel structure) and run every exam sentence through the same four checks in the same order, every time.", "quiz": [{"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["The information were useful for the report.", "The information was useful for the report.", "The information are useful for the report.", "The information being useful for the report."], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Choose the corrected sentence.", "options": ["He is good in solving problems quickly.", "He is good on solving problems quickly.", "He is good at solving problems quickly.", "He is good for solving problems quickly."], "answer": 2}]},
]
